      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>LADIFS' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, I hear on very reliable authority that velvets and velveteens of all sorts and kinds will be worn during the autumn, spotted and plain, and I have seen a very remarkable model just fresh from the hands of a dressmaker made of bnwn and white checked velvet, with the skirt set in pleats all the way round the waist. lam told t net in another season or two paniers will adorn our hip, and the lace petticoat and long pointed bod:c? will again be beloved of the multitude. Dais is in the future. For the present everyday wear in the autumn the bolero coat and the skirt which is tight in the front and on the hips, hut bears a box pleat at the back, will be general.</p>
        <p>I daresay you will soon be ;nrehasing for your- self a felt hat. One that I have by me now I like very much. On the left side two largo feathers adorn the brim. Velvet forms the bow, and is the material we shall use for our personal adornment in a variety of ways all through the winter. Metallic gauze is mounted upon headgear now instead of the too perishable tulles. It trims large chapeaux very amiably, freeing them from the weighty appearance any other adornment must possess. The tendency now is to a return of tne bonnet, these modish steeple-crowned hats. Toques are larger in outline than formerly, and there is much favour to be shown to feLs of pale blue trimmed with blick feathers.</p>
        <p>From a very close observance of the subject I am sure that braiding is to play a very con- spicuous part on autumn gowns and coats. It seems likely that we arc to have toc, much of it. A mere trimming ought to be subordinated to the general effect; but on some of the newc,,lt models the braiding patterns are so large, and the braid itself so vivid in style, as to diverge widely from this rule of dress. Braiding in moderate quantity and of unobtrusive design, has usually a rich and becoming effect. Some of the new coats are braided all over in a rather close design, a style that is far to be preferred to the large, detached patterns that have a bold and forward look. Black braid on brown cloth is a revival of a fashion that com- mends itself to good taste. A short coat of nut- brown cloth, single-breasted, is braided with over- lapping circles in black mohair, and is both a change and a relief from the wearying straight lines of tuckings, stitchings, and strappings. Their severity is better suited to masculine than to feminine garments.</p>
        <p>The prc3ent millinery . a wonderful display of inagaitieent ..3d birds with ereeted beads, and others wit' short bodies bursting Into a bewilderiog mass of paradise plumes and aigrettes. These fan' astici specimens of the feathered tribes are to he otle of the mnst attrac- tive features of our winter Th, colouriug of the feathers is ,imply wonderful. Q lilts have raised designs, and many of LOem zac very laund- some. - -</p>
        <p>All the tailor-made dresses I have lately on were mostly in almond, cress oi: grey green, brown, prune, and navy or grey blue. They were braided very sparklingly ;Lbout toe revers and top of the sleeves. Pe iSCVn tailor•trade gowns are lovely, but thzy are a plece of extravagatace we shall soon regret when conio he dark :lays and cloudy skies, and for my owl part I do ,it think that we can better navy blue, maroon, plum, Indian and Burgundy red. Greeil is mart, and a warm red- dish-brown looks 'sell on dark-haired beauties who have fresh complexions. lam surprised that you have not heard of the cover little conttivauce made to lift up the skirts in Paris. You will rr,n2.:d something of this kind very soon. I have seen some lovely tones of pale green in the new satin. surfaced ctshmerea. Terra-cotta, brick-red, cop- per, and II tmo-c2lour are very Pew and effective. lluwe-grey looks lov-ly w:th almost any colour, it is so quiet and lady like, end that it is sure to remain popular, is the opiiipeuof Your loving J ENNUI.</p>
