        <p>JOHN NOBLE, LTD.,</p>
        <p>is WOMEN'S MAT."</p>
        <p>The old saying that our S'Jrnmer consists of " two fine days, ard a thunder storm," has been discredited during the past few years, and seems to he in (Jaeger of being forgotten for want of application. But however pleasant other recent summers have been, general experience and official statistics point to that of 1899 as the finest of them all. During June, July, and August, there were no fewer than 75 days out of a possible 92, in which a temperature of at least 70 degrees was registered in the eeade. There have been longer summers, and sore in which higher temperature hae been recorded, but none of th, m have equalled that of 1899 in the uniformity of its magnificent weather, which, beginning before May was out, continued te the end of August, and in some parts well into September. The Rational Dress League still keeps up ite annual demonstration, but it is only to show that there is very little life or enthusia3m left in the movement. This year a cycle procession from London to Reading, was arranged hut afterwards the starting point was altered. to avoid London, to a point half way between the two places, and eventually only about 30 or 40 " rationalists" arrived at the rendezous. There was a cycling dinner, followed by brave speeches out over all thore seemed to hang the gloom and cenviction of a lost cause. It is eernm nly supposed that the divided garment is of com- paratively recent invention, but it is really fifty years or more old, and Lady Barberton and her friends met at Reading the ether day to celebrate the jubilee of the movement. One is forced to admit that it was a very poor muster, after a fifty years struggle to popularise the costume. The League must leek to the Continent for encouragement. In France a woman cycling in a skirt is as rare an exception as a woman over here riding in rationals. It is quite clear after all the efforts that have been made on its behalf, that the aivided costume has no chance of making headway in this country. The unabated popularity ef the cycle has been evidenced during the past summer by the fact that several London papers have found it worthwhile to pub- lish itineraries of rides wi:hin a short distance of the metropolis, illustrated in each case by a map. Kent, Surrey, and the neighbourhood of Epping Forest furnish ample material for such sketches, which no doubt prove interesting and useful to a great many riders. If only we could do without meals, the greater part of women's occupation in this world would be gone, There are many scientific men who tell us that we shall eventually arrive at such a state. When all our food will be prepared in the chemical laboratory, and a few lersnges suffice for our daily requirements. They point to tee production of artificial albumen as a great ad- vance in this direction, and among later discoveries, is that of a German professor who hese evslved a lozenge which contains all the ingredients of a glass of beer, Teus, provided with compressed beer in this form, any- one might go about in possession of all the resources of a small brewery. All that is necessary is to dissolve the lozenge in a glass of water, or, if not thirsty, it could presumably be taken neat, without water, Upon the whole it is to be hoped that this new production will prove a failure, os otherwise it would speedily threaten a fresh danger to the virtue of temperance. There is one place in the world where domestic ser- vants are said to experience difficulty in finding employ- ment, and strange to say, that place is in tne United States where the servant question has reached its most acute stage. Mr. Pickersgill, Consul-General, reports that there has been a good deal of depressien in San Francisco, and that even the domestic servants have suffered from the dearth of employment. In a paper which might appropriately have teen headed; "De Omnibus." Mr. Henry Higgs discussed recently, before the British Association, the subject of the dress worn by Lordon domestic servante on the occa- sion of their afternoons or evenings out. Mr. Higgs' concinsions were scarcely favourable to the Metropolitan ' domestic, whom he compared—to her disadvantage— with the Parisian factory girl, and flower seller. But Mr. Higgs need not have gone to Paris fora comparison, seeing that the factory girls of many provincial towns dress much more tastefully than their fellows in the metropolis. Neel blue, which 13 a very faveured colour, for tailor built gowns among cur Parisian sisters this autumn, is lovely tone, taking its came from the delicate blues of water colour painting. It is best in fine thin cloth, and the trimmings should COLIF ist of simple stitching or strap- ping only. The three quarter coats for winter wear should be earKully avoitied by those possessed pf hut Mender dress allowance, and by those whose inches (as regardslength) are limited. For an exceedingly tall woman the mode is a possible, though scarcely graceful sue. Per the medium or short woman the fashion is about the most urdeecoming and undesirable ever devised. Velvet or velveteen tee gowns are in great request, built with a saggestion of the picturesque. No straight lines, and rigid following of t aseion is permissable in the making up of this garment. The colours moreover are light, not dull, and jewelled girdles, lace collars, open sleeves, and flowing draperies, the dominant note. It is gratifying to read that very considerable progress has been made with the higher education of women in India. The movement is spreading all over India, and at Poona, for example there are a training college for teachers, a practising school, !rich school, and auxiliary primary school, all under Government supervision and housed in commodious buildings on a rite which leaves rcom for extention, fhie movement furnishes one of innumerable illustrations which might be quoted of the advantages which India derives from'Britisti occupation. The work of elevating woman to her proper position is slow, slower even than the process of giving married women their own in this country, but stip the progress is perceptible and the prospect moreihopeful than ever it has been before. Australia offered a prize of £25,000 to anyone who would rid the country of rabbits, hut after several people had tried their hands, no more satisfactory solution was felled than that of eating them. Jersey is said to have hit upon a similar remedy with regard to the octopus which had proved very destructive to lobsters. Octopus fishing, parties have accordingly been arranged, and a market is found in the Channel Islands for the fish, which is reported by the inhabitants to afford an acceptable dish. A new reindeer farm has been established in Eastern Norway and it need scarcely be added, the promoters have a particular ere for the British market. They are said to possess 2,500 head of deer,end purpose exporting venison in the cerease in refrigerator cars. Shonld the exeeriment succeed, it will of course have the effect of reducing very coneiderabiy the nrice of this delicecy,and it is quite possible that there wiH be a large demand for the produce of the farm. " MADGE."</p>
        <p>POLYPI-lON. THIS MAItVRLLOUS MUSICAL INSTRU 'ENT will PLAY by IT- SELF—Clockwork—thousand: of Tunes. You eau choose your own tunes. GEISHA AND UP-TO-DATE TUNES. Prices from 12s. 6d. to £25 ARCHER Sc SONS, 43 TO 47, LORD-STREET, LIVERPOOL,</p>
