      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>HALF-WAY UP SNOWDON.</head>
        <p>HALF-WAY UP SNOWDON.</p>
        <p>MOUNTAIN GUIDE'S ESCAPES.</p>
        <p>An interesting conversation with a Snowdon guide appears in the " worth Wales Observer." The object of my visit (writes the reporter) was Ynyswen, Llanberis, where selr. John Humphreys Jones resides. "I am now keeping the Half-Way House on Snow- don," said Mr. Jones, "and have done so for years. In times gone I used to act as a guide ; but now having pissed my three-score-three years, I have relinquie,ed that post to a certain extent. For the last thirty years I had not suffered the smallest inconvenience or pain. But in the early part of last winter, coming along the street one afternoon, I was attacked by a pain in my side. I managed to drag along to a friend's house, and there on a sofa I lay for three or four hours. After a little rest I was able to go te the station ; but by the time I got to Llauberis I was completely done up. When I reached home they were greatly alarmed. DIA pain ic my right side had increased. and all raw I was paralysed. A medical man examined me and gave me meicine, bat in spite of this I grew worse and very soon became delirious.</p>
        <p>"I grew worse thy by day, until my son•in-law persuade() me to try Dr. Williams' pink pills. The first box had a salutary effect upon me. I had five boxes in all, but bPfore I took the last I was completely restored to my former health. Dr. Williams' pink pills for pile people, in less than a week's time, brought back to me my health, and now I air able to walk six miles a day to my work."</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones addcd that his illness had supervened upon an attack of iniluenza, which often leaved behind it per- manent miachief. This may be nervous, taking the form of St. Vitus' dance, paralytis, or such milder ills as neuralgia, or sciatica ; or it may leave spinal trouble, backaches, rheumatism mid the like. But, perhaps, more often than anything else, influenzt leaves indiges- tion, stomach ailment, sluggish liver, and languid weak- ness calling for a tonio. Whatever the precise ailment Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people are the true remedy, giving strength and tone to the system.building up the blood, and removing the languid, lazy feelings that weakness causes. They are two and ninepence a box, or thirteen end nine for six boxes; and Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, London, will send them post free, if people try to push a substitute upon enquiries.</p>
      </div>
