        <p>NEWS OF TILE GAM. The match between Messrs. Morrell and Cain for the Championship of Liverpool has resulted in a win for Morrell by the score of one win to nil, and eleven games drawn. Morrell had the Black side of the "Switcher"in the game he won (Cain's notn- ination). Toe game ran on even lines until the pieces were reduced to six aside, when Cain ran into a book loss with which he was unfamiliar which caused him to lose the match.</p>
        <p>We understand that Mr. H. Mortal!, now the champion of Liverpo3l has sant a communication to the Draughts Editor of the "Liverpool Mercury." saying he is willing to concede two games in a match, to be decided by the first five wins, to any player residing in Liverpool except Cain. This challenge will, most likely, ba taken itp, as there are many good draughts players in Liverpool, and two wins start out of five is a bait which is very tempting to say the least of it.</p>
        <p>• Several local clubs having expressed a desire ts Mr. Pliuston, the secretary of the Warrington League, to start a second division of the League, he would be obliged if the secretary's of clubs would send in their applications at once,in order to arrange fixtures etc., before the season commences; his address is Mr. D. Plinston, King's Arms Hotel, Buttermarket-street, Warrington.</p>
