      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>'THE FILESMITI-I3' STRIKE. PUBLIC MEETING IN TIIE Co-OPERA.-</head>
        <p>'THE FILESMITI-I3' STRIKE. PUBLIC MEETING IN TIIE Co-OPERA.-</p>
        <p>TIVE HALL.</p>
        <p>In the Ce-werative Hall. Ss:alley-street, Warrington, on Tu scley evening. a public meeting was he'd under the auspices of the Warriteeton Treees and le tbour Coun- cil. to express sympathy with the filesmiths of War- rington and district in their dispute with their employers. There was a large attendenct. Ctuncleer James Evans presided, and there were present on the platform : Councillor S. Ur-tlev. J P., of Sa fil sid (zeaerel secretary of the Filesmishs Association), Mr. A. C. B elks (presi- dent of the Warrington Trades and L +boar Council), Mr. Lang (ex-president), Mr. D. Bullock (secrecary), Mr. J. Battersby (secretary of the Warrington and dis- trict Filesmiths' Association), Mr. Nathan Cook (secre- tary of the Wiredrawere Society), Mr. C. Brown (ex-president of the Chester Tradee Council and repre- sentative of the gas workers' and general I ibourers' union at Warrington), and Mr. E. Plinstoa (secretary Warrington Cabmen's Union). The Secaerette of the Trades Council (Mr. Bullock), stated that Couecillor Pierpoint had telegraphed that he was esiga,ged in London on particular business and therefore unable to attend that meeting.. Mr. Pierpoint extended his sympathy to the filesiniths. and would be glad t give them any financial as estance they required. (Applause.) That was almost as good as his attendance, hut still they would have liked him te pratide over that meeting. Me Samuel Woods, M.P., wrote stating that it was impossible for him to accept their levitation, and trusted teat the meeting would be a success. (Applause.) Mr. Steadman, M.P., hal also been asked to be present but he was engaged at Glasgow and unable to be present. At the last moment tee Courecil invited Mr. John Jenkins, the General Secretary of the Bakers ana Confectioners Society, to address the meeting, but he was busy removing, and unable to attend. The Council bad used its utmost endeavours to get some representative men to attend, but their efforts had not been very successful. They had, however, men on the platform who would be able to put the matter before them. (Applause.) The Cuemereer stated that upon the general principles of trades unionism he had frequently expressed himself He had never worked uaderprice for the sake ef getting a job. He their it thgy had some there to hear the sub- ject ventilated and he hoped the facts would be clearly stated to the meeting. He believed the majority of the people had only got to know where oppression existed and then general sympathy and support would follow. (Applause.) As far as he eould gather from the news- papers he understood that the men had aorked a certain number of hours for a pound per week—(A Voice! "It is true ")—He did not know whether it was true or not but no employer would get him to work for that sum. (Heir, hear.) He had always claimed the right to sell his labour in the bast market and gee: the lest possible Price for it. (Applause.) Ile further unierst fr nn the newspapers that some men were receiving £2 a week at the same trade. Surely there was something wrong in an organisation that permitted One man to work for £1 whilst another mail earned £2 at the same job. (Hear, hear,) He had never believed in strikes and he did not beieve in them now. He was of opinion that force was not a remedy. (Hear, hear.) He was hoping for the time and he trusted in soma measure it would be brought about when righteousness should obtain and when force should be no longer known. (Applause.) In the setting of a dispute by warfare he had often noticed that the weakest got the burden in the finish. (Applause.) Mr. Urreee, who had a cordial reception, observed that he felt in coming before a Warringtoa audience he was speaking to men who bad proved themselves thoroughly in earnest with reference to what they believed to be rieht. He had not come there in order to foment strife. He had been the general secretary to the Filesmiths Associatien for 22 years and when he was asked to come and speak he felt it his bounden duty to come. He. had had no part in this dispire, but he thought as a society of filetiniths they had always striven to do right. (Hear. hear.) He had had placed in his hand a resolution which read as follows r—" That in tbe opinion of this meeting the action of the Warrington filesmiths in coming out on strike is justifiable and this meeting recommends all trades unionists to give them financial assistance. (Applause.) In moving that resolution he did not intend to enter into the details of this particular question beyond stat- ing that he had every reason to believe from the inquiries be bad made and from various matters placed before him and his executive at Sheffield that the Warrington men were perfectly justified in trying to improve their condition at this juncture. (Applause.) The first ques- tion which suggested itself to him was whether the eause was a just one, because if it was not it could not succeed. No dispute could succeed without it had justice and right on its side. (Hear, hear.) He took it that every workman had a right to live. These filesmiths had a right to live ; they were not only respectable and intelligent citizens of this town but else men who had laid themselves out to render good service to the coun- try in the capacity in which they were engaged. These men had served an apprenticeship to their trade and it had not been disputed that many of these nvie did not get on an average more than El. per week. (Shame.) There head been no list of wages produced by the firms concerned showing that some were getting exorbitant wages. (Laughter.) What the men were endeavouring to do was to equtliee matters a little and raise the standard of wages for the lowest classes of work. lie knew for a positive fact that some of the men out on strike would not be benefited one penny after the whole struggle was finished. They were standing simply because they felt that their weaker brethren were unable to obtain without their assistance that which they had a perfectright to—a fair remunera- tion for the labour they performed. ;Applause.) The men wanted nothing beyond this, and it was their duty as men and citizens, as brethren associated in the great renks of industry, to back them up as far as possible until a satisfactory solution of the whole question was obtained. (Applause.) What was to be said of these men who were earning less than £1 per week? If they were to meet the responsibilities of life they required More wages to do .it. (Hear, hear.) With all due defer- ence to the filesmiths of Warrington he must say they had been quiet too long. If they had indicated their desire to improve their position earlier they would have been listened to. It was now said that thane men had teem satisfied with these wages for 50 years.and he supposed it would be areued from that that they ought to be satisfied for another 50 years with the same wages. (Laughter.) Ile did not remember that there had ever been any desire on the part of em plovers to improve the po idea of their employ&amp; until the men expressed their desire to improve it for them- selves. Workmen must ambito together if they were to obtain their east rights. Tee capitalists were well ab eto look after themselves. (Hear, hear.) He was aware that capital and labour must work together, and for the Success of both, there must be harmeny. but harmony could not exist when one party was getting all the plums and the other, with great difficulty, was ob- taining a bare crust. If he could examine the banking account of Messrs. Thewlie and Griffiths or Messrs. Stub, Limited for the last 50 years he thought he would find that they had not become poorer but had improved their position. (Hear. hear.) He was glad if they had. but he would say that if they had done well it was their beunclen duty as employers of labour if they had been well served by their workmen to consider their workmen in any advances made. From his know- ledge of the Warrioeton file trade he knew the reputa- tion gained by Warrington files, and which went throughout the length and breadth of the empire. (Hear, hear.) Wherever files wore used the names of Warring- ton manafactures were a guarantee of a good article, and they were able to command a better price than many other firmn. That indicated that these men who had been for 12 or 13 weeks outside their factory gates had done their duty to their employers. (Hear, hear.) They had acquitted themselves like men ; workmen that need not be ashamed of their work; and it was only fair and just that in these days of advancement and improvement that they should receive some eq livalent for their labour. (Applause.) He had it on the best authority that these men made an offer to their lemployers to work conscientiously for sixpence per hour. That would be an improvement on what they were getting by piece work, but the masters refused te agree to it. Sixpence per hour was cer- tainly vary much below what any skilled work- man ought to be able to command. (Hear, bear.) He contended that when a man had served an appren- ticeship of seven years and become proficient in making a most useful article of trade, he ought to be placed ou terms of equality with the average skilled workman. (Hear. hear.) The Warrington filesmiths were willing to submit their case to an independent arbitration and were prepared to stand by hie decision. But the employers were hoping to starve these men irate submission ; they were hoping these men would euccumb to the dire necessities of their claims at home. These men had proved themselves deserving of support. For thirteen weeks they had stood together in this dispute, and they were now being well backed up. His friend, Mr. Battersby, informed him that last week he received greater financial assistance than at any previous time. The men were encouraged and strcogthened by the manner in which they were le; log supported, and it only required Trades Unionists to be determined for victory ti be secured, If it continual another thirteen weeks, support would be forthcoming': The employers had been approached but they would not listen to any suggestions of the men. He believed that that meeting was thoroughly in sympathy with the men, and whatever might be the outcome of the dispute, he f eit it would te a success to the men. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WORD TO LADIES.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO LADIES.</p>
        <p>Send two stamps for our new and original Illustrated Booklet, containing plain and practical advice how Irregulari- ties. Suppressions, etc., may be prevented or removed by simple means in a few hours. Recommended by eminent Physicians, and thousands of Ladies, as being the only Genuine Remedy. This is not a quack medicine. Rstablished 30 years. LESLIE MARTYN, Ltd„ Chemists, 34, Dalstoa-lane Lorrio&lt;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ITIE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899.</head>
        <p>ITIE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899.</p>
        <p>Mr; Beencs,of the Warrington Trades Council seconded the resolution. The case of the filesmiths had been put before the employers, and as the employers toad refused to entertain the proposal!. the least they could do as trades unionists was to support the men as long as they continued out of work. (Hear, hear.) The statement which had been put forward by the men had not been refuted, and it might therefore be taken as correct. , He thought the resolution was one that woeld commend itself to all citiznia of the town. (Applause.) Mr. NATHAN Coax said it gave him very much pleasure to add his sympathy to that already expressed on be- half of the file cutters. Be looked upon the question as one where men wi4hed to better their positions, and he did Mt think the employers would be any the worse far making the concessions which were asked, because he maintained that when cheap labour was intro- duced into any class of business teere was a percentage of " skimming " to be done. (Hear, hear.) The question of the better housing ot the poor had been introduced that evening and it was often said "Why don't men go and live in better houses." The simple reason wa.s.as was illustrated by that strike, that the men did not get sufficient money to remove to better houses. (Applause.) He contended that a man who did not get more than £1 a week for his work could not live in a better class of house than he Was doing at the present time. Some labourers were getting even more than flee cutters and there was no responsibility attached to their work as was the case with the file cutters. In the file cutting trade there was a certain amount of respensibility attached to the workman and the master Was able to realise something by it when he sold the article which was made. If £1 a week was fop- posed to recognise work of that class, then he said et was time there was a change for the betterment of the file cutters. He understood that one lady empleyer in Warring:on had aoudad to the re- quest of the file cutters end granted an , increase. He referred to Mrs. Stephenson. (Applause.) If she could concede the advance asked for why could not the others? Sorely the lady must have weighed the matter up carefully and seen that those concessions were necessary. All honour to tee lady. (Applause.) Be would be very pleased if that question could be sub- mitted to a Board of Arbitrators to see whether a definite understanding could not be arrived at. (Hear, hear.) The wiredrawers had decided to make a levy of three- pence per week for the support of the alesmiths. (Ap- plause.) He thought they would all feel that it was their duty to help their fellow men in this struggle. (A.oplause.) Toe resolution was unanimously carried. Mr.C. BROWN then oroposed a resolution to the effect that the on.retitee believed in the principles of trades unionism and cendemnel the action of the employers of fllesmiths in attempting to break up their union. He stated that as repreieutative of the unskilled workmen in the town he caul I assure the filesmitbs that they had their fullest Sympathy. Many of the members of the gaewarkers and general labourers union had pledged themselves to subscribe threepeoce per week as long as the struggle continued. The filesmiths were fighting a just battle. Workers had had a hard struggle to gee a living wage in the past. It was difficult to decide what was a living wage,but they were certain teat Ll. a week was not —(Flear, hear)—nor;was thirty s lilting. (Ap- plause.) Hs would ask them how was it possible to bring up a family respectably on 20s. a week. He ' blamed the Alesmiths for having worked 50 years for the small pittance held out to them. The employers could not say that they had taken action in haste. (Laughter.) Whether they repented at le:sure or not, the filestniths could not. be charged with not having taken sufficient time to censider the matter. They knew that a combination of employers endeavoured to smash the engineers union, but if the local employers had set out to wreck the filesmitles union they had set themselves a tack they would be sorry for. He had been reading lately an admirable work entitled "In His S:EpS "and he thought the secretary of the Filesmiths Union would be justified in sending a copy to Mr. Petf r Stubs and the other employers. In conclusion he advised the filesmiths to stand shoulder to shoulder in this disputel and victory would be theirs. (Applause.) Mr. LANG seconded the resehotion. He traced the history of Trades Unionism dwelling upon the formation of the guilds up to the larger development of Trades Unionism as it was known at the pres net day. He be- ' lieved the filesmiths were going to come out of this struggle even in a hotter condition than they entered it. He believed the intention of the employers was to try and cripple the Union. (Applause.) Mr. BATTERSBY supported the resolution. He referred to the combheel action which had been taken by the men and said the masters little thought %bee they entered upon this struggle that the mere would be able to hold out for thirteen weeks. He could say at the close of the 13 weeks that their finances were in a very satisfactory condition. The subscriptions of the previous week had exceeded those of any previous period. They must not, however, relax their efforts It was not altogether a matter of a five per cent, advance which they asked for, that the masters refused, but they tried to break up their Federation. That night the filestniths had received general support, and had been greatly encouraged. If the resolutions which had been passed were carried out in their entirety he had no tear of the result. (Applause.) - The resolution was then carried unanimously. Mr. EDWARD PLINSTON proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman for presiding. He sail he could not help expressing his admiration at the way the filestnithe had fought their battle up to the present. The speaker proceeded to severely crit'c'se the action of Mr. Stubs, and in conclusion hoped the filesmitbs would have courage to fight the battle to the end. Mr. BULLOCK seconded. Mr. UrrLay supported, and the resolution was unani- mously passed. The CHAIRMAN briefly acknowledged the vote of 1. thanks.</p>
        <p>The services of the Trades and Labour Council in organising the meeting were also acknowledged on the proposition of Mr. BATTERSBY. In reply to a qnestion as to various rumours which had been circulated re3pecting the filesmiths, Mr. BATTERSIIT said that if the statement he had put forth was not true, the employers, who were the most interes- ted. would hive refuted it. The meeting then closed.</p>
        <p>te to hold nit W 33 Priestley him self. He never sur- ndered, and one of his last publications was the title "The doctrine of Pleogiston established." Other crs- coveries of Priestley are very numerous. Is be says, "I hardly know any experiment that is more adapted to amaze and surprise than this is,which exhibits a quant- ity of air, which, as it were, devours a quantity of another kind of air half as largo as itself, and yet is so far from gaining any addition to its bulk, that is con- siderably diminised by it." Spirit of salt was proved to be composed of gas dissolved in water. Again, spirit of hartshorn was proved to be likewise a gas held in solution, but of an opposite nature,fer it is alkaline ta vegetable colours. It occurred to Priestley to mix those two "airs," for might not an "acid air" mixed with "alkaline air" produce a 'neutral air," which, perhaps, might be found to be the same thing as common air? So they were mixed, and a solid was produced found to be sal ammonia. Priestley said, "I have met with few persons who are soon weary of looking at it, and some could sit by it almost a whole hour, and be agreeably amused all the time." Nitrous oxide, to which we owe so much when driven to the dentists harrowing room, was first made known to us by Priestley, and also that gas carbon monoxide which we see burning on the top of the red-hot coals in our grates with a vale blue flame, and which has since become of such great impsrtance to metallurgical and other works. It would serve no gosd purpose to attempt to enumerate all the dis- coveries that can be gleaned from Priestley's writings ; 'it will be noticed that they all relate to gases, and illustrate in a remarkable manner the ingenuity he dis- played in dealing witta this, the most elastic form of matter. As a discoverer be will always stand high, but beyond this he did not attempt to go. To theorize and , deduce laws from his results had no attraction for him, as is forcibly seen by the tenacity with which he stuck to Stahl's theory of conbastion when his own experi- ments, as was immediately seen by other chemists, proved the fallacy on which it was built. He was not a careful plodder after truth, but, as he says himself, he left a great deal to chance, but we, looking at his work, see that it was not chance; but the ingenuity and re- sourcefulness of his mind which has placed him in the first rank amongst chemical discoverers. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>MORE FESTIVITIES AT CUERDEN HALL.</head>
        <p>MORE FESTIVITIES AT CUERDEN HALL.</p>
        <p>The members of the Manchester District Band of Hope Choral Union and their orchestra were enter- tained by Mr. R.A. and Miss Naylor at Cuerden Hall on Saturday. The party which numbered 180 arrived at Thelwall at 3 o'clock and after being re- ceived by Mr. and Miss Naylor inspected the Hall. Outdoor games were then indulged in until the tea bell rang, when an adjournment took place to the Temperance Hall, where an excellent repast was provided. Tea over, thanks were accorded to Mr. and Miss Naylor, on the proposition of Mr. J Naftali. treasurer of the choir, seconded by Mr. Stone for the ladies, and Mr. J. Holden for the gentlemen. In tendering these thanks Mr. W. Chanclos Wilson, the conductor, said he had been asked to convey the thanks of the Choral Union in a concrete forni,and accordingly he desired Mr. and Miss Naylor to accept as mementoes of that day's visit, alabaster busts of three of the most popular musicians of this or any age—of Handell whose "Messiah" was the people's ideal of an oratorio; of Mendelssohn. whose' Elijah" was probably the finest all round oratorio yet produced, and of Mozart, whose sacred and operatic music never failed to elicit raiturous appreciation and to give ethereal delight. —The gift, so wholly unexpected, produced great pleasure, as great to the donors as to the recipients. In responding and accepting the busts, Miss Naylor, amid great applause, expressed the very great pleasure the choir's visit had given them —Mr. Naylor, who followed, expressed a similar pleasure. and an especial delight in learning how,at their own cost, and with great thoroughness the Choral Union was doing a grand musical work in aid of the temperance cause in and around Manchester.—The weather continuing inclement, the remainder of the proceedings took place in the ball. At 840 the National Anthem was heartily sung and after three ringing cheers for their host and hostess the party returned to Manchester, having thoroughly enjoyed themselves and being most enthusiastic in their praises of the many kindnesses they had ex- perienced.</p>
        <p>C._'ICURA RESOLVENT is so pure, sweet, and wholesome that all ages may take it with pleasure and benefit. Its mission is to cool and cleanse the blood in eczema and other torturing, disfiguring humours, rashes, and irritations, while warm baths with CuTICURA SOAP cleanse the surface of crusts and scales, and gentle anointings with CUTICURA Ointment soothe and heal itching, burning skin.</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere. Foreign depots: F. Nzwrianv k SONS Lon. don; L. AnDY, Paris; R. Towxs &amp; Co., Sydney- Porrzn DRUG AND Clam. Cola., Sole Props., Boston, U. 5.4..</p>
