      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DISCOVERIES OF JOSEPiI PRIESTLEY. HIS CONNECTION WITH WA RRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE DISCOVERIES OF JOSEPiI PRIESTLEY. HIS CONNECTION WITH WA RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>LECTURE AT THE NIUSEUNI,</p>
        <p>Mr. P. C. Ruddock, 1.1. C., delivered a lecture on 'Joseph Priestley," in the Museum Lecture Roam OR Monday evening. At the outset, the Lecturer referred to the various theoriea which had been advanced on the phenomena of combustion or oxidation, and ex- plained Stahrs theory on the phenornenni of min- bastion and calcination, which was universally be- lieved by chemists till it was overthrown by Lavoi- &amp;or, working on Priestley's discovery of oxygen in 1774.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PRIESTLEY'd EARLY TRAINING.</head>
        <p>PRIESTLEY'd EARLY TRAINING.</p>
        <p>Priestley was born in 1733,aud unswervingly adhered during his whole life to Statil's theory, th Pugh the very experiments and discoveries which he br3ught forward to prove its truth were, in other hand, to be the chief cause of its downfall. Priestley was born at Fieldhead, near Leeds, and, losing his mother at the early age of seven, was adopted by his auat, a Mrs. Keighley, a woman in good circumstances and a dissenter. She sent him to a public school, where he showed a passion for learning, and made censiderable progress in Latin, Greek, and Hebrew. The boy was of weakly, con- sumptive habit, and was advised to turn his thoughts to trade and g 3 out to Lisbon. rills induced him to study the modern languages, and he became proficient in French, Italian, and German, without a master. His health, however, improving, he gave up this ilea, and following the wishes of his aunt applied himself to study for the Ministry, and when 19 was sent to the Dissent- ing Acadetny at Daventry which the famous Dr. Dad. dridge had brought into repute. Here he spent a very happy three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>HIS FIRST CHARGE.</head>
        <p>HIS FIRST CHARGE.</p>
        <p>His first charge was at Needham Market, in Suffolk, where he was never in receipt of more than £3O a year. He was unpopular with his congrezation, which accord- ingly fell off. He nest trier) teaching, but here too his scheme failed and ha had to be helped by hie fliends. Years afterwards, he tells us, when he was farndus, he preached in the same place and multitudes flocked to hear the same sermons which before they had despised. From Needham he went to Nantwich, where he stopped three years, Here he was able to buy books and a few philosophical instruments. He established a school which he taught from seven in the morning till four in the afternoon,and after that had private pupils till seven in the evening. It is indeed a wonder how under those circumstances he was able to prepare .his Sunday dis- courses and find time for private study.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ILIS C3NNECTION WITS _WARRINGTON ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>ILIS C3NNECTION WITS _WARRINGTON ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>While Priestley was at Needham (the Acad7m. yat Warrington), was instituted and many of his friends, including Dr. Taylor and Dr. Benson, recommended him as "tutor in the languages," but Dr. Aikin was preferred b3fors him. However, on the d3ath of Dr. Taylor, Dr. Aikin was advaused to teach divinity and Priestley was appointed to succeed lairn in 1761, Shortly after the Academy was removed from Bridge Foot to larger premises in Aead3my-street.where Priestley had a house, and there he stayed till 1767, at a salary of £lOO a year and £l5 for each boarder, teaching Latin, Greek, Hebrew, French and Italian, logic and elocution, oratory and crit;cism, history, general policy, civil law. and anatomy. For a recreation he carried on his philosophical pursuits, and, becoming acquainted with Benjamin Franklin, he was persuaded to write'a history of electricity, for which he was elected a fellow of the Royal Society and Doctor of Laws of Eiinburgh Univer- sity. In the Academy be spent his time very happily, but the institution did not flonrish. A quarrel had broken out between Dr. Taylor and the trustees, when the friends of that gentleman rallied round him and formed a ho3tile camp from which the Academy never recovered ; the students eradually dwindled away, and it was finally closed in 1786.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>REMOVAL TO LEEDS.</head>
        <p>REMOVAL TO LEEDS.</p>
        <p>This dispute and the smallness of his income decided Priestly to leave and he accepted an invitation to take charge of &amp;Dahill Chapel, Leeds. Here he commenced his great chemical career and published his first tract on air. Fortunately for science, Priestley in 1772 was induced to accept the office of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LITERARY CIMPANION TO LORD SHELBURNE</head>
        <p>LITERARY CIMPANION TO LORD SHELBURNE</p>
        <p>at a salary of £250 a year and a house. He had little to do, and he was able to devote most of his time to chemical work. Here he remained seven years.and then by mutual agreement they parted, Lard Shelburne settling on him an annuity of £l5O. Priestley was now nearly sixty years of age. He settled in Birmingham, accepting the charge of a congregation. Here he bad ample leLure for his work, and by the liberality of his friends any apparatus that he required. This appears to have been the happiest part of his Life. Surrounded by friends, and with ample means, he draws a delight- ful picture of his happiness and serenity of mind, being thankful that he lived in an age and country in which he had full religious liberty, and in which he could carry on his investigations.</p>
        <p>BOYCOTTED AT BIRIIINGHAM Troublous times were, however, approaching, when the spirit of the French revolution was felt in this country, and men fearing for the constitution, and the Established Church, united together to repel attacks. Priestley was an avowed enemy of the Church, and Openly expressed his • pvlitical opinions. He was attacked by Burke in che House of Commons, by the clerey of the Church of England and by the press,:until in 1791, on the occasion of a public dinner, at which he was not present, on the anniversary of the taking of the Bastile, the mob actacked, and wreckod,in the name of Church and King,Priestley's house; his apparatus and library were destroyed, and he and his family barely eicaped with their lives. His friends kept him in hid- ing for some weeks,and his sons,disgusted. left &amp;Aland and settled in America. With the exception 'of a few old friends who stood true to him he was generally bay- zotted. The members of the Royal Society declined admitting hion to their company,servants were afraid to stay with him,and tradespeople to supply him, till,much against his will,he decided to follow his sons to America. He settled at Northumbland,on the Suegnehanna,when, surrounding himself again with books and apparatus, he occupied himself to,the last in the study of theology and science.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PRIESTLEY'S DISCOVERIES.</head>
        <p>PRIESTLEY'S DISCOVERIES.</p>
        <p>ITContinuing the Lecturer said it would be seen from this rapid sketch that Priestley had no early training as a chemist; indeed it was not until he was abont 37 years old that his attentim was first attracted to chemistry. While at Leeds he lived near a brewery, and his attention was drawn to the "fixed air," or as we call it carbon dioxide, which is given off so abundantly during the fermentation process. He procured some of the gas, but his means being too limited to allow of his buying much apparatus he had to fall back on his own resources. One of the first pieces of apparatus be devised was the pneumatic trough for collecting gases. He at once noticed that the fixed air conferred "a pleasant acidulous taste" to water, and that pressure assisted very considerably in keeping fixed air confined in water. He goes on to men tioa that in a few minutes he could make glass of exceedingly pleasant sparkling water.which could hardly be distinguished from very good Pyrmont, or rather seltzer water. . . Ido not doubt, therefore,tnrt that, by the help of a condensing engine, water might be much more highly impregnated with the virtues of the Pyrmont spring; and it would not be difficult to con- trive a method of doing it." Here we have the idea thrown out of the manufacture of soda water. Priestley next investiqated the properties of hydrogen or inflammable air. He placed some calx of lead—that is lead from which the phlogiston had been expelled— within a cyclinder filled with hydrogen and standing over water ; he heated the cahr by means of a large burning glass and noticed that the calx was rapidly transformed into lead, so that the water rose in the re- ceiver. Lie repeated the experiment with many different kinds of oxides, and found that the inflammable air was totally absorbed; he therefore concludes—"that phlogis- ton is the same thing as inflammable air, and is con- tained in a combined state in metals, just as ,fixed air is contained in chalk and other calcareoussubstances; both being expelled iu the form of air."</p>
        <p>THE DISCOVERY OF OXYGEN.</p>
        <p>Priestley tried the effect of heat obtained by means of his burning glass on a great many substances. generally contained in tubes filled with mercury and standing over the mercurial trough. This led him to the greatest work of his life, the discovery of oxygen. Let me give the account in his own words. " With this apparatus, after a variety of other experiments, an account of which will be found in its proper place, on the I,t August, 1774, I endeavoured to extract ale from mercurius calcinatus per se'— that is, calx of mercury, and I presently found that, by moans of this lens, air was expelled from it very readily. Having got about three or four times as much as the bulk of my materials. I admitt,cl water to it, and found it was not imeibed by it. But what surprised me more than I can well express was, that a candle burned in this air with a renarkably vigorous flame. . . The feeling of it to my lungs was ' not sensibly different from that of common air; but I fancied that my breast felt peculiarly light and easy for some time afterwards. Who can tell but that in time this pure air may become a fashionable article of luxury? Hitherto only two mice and myself have had the privilege of breathing it. . . But, perhaps we may also infer from these experiments that though pure dephlogisticated air might be very useful as a medicine, It might not be so proper for us in the usual healthy state of the body ; for, as a candle burns out c:uch faster in dephlogisticated air than in coalman air, so we might, as may be said, live opt too fast, and the animal powers ha too sooa exhausted in tnis pure kind of air. A moralist, at least may say that the air which nature has provided for us is as good as we deserve.' Priestley was thus led to the conclusion that common air was not a simpie substance, but that it was com- posed of one volume of dephlogiatieated air, and four volumes of phlogiaticatel air. He imparted his dis- covery to the French chemists in Paris, and Lavosier at once seized upon it as the one fact wanted to expiain combustion and to overthrow Stahl'a doctrine. The new theory was rapidly accepted, until the only man of re-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
