      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>VOTE OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTE OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>GltMellor BENNETT propose' a voto of thanks to the lecturer and said he had always felt that they in War- rington did not know enough of Joseph Priestly. Of all the famous men connected with Warrington Dr. Priestly was perhaps the most famous. At Leeds and Birmingham they had statues of Priestley and he hoped tbat at SDnio future time Warrington would have one too. Dr. Priestley resided in Acadqmy-street when at Warrington and it was a very curious and interesting coincidence that his house should now be used for offices by one of the largest firms in this country connected with gas appliances. It was here that Dr. Priestley de- voted so much of his time to gases and the ',constituent Darts of air, and now 150 years later the house had been devoted to the practical purposes connected with gas ap- pliances. Ele hoped that the Warrington Academy would l4ny, con inue to remind them of Dr. Priestley's cannection with Warringt3n. (Applause.) Mr. Dons= seconded the vote of thanks which was heartily carried. The lecturer having replied, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BREATHING OF THE WOODS.</head>
        <p>THE BREATHING OF THE WOODS.</p>
        <p>Those long sighs of the forest in summer, when the fresh, coo! air sweeps through the dark recesses of the trees and caresses the infinite host of leaves until they tremble and rustle with the delight of renewed life and motion! Should you not rejoice had you the power to draw the invisible element into your lungs that way ? You would be fortunate, as most of us do not breathe as outside Nature does. Scarcely 'once a day do we bless our bodies with a thorough and really filling in- halation. There is always a small amount of air in the lungs to keep them from collapsing, and as for the rese we breathe in short unconscious gasps as a baby nibbles at a biscuit. Which is a bad habit. The blood suffers from lack of oxygen, and the burning of carbon—the source of heat and power—is low and faint. This is not all, either. The lungs are one of the roads whereby the wastes are thrown out of the system, and when these organs are run only on half time, so to speak, the deadly stuff re- mains, and helps to7start those various forms of illness of which nobody has encasion to feel proud. Among the heaps of letters that havelain on this time- worn oak table of mine, thousands have contained a sentence after the following pattern :—" My breathing was short and catchy, and it was all I could do to wal about." It is Mrs. Susan Crudge who talks now. She says she lived—through no wish of her own—away below the mark for many years. It was like making her life's journey by the Underground Railway, on which the prespect is nothing to brag of, and 'the breathing diabolically bad.</p>
        <p>Her strength, she says, was not strength at all, but weakness. And inasmuch as the pleasures of the world —like coin scattered on the pavement—are grasped by strength and activity, we conclude that she didn't have many of them. The common physical satisfaction of eating,which man shares with all living things,was denied this woman.tihe ate morsels of food,of course,but her eat- ing was like her breathing—a poor example of what such work oaght to be. Oa rising from the:table,or soon after,Mrs. Grudge was eez3,l with pain at the:chest and around the sides.</p>
        <p>Night, to her, was .a dismal and dreary brace of hours. She tossed and rolled about, slept little, and viewed the morning light, when it came,:_with _blurred and tired eyes. .</p>
        <p>In January, 1896,5 he had something in the nature of a paralytic stroke, which aff tided the :whole of the left side. She could scarcely speak or move. Several doctors attended her, but failed to free the palsied :nerves. For over two-and-a-half years her condition continued practically unchanged. Besides the m)dicines !prescribed by the medical men s'ae used others—hut none of them had any effect. Th'e letter from which the foregoing facts are taken ends in these words " My state W 1.4 pitiable, and it seemed as if there was no help for me. In August, 1898 1 read about Ntothor Saigal's Syrup, and thought there might be a mere chance of its doing me good. I fancied this from what others sail of it. I got a bottle from the International tea stores in East street. After taking that and another bottle there was a decided improvement. I ate well, and felt in all respects stronger and better. Tnis furnished me with the best of reasons for pinning my faith to Mother Seigel's Syrup, and I kept on taking it, leaving off all other medicines. " The paralysis—as is always the case with that com- plaint—yielded but slowly; and I think it"a wonder that the Syrup had any effect upon it at all. But it did and at the date of this letter Liam once more able to get about. Had I known of and used Mother Seigel's Syrup four or five years earlier, I am convinced I never should have had the paralysis. I may add that for many years I suffered from obstinate constipation, but am troubled with it no more." (Signed) (Mrs.) SusaN CHUDGM, 2, Oak Cottages, Cried(' on-road, Rowbarton,. Taunton, March 15. 1899.</p>
        <p>Neither (ire all probability) woed she have had the paralysis. The indigestion and the constipation were the only causes of all the trouble. The body was crammed with the poisons they engendered, and the rest came along in due coarse, as a blister springs up on the spot where a hot iron touches the skin. Keep the di- gestion active and tbe bowels open with Mother Seigel's Syrup—that's the thing to do. Then shalli you walk, work, sleep, sing, eat, and drink, and breathe like the woods brushed by the summer wind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA.TH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA.TH.</p>
        <p>A DISORDERLY WOMAN AT STOCKTON HEATIL—At Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, before Mr. J. C. Parr (in the chair), Colonel Ridgway, and Messrs. 11. Nield, James Tinsley, and J. White. Mary Payne. a married womm, of Stockton Heath, was summoned for havioq been drunk and disorderly at Stockton Heath on the 22ad July.—She pleaded not guilty.—Ex-Police-Ser- geant Gosling stated that at eleven o'clock on Saturday eioht, July 22nd. he was in company with P.C. Tarte, also of Stockton Heath, near to the Police Sta'zion, when he heard someone kicking at the back door of the Police Station and then at the front door. He went te the station and saw the derendant, who was drunk, and asked her what she wanted. She said she wanted him to go to the 'bus and arrest a man who had assaulted her. He advised her to go home but she refused, and com- menced to use very bad latauage.—P.C. Tarte corrobor- ated.—John Malkin stated that shortly after nine o'clock on the date mentioned the defendant called at his house for six pennyworth of whiskey and a pint of beer, in a bottle, for her huiband. She was quite sober. A party in the house at the same time bought her a drink. She was in the house a quarter of an hoar.—Defendant said she was sober. Her brother-in-law assaulted her in the street, and she went to the Police Station.—She was fined 53 and costs.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES, New and Second- %lnd.—J. S. asataND:sl to 55, Midge-street, Warring- ton —.4 dal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>HEATHSIDE CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE CONTINUATION SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>PRESENrA.rION TO MR. T. GORDON.</p>
        <p>The prizes and cert'ficates won by students !he fie tthsida .Evening- Continuttiou S•3hool were dis:ributed on Monday evening by Mr. L. Greening The distribution this year, was rendered additionally interesing by tho fact that a presents.tion was to be naide to Mr. T. G:)rc7on, tho Ifeadrnait-r of the classes, and first • assistant master of the day schools,who is about to sever his con- nection with the schools, having accepted the headmas- tership of a school at Westhoughton. Mr. A.. J. Jolley presided over a good attendaece 4if scholars,teachers and parents, amongst those present being the Rev. E. R. and Mrs. Tarbuck. Messrs. L. Greening, D. W. Finney, W. Caantry, R• Chorl,lc, T. Gordon, Edden, Enson, Mal- colm, Longslia.w, Miss 'Finney and _Miss Piinston.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN who was cordially received said they had me:; together that evening for a very pleasant pur pose, it was to hive the pr:z !s gained last winter, distributed to the suceessful students. ' They all knew, at • least, he took it that they all knew that competition was one of the conditions of life that held good from the lowest forms of life,right up to the highest, and competition often struck them as being very unpleasant and sometimes even as harsh and cruel. The competition that took place in that school had locen of a friendly and not of a hostile charac- ter, and had always been tFneficial to those who had taken part in it. Some who had dome so were about to receive a substantial recognition of their success. Others , who had not obtained prizes had nevertheless derived benefit, as they gained not only increased knowledge by attending the classes but the training of mind and char- acter that competition brought them. Liter in the evening they would show their good will in more thin one way to Mr. Gordon. Ever since he came to Heath- side he had been not only a friend to the managers and scholars but to every one with whom he came into con- tact. He would now ask Mr. Gordon to road the report which he would subsequently submit to the managers at their next meeting wnich would give an idea of the sucussful work that was being done at the school. THE' HEADMASTER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. GORDON then read his reporc, which was as follows: "I have the hocour ta submit to you the following re- port of last session's work of the Evening Continuation School. The session opened with an entry et 105 students and cloged with 134 on the roll. The average attendance was 1056. The school was opened 46 times. The total number of hours of instruction was 9.719. The popular subjects were shorthand and chemistry. You will be pleased to know that each of the students who entered for Pitman's examination in shorthand was suceessful in obtaining his certificate. There were nine students over 21 years of age. I have to thank the various gentlemen for their kindless in giving !return which were in the following order :—(1) " Primitive Man," by Mr. Smith ; (2) "Spiders," by Mr. Lianceus Greening-; (3) "Introductory Study of Insects," by Mr. E. E. Lowe; (4) " A Tour through Cape Colony," by the Rev. H. V. Pigot, M.A. ; (5) "A descriptive tour through Ta',- mania," by Mr A.J.J4ley. The whole staff of teachers worked conscien'iously and well. 11. M. Inspector's report was " This school has again done good work. The pupils have been attentive arid interested, and the instruction given with zeal and care." The Evening School commenced for the session 1899-1900 on September 7th when 131 male students enrolled. There are now 150 on the roll. This number shows an esitry of 26 in excess of that of last year. Classes are already formed in reading, writ- ing, arithmetic, mensuration, shorthand, chemisry and drawing. This year I have formed a class for men which is well attended, there being 40 students. I have to thank the Chief Constable (Mr. Luke Talbot), for kindly offerilig to arrange the time of the policemen's duty that those who wish to attend the men's classes may do so. And now gentlemen I may say that it is with great regret that I am severing my connection with this Continuation School, the conducting ot which has always been such a pleasure to me. I should like to thank the Rev H V Pigot, Mr. Greening and others who havealways taken such interest in the school and done so much towards the success."</p>
        <p>THE DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that last year the prizes were die- tributed in the presence of Mr. Robert PLerpoint, the Borough member, who showed his interest in the work by attending. That year they had hoped to have had the presence of the Mayor as their local municipal chief. The Mayor had expressed to him Wetter and personally his great regret that he would be unable to be present, and his warm sympathy with the work carried on there, which was in no way in hostile competition to the work done by the Technical Institute.=As th,nlayor could not attend be invited their local scientific chief, Mr. Greening to distribute the prizes that night. Ho would now ask Mr. Greening to perform that pleasant duty. Mr. L. GREENING said that the report Mr. Gordon had just read was an extremely satisractory one. The success of the classes was mainly due to Mr. Gordon alai the staff of teachers who helped him. As Mr. Jolley had said the school was not considered in any way hostile by the Museum and Technical Instruction Committee. They welcomed heartily the euccess of those schools. It was not always easy to form continuation schools. He remembered in 1892 when they initiated the movement lat People's College they bad 90 students, and they had gone en increasing rapidly, but although soch success attended the classes at Peoples Collegs they had had failures in Warrington. They opened a school at the north end of the town, but unfoitunetely had to close it because students would not go near it. If parents realized what benefits the children got at continuation schools, they would be a little more active in seeing that they attended them. The object of continuation schools was to allow boys who had left school and gone ;to work to keep up what they had learned, and also to take a higher education. They acted as feeders to the Technical School, because they knew the Technical Institute had had to refuse to receive students because their writing and other subjects were not sufficiently advanced. The continuation schools were thus very valuable as feeders to the Technical Inctitute. They might ask what was the use of all that ? When they came to fight the battle of life they would find that the boy who was the best educated would get the best posi- tion and that meant money to him and also happiness. They could not overstate the importance of 'education. He did not want them to be always studying, that was a mistake ; he wanted them to take part in exerCiSeS, as without a healthy body they could not have healthy minds. They knew perfectly well that competition amongst all classes of the community was very severe. The competition in Eneland was nothing to that on the continent. They could easily understand that, when they applied tor a situation if they could no write shorthand or write a good hand, they would probably fail to get that position. The more highly educated person would succeed. It was extremely important that the rising generation should be prepared to take their position in England against foreign students. They could not all have prizes that night, bat the prizes given were nothing to what the priz) would be in after life. (Hear, hear.) It was a very good thing te be well read in various depirtments. They miy not think so now, bid a hen they were men or women they would regret it if they had not attended those schools to improve them- selves, and so help to pass a pleasant life. If they did not get a prize that night it was better that they had tried and failed than not tried at all, because if they tried they must be learning something. From the report he noticed that 10,000 hours had been spent there by boys and girls who would no doebt benefit from it hereafter. He hoped those who had failed that year would be the prize winners next session. (Applause.) The prizes and certificates were then distributed to the following studenti :—Boys : Harry Hill. Gilbert Simcock, John Titchard, Joseph Wilson. Ereest Prince, Stanley Griffiths, Jaws Florstield, Ernest Sanders, Thomas Hughes, Arthur E. Baker, jamcs pears". Thomas RiQd, Stanley Hall, Israel Gibeos, Jesse Marsh, Harry Foulkes, William Smallwood. William Roberts", Henry Mills. John Hilton. Thomas Bosward, George Tunstall. Henry Fleet, Frederick Hartley, John Rogers, John Roberts. Jrhn It Wilson, Charles Wood, Frank Brandwood, Frederick Wood, Henry Flood, Frederick leloy, Harold Perreue. Girls : Maggie Clayton, Mary Rhodes, Betty ?diodes, Eva Crookes, Maud Allen, Beatrice Cooke, Liiy Clayton, Margaret Short. Amy Sewell, Mille? Williamson, Ruth Roberts, Maud Emson, Nets! Jackson.</p>
        <p>THE GIRL'S CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that befor, taking the next item he wanted to say a word about th work being dole on the girls' side of the school. Mr. Gordon had FubTnitt3d a repeOrt relating, to his side, but Miss Plinston had modestly declined to make a statement about the girl's classes. He desired to take that opportunity of ae.know- ledging in the heartiest way, the work Miss Plinston had done, and to publicly express their cratitirde to Mrs. Pigot for the work she did last winter in connection with the girls' schools, and to Mrs. Tarbuck for the as- sistance she had given near the close of the session. He was pleased to see the school had made such good pro- gress last session and that the prospects for the coming session were even inighter than they were last year. Mr. Greening had referred to the fact that the municipal committee started one school which failed. He did not wish to criticise the work done,or to suege,t anything in the way of adverse criticism of the management of the school. He did Riy, howf vAr. that the</p>
      </div>
