        <p>success of those schools had been very larjely due to the personality of the principal teachers—Mr. Gordon and hiss Plinaton. On behalf of the maria;erz., and he ventured to say on b !half of the inhabitants of that part of the tLwn, he respectfully desired to express their gratitude to them. He was pleased to see in the list of prize winners the names of several men. He wished to maze it known that age was not recognised either on the boys' or girls' side. They had a class for men and he was glad to know that the Chief Constab:e had taken an active part in hAping forward that class In' arrang- ing for scone of his mea to attend r,'gularly and arrange tijir duties to enable them to do so. They were also indebted to Messrs. Pearson and Knowles for the way they had conic forward and elle mraged them in their work by offering to pay the fees for their employees or their children ti attend. Thsy were about to make a</p>
        <p>PRESENIATION TO MR. GORDON which wood be a litt!e recognition of his services. They wished that the recegnition had possessed more intrinsic value than it did. Mr. Gr eaing would make the preseotation on behalf of the scaolars and the teachers and managers of that and the day school. Mr. Finney had asked him on his (Ur. Finney's) behalf to make a presentation of a handsome lamp as a little recognition on his part of the good feeling that had existed betweeh him and hie Lieutenant—Mr. Goroa. (Applause.) Mr. GREENING who was received with applause said it was exceedingly gratifyieg to him to be asked to make that presentation Co their friend Mr. Gordon. Personally he was very sorry indeed that Mr. Gordon was leaving Warrington and on their behalf he was sure he was doing right in expressing similar footings. Heathside Schools had presperA considerably by the able work'Mr Gordon hid done. It was an object IFQ•son to the scholars to see that good work was rewarded in some little way. He btLeved he (Mr. Gordon) ,bad been there for six years and the work he had done in tne day schools as second master and at the Continuation S3hool was very much appreciated. Ile started the evening classes with 40 scholars, to-day there were about 185 The repoiti they got from the government were exce,dingly favourable and pointed out the capability Mr. Gordon possessed for his line of work. He was sure they would agree with him when he said that they wished him every success in his new sphere. They hoped he would al- ways be blessed with good health to carry on the excel- lent work of teaching the rising generation. It was very much to his credit that he had succeeded in obtaining the head mastership of a school. On behalf of the managers, teichers, scholars mei friends oE that Insti- tution he .had the pleasure to present that beautiful dressing ease end silver inketand to Mr. Gordon, and ask him to accept them as a small token of the respect that they had for him. He hoped he would live a long and happy life to carry out the good work he had com- menced. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Greening then handed to Mr. Gordon the articles named. The inkstand bore the folowing inscription : " Presented to Mr.T. Gordon by the managers, teachers, and scholars of Ileatlisi.in Dav and Night Schools, September 25:h, 1899,' and aI4o the initi4s " T. G." The Rev. E. R. TARBUCK said he was vers. glad indeed to be a participator in that interestieg ceremony. Of course, Mr. Gordon, was not as we'l known to him as he was to most of them. Many of them had passed through his hands, and he was afraid here and there many of them remembered with some feelings of ingratitude the pass ng attention he had paid to their physical frames. (Laughter.) If Mr. Gordon was not as well known to him as others in that room, they had bad a representative who had known him and admired him the !whole time, to be their moutheiece in making that presentation, aid he felt vary gratefel indeed to Mr Greening for the nice way and satisfactory terms in which be expressed their feelings tew irds Mr. Gordon. They were sustaining a still greater lo.lt in a few days in the person of their revereni friend Mr. Finney, and that had naturally handicapped them in preparing fer that presentation. Ever since he had made the acquaint- ance of Mr. Gordon, he had felt very deeply indeed that he had earned his gratitude and sincere respcct. (Sear, hear.) Mr. Our.. had tred his powerful influence in his pro- fession of a school master, not only to train the intelli- gence, but also to train the moral and spiritual la, log of those committed to his charge. Ile felt assured of that from the times he had listened to him giving his les- sens, and from his persenal intercourse with him. It was with the very greatest ple,sure mingled with a great deal of sorrow that he bore what Ittle testimony he could to his efforts. Ile wished him prosperity and a lone life of usefulness.</p>
        <p>Mr. GORDON sail he thanked all those who had sub- seribed towards these rresents. He was simply de- lighted with them—they must excuse him not being mere explicit as he was so regretful at leaving them and could not say all that he would like to. He might say that those schools, especially the continuation school, bad been to him something like a child was to its parents. When he and Mr. Edden commenePd it four years ago they only started in R poor way hut they tboueht they had done exceedingly well, and never thought the schenl would increase to its present size. Now. as the Inspector said, it was one of the largest in the district—he believed with the exception of the People's College it was the largest: To be successful they must do their utmost to eain the highest position they could for the school. He never ceased to think which way he could bneefit it or bring other students. This year he had forme4 a class for the men and had seen the Chief Constable, who fell in with his suggestion at once, and said he weuld like his men to be able to attend the class. Evening schools had lately been failing,ae was Phr‘wn by the report of the Lerdon School Beard. He (Mr. Gordon) started last veer with 105 and finished with an average of 105 6 which he thought was a splen lid record. He wou d like to raw a word about the kindness Mr. Jolley had always shewn to him. He had never asked him to help him but what he had kindly consentel. Mr. Jolley was a friend to everyone whom he thought were acting in the right way. Mr. Pigot had tailea a great intarest in the evening school. There was an old saving that they never knew the value of their friends till they missed tivm. They had missed Mr. Pigot :tad they knew new the value of his services. He thought the scholars should de their temest to etre ,ed as the tea^hors gave up their time for their benefit. Mr. Geaden also txpressed his thanks to the teachers for subscribing,and in conclusion thanked all for their kind- ness and said he henal Char he should meet with as much kindrvss in future as he had done in Warrington. (Applause.) Givers were then given for Mr. Gordon. Mr. FINNRY proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Green- ing for his kindness in dntriboting the prizes and making the prezent 'Mon. He said Mr. Gordon had been an excellent first assiitant. He wishxd him more success in his next position than he had had in Warrington. He was very thankful to Mr. Greening for the interest he always taken in the school: _</p>
        <p>Mn. CrroßtEr secaoded and the motion was carried w;th arclatnation.</p>
        <p>M. GREENING briefly legpordol and pr. p)sei a vote of thinksto Mr.Jolley for wesiding. Mr. cl/ANTIIT Senordeci, and the Rev. E. R. TARBITCIC supported in appropriate terma. The motion was carried with acciamrnation. and Mr. JoLLEr having respondeci.the ptocecdings terminated.</p>
