      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>THE' EX A MINE a.</head>
        <p>THE' EX A MINE a.</p>
        <p>SEPTEvIBER 29111 1899.</p>
        <p>SIR M. WHITE RIDLEY has The Suzerainty stated that the word suzer- ainty has been omitted from the recent despatches to the Transvaal, and Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT, in his letter to the Times is right in saying that it was unfortunate this bone of contention "was ever brought in." It has,how- ever, been always in:the dispute,in a certain sense, 'because the Boers have never agreed with us as to its meaning, or as to the effect of the Convention of 1884 or the Convention of 1881. The contention of the British Government has been that the suzerainty was left in the exact position it was before, and, as a matter of fact, the Boers have submitted treaties to us, unless our memory is at fault, since 1884, what- ever their argument may be about"inherent right."l The present controversy only indirectly turn s Upon the suzerainty point. The Boers omit all reference to the reservations, and British con- troversialists, not unfairly, insist upon the reserva- tions as conditioning the independence which the. Transvaal enjoys. It cannot be admitted, however, that the Transvaal has what is described as "inherent right" as a Sovereign independent State, even if we omit the word suzerainty, be- cause of its vague character, and employ such a substitute as,lMr. CHAMBERLAIN used in the con- troversy with Dr. LEYDS, viz., "a' position of superiority." Behind the franchise question is surely that of the right of seeing that the other reservations of the Convention of 1834 are regar- ded. This does give to this country some right of partial intervention in the international affairs of the South African Republic, and such a right is being exercised in the present dispute. The posi- tion of this country towards the Transvaal is not, and it never was, exactly the same as its position had been towards the Orange Free State. We have never claimed to exercise any authority over the treaty-making arrangements of the Orange Free State, but we have never ceased to claim this right in connection with the Transvaal, This is the essential difference, and it is im- portant it should be borne in mind in all our argumentations. Stress is laid upon LORD DERBY'S telegram, giving an account of the effect of the Convention of 1884, but it cannot be regarded as superior to the Convention itself, and there is a sentence in it which has misled the Boers as well as other people. Even Sir WILLIAM HAR- COURT is slightly in er2or in the interpretation he puts upon it. The "same complete independence in the Transvaal as in the Orange Free State" does not mean independence of the reservations mentioned in the Convention of 1884. It means free government in ordinary affair3,apart from the terms of the Conventions and* the promise of equal rights with the burghers, given by President KRUGER as his idea of what the British residents in the Transvaal were to gain as the result of the Convention of 1884, and which they have never yet enjoyed. Subtle explanations are the forte of the Boers, and they have taken out phrases and -passages and made them bear an entirely unnatural meaning. They must now see the folly of this course of action, and they ought to be convinced that our intentions are honourable and consistent with courtesy and common sense. Whatever may be the right word to use to describe the kind of authority we left to ourselves in restoring to the Boers their Government, there can be no doubt that some authority was claimed and admitted, and that when the resent dust and heat have dis- appeared it will remain intact, in the event of a peaceful solution be:ng attained. We cannot forfeit that right to avoid a disagreeable situation.</p>
        <p>THE earthquake in India The Indian comes as an addition to Disaster. other misfortunes. The failure of rain has caused a large amount of distress almost amounting to famine, and the Viceroy has determined to personally visit the affected dis - tricts. The earthquake in Darjeeling has now de vastated a considerable region and caused great loss of life. The region has been visited by earth- 'quake before, but hardly with such serious conse- quences. The district is one of the elevated health stations on the southern slopes of the Himalayas, and it is much frequented by Anglo-Indians, some of whom appear to have perished. The earthquake Was accompanied by heavy rains and landslips,and Much damage has been done to the railways, to the tea-plantations,and to private property of all kinds. The disaster seems to have come without any Warning on Monday night, and to have been com- Posed of a series of shocks. There will be further details on several points, but they can hardly miti • gate the severe nature of the general event, and they may even increase it, because all the facts in</p>
        <p>I these sudden catastrophes take some time to come lout. It is a great calamity, and it will probably affect the minds of the more superstitious inhabi- tants of India, who have already seen in the threatening famine the end of the luck which the new Viceroy was supposed to have had at the commencement of his rule. The rainfall in parts of India has be en abnormally low for the season but most of the hilly regions have escaped, and even in the affected provinces the famine will be smaller than was originally feared. The Government at Calcutta will meet this new disaster with measures of relief and restoration. The land which oas slipped canno be restored to its original position, but the rail- ways and roads can be relieved of their superin- cumbent rocks and earth, and further damage may be averted by timely engineering precautions. Probably LORD CURZON will himself visit the re gion, and do his best to infuse hope and couraga by his presence into the easily dispirited and some- what fatalistic natives. The re-housing of the people is always a simple problem in India, and there will be immediate efforts to protect them from any further rains of the kind that have fallen. Twenty inches of rain in twenty- four hours fell during the upheaval, and the effect of such a torrent must have been terrible. In this country we think one inch in a thunder burst tremendous. Multiply it by twenty- four, and we can perhaps magine the havoc it would cause. The Indian Government will, we do not doubt, effectually cope with the disaster. The worst we have to fear is that other districts have been, or will be, visited by earthquake, as was the case on former occasions, some of the secondary shocks being even more serious than the primary ones. The news has come at a time when the public mind has been drawn from Indian affairs by occurrences nearer home. But India is always with us, and events happen there with a startling suddenness which makes it a land of perpetual marvels and anxieties.</p>
        <p>North Walsham grammar school possesses a brick upon which Nelson cut his initials when a lad. Mr. Rider Haggard's account of the acci- dental discovery of this memorial supplies yet another argument in favour of tireless energy on the part of the various commemorative and pre- servation societies. Mr. Haggard's father used to attend the North Walsham school, and when pay- ing a visit more than half a century later recol- lected the existence of the Nelson memorial. But no one then at the school had heard of the brick. The tradition of the thing had died away. "But the more he thought of it," writes Mr. Rider Haggard. "the more positive he became of its existence,"and he procured a lantern and searched the wall in the gloaming of an autumn day. Sure enough the relic was discovered. The bricks above had been crushed and battered by falling trees , and by natural decay, but this one was sound. , The incident might well be laid to heart. Nelson relics are scarce enough. How much would we give for these fresh reminders of the hero's daily life! Future Englishmen will very probably be saying exactly the same thing in reference to our modern heroes. Londoners especially might take the lesson to heart at this moment, when so much higgling is being displayed in connection with Gladstone's home at Dollis Hill. Gladstone relics are very plentiful just now, no doubt, but a hun- dred years hence they may all but have dis- appeared, unless we agree to safeguard the inter- sts of posterity in an adequate fasaion.</p>
        <p>The Cape-to-Cairo Railway is exercising moral as well as political influence. It is awakening dormant sympathies in the savage breast. Mr. W. T. Stead reports that on the northernmost section an Arab sheikh has made an impassioned remon- -stranc3 against the cruelty of making such small steam engines drag trains so huge and so heavily laden ! The striking photographs with which Mr. Stead illustrates his article in the "Windsor " give us a very fair idea of the vastness and com- plexity of this undertaking. Yet it cannot been compared with the splendid project which Russia is achieving across Europe and Asia. The engineering difficulties are not so stupendous, for nature has not placed so many serious obstacles in the way. There is one very significant photo- graph depicting the course of trade on the Matoppo Hills, where Mr. Rhodes and Mr. Colen- brander held their celebrated indaba with the mutinous Matabele. Each waggon in the cara- van has to be drawn by no less than sixteen bullocks, and sudden disease might easily stop the progress of the caravan indefinitely and swallow up the small fortune of the traders. That will no longer be possible when the railway is at work. Another instructive piece of information conveyed by the pictures is that the line is obviously being laid for a very long period of time. The work- manship is of the best, and bespeaks the entire confidence of the promoters in the future cash value of the undertaking.</p>
        <p>The filesmiths' strike at Warrington which has dragged its weary length along for twelve weeks , appears to us a fit subject for arbitration. " Al- ' ways arbitrate before you fight" has been re- peatedly urged upon Governments when consider- ing the issue of war and it would be a good thing if employers and employed could be led to intro- duce this desirable innovation before entering upon such a struggle as that which is now taking place in the file trade. From all appearances there is every probability of the strike continuing another twelve weeks and with the same unsatisfactory re- sult. A dispute of this description cannot fail to have serious effects upon both masters and men. Trade will be diverted to other channels and perhaps never find its way back again to Warrington, whilst in the meantime the men are living on strike pay largely contri- buted by their fellow trade unionists, who in some instances, can ill afford to pay the levy. In the interests of both, an early settlement is desirable, and as men and masters have failed to come to terms perhaps the introduction of a mediator might have good results.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WARRINGTON MARKETS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday wheat (white) fetched 33 lid and red wheat,33 101; oats,2s 2d and 2s 44. Flour,best seconds, per 2401 b., 18s 01 Bran, lid and is per 20:b. At the Pro- vision Market prices were as follows:—Fresh butter is 3d and is 44 per lb ; cheese, 6(1 to 84 per lb; fresh eggs, 8 and 9 for is; fowls, 43 6d to 53 61 per couple; English beef, 4d to 91 ; lamb 6d to ; mutton 64 to 9d; veal, 7d to 101; pork, 64 to 84. P.S.A. AT THE Y.M,C.A. OPEN MEETING.</p>
        <p>Last Sunday was an open meeting, and the room was uncomfortably crowded, many having to find room on the landing. Mr. Isaac Barlow presided. The Golborne•stnet Baptist Church choir gave a musical service, which consisted of the following items ;—Antbem, "Arise, shine," choir; song," Star of Bethlehem," Itliss Christian ; oboe solos, " With verdure clad and " How vain is man" ("Judas Maccabus "), by Mr. George Clarke; anthem, "Come, Holy Ghost ":. "lust " Love Divine," Miss Christian and Mr. A Griffiths song, "The Good Shep- herd,"Mr, A. Griffiths. The collection was very satis- factory. Messrs. Travis and Reeves were the accom- panists, and the conductor was Mr. Frank Hick- man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DEATH OF A WELL-KNOWN BA.RRISTER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A WELL-KNOWN BA.RRISTER.</p>
        <p>The death is announced of 'Mr. Thomas Swift, a well-known barrister, of Liverpool. he was taken ill in the street on Tuesday, and died in a neigh- bouring shop to which he had been taken: He was born at Prescot in 1832, and, after practising as a solicitor, he was called to the Bar in 1882. He was for a time a member of the St. Helens Town Coun- cil. Mr. Swift was one of the counsel in the May- brick case, and was also well-known in connection with licensing cases. He has on several occasions appeared at the Warrington Brewster Sessions, and was well-known in the town and district. The funeral of deceased took place at St. Helens Ceme- tery on Thursday.</p>
        <p>-A9lr. for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLA.SS MINIMAL WATIMS —Advt.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOTS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT WIDNE3</p>
        <p>On Monday Th imae Leonard, aged four, 10, Barn- street, was admitted to the A:cc:idea:, Hospital, suffering from injuries received last Friday whilst playing with a hanicart of bricks and m )rtar. which fell on him. He was attended to by Dr. Hutchinson.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this Bo rd Itst (rnursday) night, the resignation of the Rev. H. B. G )re was read and acc,pted The salaries of teachers were increased according to the scale, making with several new pupil,teachers an annual increase of about £6O. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of this Couneil was held yesterday (Thursday) under the presidency of Mr. F. Wiswall, C. E., J.P. The business was tbe purely formal one of authorising the payment of interest on the water mort- gages, and did not occupy two minutes. The tnetting then resolved into Highway Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>FALL FROM A ROOF AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>FALL FROM A ROOF AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About noon, on Tuesday last, a youth, named Thomas Knockton, plumber's labourer, of Windmill-street, Run- corn, met with an accident which resulted in slight in- juries to his spine. He was following his oczupation on the roof at Ws Works and in some unaccountable manner slipped and fell a distance of about 20 feet. Constable Breeze attended with the ambulancF., and ren- dered first aid, and subspque itly Dr. NIcD mgall was called in.—At preient Knockton is slightly better and improving as well as can be expected.</p>
        <p>REMARKABLE DELUSIONS.-A STREET SCENE AT WIDNES</p>
        <p>Early on Thursday morning Thomas Bates cauEed a sensation in Sankey.street, Widnes, by jtimping through the kitchen window ot the house where he lived. He raced along the street, blood streaming from his face, until he was stopped by Inspector Strickland, t 3 whom he graphic Illy described how three soldiers with bayenete fixed had killed his father arid mother. Bates recently returned from India after serving in the army, and had drunk heavily.—The magistrates sent him to Whiston Werkhpuse for 14 days. He will be medically examined.</p>
        <p>RMING FIRE AT WIDNES. An alarming* broke out at half-past nine on Wed- nesday morning on the premises occupied by Mr. Wil- liamson, Ditton Cross boot ship, Widnes. It transpired that two boys from the gasworks had been repairing a meter, and by some means had not turned the gas out when leaving the cellar. Smoke was seen issning from the floor by Mrs. Jones, who was in the window at the time. She ran out of the shop, and informed Mr LrAnes a furniture dealer, next door. He attempted to get into the cellar, but owing to the smoke he was driven back. The latter went to the fire station, and during his absence one of the lads descended by a grid into the cellar and turned out the gas. Much damage was done to the boards and beams of the ceiling of the cellar, and a number of pairs of boots an shoes were damaged by the water. How the gas got alight is still a mystery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE ATTEMPTED SUICIDE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Wednesday, before Mr. Benjamin Brown, Patrick Lamer, an elderly man, was brought up on remand charged with attempting to take his life on Monday, 18th September, by cuttin,z his throat with a pair of scissors.—Supethtendent Feely said that he had received a certificate from Dr. Price, of Walton Gaol, stating that the man was sane.—The prisoner on being asked if he was responsible for his actions, replied that he would not b.—Bridget Lamer, prisoner's daughter, said he was a ~iagle woman, and resided with her father at 21, Seel-street, Widnes. He was a labourer at the chemical works. He had been very strange in his manner when out of work, and when in work had to come home on several occasions,suffering from pains in his head. Her mother had died twe years ago, and he had not been the same since. About nine o'clock on Monday night he came downstairs and t-ok a razor from behind a picture. She, however, wrested it from him, and ran outside for assistance, informing Police Constable Falkington. On going upstairs, they found prisoner hacking at his threat with a pair of scissors. These were with difficulty got from him. He said, "It is no use, I will do it yet for I h.ve trouble on my mind."—He was committed to the County Quar- ter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN BEAT BOTH OLDHA.II AND TYLDESIEY ON FORM.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BEAT BOTH OLDHA.II AND TYLDESIEY ON FORM.</p>
        <p>BY THE "SCORCHER."</p>
        <p>The game at Oldham turned out as expected, and both teams faced each other not the least bit afraid. Certainly it was an unfavourable day for the Lin- nets, and Oldham too would have liked finer weather, but what did the climatic aspect matter to two teams eager and ready to battle with one another, as only Runcorn and Oldham can. Once the fun commenced, it was clear that both teams were playing their level best and had every stop out, so that whoever won was bound ts do so on their merits, and this, as results showed, Runcorn did. Oldham were beaten in the pack, at half, three-quarter, and at back. Yet, the score, 3 points to two points, seems to belie this assertion. But consider Runcorn one try, Oldham one 'goal, and having looked at it that way ; think also, of the general expression of the Oldham spectators all round the field, "that they would have liked to buy the whole of the Rnncorn 15 en .bloc and play them as Oldham," then those will() were not present will believe. The game was indeed a great one. Not a single moment of laxity about it; always exciting; interesting, and to a great extent, undecided : for a chance drop on either side would at any time have given a decided lead to the others. The Linnets must be, however, credited with a narrow win, and Oldham were con- fessedly heaten on their best form Leastways on, the best form they have exhibited yet. And furthermore, it is not the first time that the same team have beaten Oldham for they did it at the later stages of last year, and bar accidents will do it again this. Then what will Oldham have to say; perhaps they will then want to buy the ground as well as the team ! Of my favourites, everyone played well, but there is still rcom for improve- mentiThe forwards are a fine lot and keen tacklers, but some of their old passing tactics will be wel- comed, and also a shade more speed if they can put it on. Therefore take a tip from the scorcher and so strive that now you are top of the tree you stick there.</p>
        <p>The Tyldesley game was another exhibition of the powers of the present combination doing duty for Runcorn. Just fancy,immediately after thrash- ing Oldham, in a stiff gam, in a stiff shower of rain, and at their best! to face Tyldeslay on their own ground with only a day between. Why Run- corn were fairly on the "hop" and nothing great was expected from them. True Tyldesley have not won s. match, but on the other hand Runcorn has been defeated on that illfated ground for the past three seasons by them. Tyldesley ware desirous of keeping up that, to them, very satisfactory 'record. From the outset the Linnets showed superior metal, and Tyldesley seemed to realise that special efforts would have to be forthcoming if they hoped to win. They strove hard and contested every inch of ground,and Runcorn fought—on the Monday— one of the hardest battles this season. The weather was against them and several of their members were suffering from the" gruelling" of the previous Saturday's fight at Oldham. 1 bus it was that the tactics of "defence not defiance " were persisted in after a lead was obtained. As a rule it is not in the nature of Runcorn to play any other than an open game all through, but not even I will blame them for Monday's display. They played hard, and without an interval, and were a credit to themselves and their supporters. Tyldesley are a sturdy, strong, hardworking lot, with plenty of talent which I feel confident epuld be turned almost into another Runcorn if—may I be allowed to say it—they had a "Scorcher."</p>
        <p>Winning these last two matches fairly on their merits places Runcorn into the position of the Premier team for this season ; and provided the same 15 are serviceable for the remaining contests there will be rosy views of the cup —or, as" one of the boys" puts it—of " both cups "this year.</p>
        <p>Lieut-Colonel Lowe, retired officer, was lined £5 at Bournemouth on Monday for stliking a lady cyclist with a stick.</p>
        <p>According to " Stubbs's WcPkly Gazette," the number of failures in Eagland and Wales gszettni during the week ending September 23rd was 128. The number in the corre3nrinding week of last year was 148, s'3owing a decrease of 20'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY' SEPTEMBER 29, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY' SEPTEMBER 29, 1899</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT.I</p>
        <p>The Nonconformist section of the Runcorn School Board, I understand, desire the election of the Rev. Father Chambers, in the stead of the Rev. H. B. Gore, resigned, in accordance with an understanding that vacancies should be filled by" arrangement." The resignation was accepted last night, (Thursday), and no doubt the next meeting will see the move made. To any other arrangements that might be put forward, the un- denomißational section will object, and as the sug- gestion leaves the Board similarly constituted as ' heretofore no doubt the affair will be agreed to.</p>
        <p>Several of the Runcorn footballers selected for the representation of Cheshire against Cumber- land entertain some qualms about accepting the jersey for the day, and a deadlock is reported as likely to occur. Personally it is my belief that Run- corn will prove true and sportsmanlike and take the field like men. If they don't my good opinion of them, as well as the opinion of many mores will receive a considerable check The policy of saving the thain for its own benefit, at the sacrifice of what little bit of county football we do get, is decidedly selfish and mean; and such that no true sportsman would be party to. The eyes of Runcorn, Cheshire, England and the World are at present centred upon the Runcorn footballer, and their doings, and surely they will not have it said they were mean and selfish and shirked their duty. No, I think Runcorn will be at Cumberland,and feel sure if they do go fortune will continue to shine upon them—for it only shines on the brave.</p>
        <p>I hear that an interesting presentation took place at Weston, on Wednesday night, when Mr. S Dodd, Runcorn, received a beautifully illurninated address testifying to the appreciation that the "Cadets," of the Sons of Temperance, of that place, have for him.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn. School Board have decided upon a new departure which will be hailed with delight, i.e.,the provision of an evening class for men whose education has been neglected. Buch a class will fill a long felt want and will doubt- less be availed of largely. If it is not readily taken up the Board will most likely withdraw the opportunity as the announcement of the open- ing of the class is ma 'e conditionally' that a suffi- cient number present themselves" The ordinary evening classes which have been so successfully carried on are announced to re-open on Monday, October 9th.</p>
        <p>POACHING ON FROUSEIAM MARSH. A WARNING.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday at the Frodsham Petty Sessions before Messrs. C. Reynolds (presiding), and A. Thomas. Henry Morton Davies, farmer, Church-street, Frodsham, sum- moned George Farrell, Water-street, and Henry Grice, Townfield-lane, for trespassinc, on land belonging to him at Frodsham Marsh, on the 30;h of July. in pursuit of game,—Mr. A. Newman, (Messrs Kershaw and Co.), Manchester, prosecuted, and Mr. Brassey,solicitor,Ches- ter,appeared for both defendants.—Mr. Newman, sub- mitted evidence showing that Mr Davies,Mr George Jones and his son, Thomas Jones-,were watching for poachers. on the morning of the 30% of July, on land in Mr. Davies' occupation, and over which he had the right of sporting at Frodsham Marsh, and almost adjacent to Inca. They saw the two men—the identity of whom Mr. Davies would swear to,—and t e dogs in company with them, in and out of the covers as if in pursuit of game, and complainant and the Jones' gave chase, but did not capture the men. Mr. Newman added that he also represented the Manchester Ship Canal Co., who were desirous of doing something to protect the interests of their tenants. The systematic poaching that was toeing carried on was not the least they suffered from, but the continual trespass caused numt 3rs of sheep to be driven into the canal or across it, and many valuable animals were either lost or drowned. Should the Bench therefore consider the case proved, be asked that they inflict a penalty sufficient to act as a warning to others, ' but at the same time bear the fact in mind that they did not wish to unduly press it.—Mr. Davies gave evidence to seeing the men there and the doge. Be bad been out from about 3 o'clock in the morning. When he first saw the men they were about half a mile away.—Mr. Brassey, for the defence, denied that either of the men was on the Marsh that morning, and said evidence of alibi would be submittei. He also pointed out that a motive for the complainant Davies bringing the charge against Farrell was the fact that sometime previous Farrell had threatened to bring a charge of poisoning his dog against him, and from that time Mr. Davies had a grudge against him. They were both men of good character and nothing was known against them previously.—George Farrell, said on the Saturday night he went to bed at about 10 o'clock, and did not go out again until 5.30 on the Sun- day night.—By Mr. Newman: He saw Grice on the Monday morning having called him by tapping the window when he was passing. Grice told him that Mr. Davies had been to his house but he did not say any- thing more. He had three guns.—Mr. Newman: What do you keep three guns for P—Witness did not reply.— Witness had seen his brother go to bed and was sure h? could not have been out. He had not taken a gun on the Frodsham Marsh for the last two years.—Mrs. Hannah Farrell, gave evidence of her husband. being in the house as stated on the Sunday. - Henry Grice said it was true that Mr. Davies called at his house and found him absent on the morning of the 30th of July, that was about 7 o'clock. Had he called at 5 'or 5-30, however, he would have found him in. He went out about 5.30 as near as he could tell for a walk, and took his dog with him. He first beard of the affair that morning on getting home.—Cross-examined by Mr Newman; He had not been on the Marsh during the last 18 months.—Mithredeth dickers, bricklayer, of Water-street, deposed to meeting Grice as alleged by him on the morning of the 30th of July.—Asked by Mr. New- man whether he could tell whether he was coming from the Marsh or the house of Farrell witness gave no answer.— i he Chairman said they would take the case as proved, and would convict the men. To Mr. Newman: I understand that your people do not wish to press those defendants ? Mr. Newman : We simply ask that it be a warning to others: it mult be put a stop to.—The Chairman : It is a very awkward thing, these sports- men without rights. (Laughter.)—Fined £1 including costs each, or 14 days.—Mr. Brassey applied for time for the defendants to pay.—The Chairman said it was not the practice of the court to grant time. It was left with the Superintendent of Police.—Mr. Brassey : It is a practice in other courts.—The Chairman: But not in this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>FLOWEIt QUEEN AT THE PUBLIC HALL. A PRETTY AND EFFECTIVE PERFORMANCE</head>
        <p>THE FLOWEIt QUEEN AT THE PUBLIC HALL. A PRETTY AND EFFECTIVE PERFORMANCE</p>
        <p>During Wednesday and Thursday evenings the Public Hall, Runcorn, has been filled with large and apprecia- tive audiences, drawn together to witness the perform- ance of the well-known delightful descriptive floral scena " The Flower Queen," and, have sat and listened to a genuine treat nfferded by ab mt 120 Runcorn female voices. On Wednesday night the hall was especially full, and the proceedings were graced with the attend- ance of Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., who was also heard to exprecs great pleasure at the treat afforded him. The principal artistes were: Miss Gertrude Newton (rose). and Mr. Libbis N. Burch (recluse) and the following ladies represented the pr nciple flowers : Misses Gertrude Newton, rose ; Edith Clare, lily ; Withirsgton, crocus ; E D Wright, dahlia ; Crosby, helio trope ; Leathwood, mignonette ; Jecsie Stone, japonica; C Handley, sunflower; D Carneven, violet ; C Lanter, tulip ; Simkin, hollyhock: Alice Taylor, dandelion ; and Olive Clare, passion flewer. The Runcorn Orchestral Society were responsible for the acelmpenimerr,s under the leadership of Mr. Maurice Kruger. Miss Forrester was the pianist, and the wielder of the baton was Mr. H W Handley. The orchestra opened the concert on Wednesday with the overture " Rosamunde " and on Thursday with " Light Cavalry" and a march from " Tannhauser," all of which pieces were creditably executed. Their accompaniment to the " Flower Queen " wag also very masterly. The principals Miss Newton,and Mr. Burch,both proved canal le artistes and were successful in impressing their audience with the quality of their voicea. Of the locel ladies all filled their parts well, hut special mention should be made of Miss Simkin, Miss C. Handley, Miss Stone, and Miss A. Taylor, (the latter excelling in a really diffiult part of the chorus ; nothing but praise should be be- stowed, for the whole performance was maintained by them at a high standard. The semi-choruses of fl,wers : poppies ; all the aspirants ; heather-bells ; wild flowers ; bluebells ; and flower garlands, were also of sweet rl6h qualiey. Especially taking, however, were the dances and evolutions, with lime light effects, which the juven- ile section of the ehorous rendered from time to time, added to Which the superb scenic effect of the stage, got up to represent a section of forest, contributed an har- monious ensemble. The dresses too, of the ladies, call for cemment,and though we do not intend to describe the 120 in detail, we wish to fay that collectively they presented a pleasingly rich and fascinating spectacle. The proceeds of the undertaking will be devoted to the Bethesda Sunday Schools. Great credit is due to Mr. H. W. Handley, and his staff of willing co- operators for the success achieved, and to the following ladies for the excellent way they instructed the children in their several dances: Misses Forrester, Simkin, Cameron, Riley and Wright. Miss Perkins, who was, I perhaps, one of the most energetic, deserves especial credit for her services —The flowersocenrry,and general get up were perfect, and showed that a master hand was engaead in their display, we know not whom to credit, but " they " (?) deserve highly to be named for their most excsllent arrangement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SEWERAGE OF HELSBY,</head>
        <p>THE SEWERAGE OF HELSBY,</p>
        <p>IMPORTAN r INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>Yesterday (Thursd,v), Colonel Darnfor3, C.E., an ! ingrctor ot the Local G wernmnt Board was engaged: i for three hours at Helsby National School, inquiring into the application of the Runcorn Rural District Council for sanction to borrovr £5,200 for works of sewerage for Helsby. There was a good attendance of ratepayers and others. Mr F G Ashton, Clerk to the Rural District Council, stated that the asiesiAble value of the township of Helsby was £8,392, and there were 305 houses. The estimated populacion was 1,525. At present there was no proper system of drainage for the township. Mr A E Geisthoroe, represented Mr W H Radford, of __ . . .. . . . . .</p>
        <p>Nottingham, the inquirer who had pr,pared the scheme</p>
        <p>and decrib,id the details of the proposed drainage, and sad it was intended to pump the sewage fr-im the sewer at Hunting Bridge int i H iol Pool Gutter, which flowed into the Ship Canal. In reply to questioni, witness said it was not intended to filter the sewage but to turn it crude into the Ship Canal. It would be a matter for the Council as to the c e tocsin; of the banks of the Hoot Pool Gutter from deposited sewage.</p>
        <p>Mr. Newman representing the -Ship Canal Company asked was the wa-er ti lii at Hunting Bridge ? Witness Yes : It was always tidal. Mr. Newman said the gates at Eistham were not opened when the tide in the Mersey did not rise higher than the water in the canal and sometimes the gates were not opened for days. Oa those occasions there could lie no tide at all in Hoolpool Gutter. He asked if the Ship Canal had not been there what would the engineer have done then.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gelsthorpe replied : Turn the sewage into the Bierseyl_ _</p>
        <p>Mr: Newman pointed oil% that the Mersey Conser- vancy Board had prevented one local authority turning crude sewage into the river, and the Ship Canal could cot take sewage unless treated. Dr. Adams, Medical Officer to Runcorn Rural Council said a system ot s werige was absolutely necessary for Helsby. He had not advocated one until lately because the to wnsnip had been without a water supply. There were 30 houses on Alvanley-road not included in the present scheme and some means ought to be taken to include them in future. There had not been much . _</p>
        <p>infectious disease in the township recently but property was very scattered and the chances of infection very rare.</p>
        <p>Dr. Vascher, medical officer to Cheshire County Coun- cil, said in 1897 be reported that the water courses of (Rigby were very much pollutel by sewage. Streams were male into channels for sewage; He did not think the present scheme would improve matters at all. It would not cleanse the streams nor would it abate the present nuisance. If the scheme were passed the nuis- ance would be far greater than it was at present. The last half length of sewer had only a fall of five feet and would become an elongated cesspool. No scheme would be successful unfesi the sewage was treated before it was turned into the Ship Canal. Mr. J. Farrington, inspector of nuisances, described the present system of drainage and said there were at present twelve outfalls. Mr. James Taylor supported the scheme and said the Telegraph Works employed five hundred persons for whom water closets were provided and it was absolutely necessary that there should be some scheme to take the sewage away. _</p>
        <p>Mr7C. E. Linaker opposed the scheme on behalf of the Marquis of Cholmondeley,owner of nearly the whole township of Helsby, for Captain Park Yates of lace Hall, and the Frodsham and Helsby Drainage Board, WhiCh had control of the water course in the district. Hoot Pool Gutter into which it was proposed that crude sewage should be drained was one of the main water- courses and since the contruction of the Manchester Ship Canal this watercourse had not been so much afftcted by tides as it was previously. The drinking water of cattle was gained from this brook during the summer months. It was the only water supply to the Ince estate in the summer months. He had known the place for 20 years and in his opinion a scheme could be devised by which the sewage could be disposed of by gravitation, a moderate scheme was desirable. Mr. H. Bancroft, eneine•r, Manchester, opposed the seheme for a number of ratepayers. He contended that there was no necessity for pumping, which meant a large annual expenditure. The latter portion of the Hoot Pool gutter,a distance of 2300 yards would under this scheme become an eloneatei cesspeol,because there was no provision in the scheme for dealing with the , matter in solution and it would be injurious to the health of the people and dangerous for cattle to drink the water. The scheme was not in conformity with the requirements of the Local Government Board that if sewage were not treated it must be turned into tidal water. Witness contended that the scheme instead of costing £5,200 as estimated by Mr. Rstford would cost £8,170; which was £5 15s per head of the population whereas the general cost was between £3 and £4 per head. His estimate of the scheme and the annual ex- penses showed that it was about 11 411 tø the rate making the rate 3s 8i iin the £. He read an extract , from Mr. Ratford's report stating that one objection to turning crude- sewage into the Ship Canal was that many authorities had been forced to take,;their sewage out of the Ship Canal. Mr Newman (Messrs Grundy. Kershaw and Company) opposed the scheme on behalf of the Ship Canal. He said no scheme would be satisfactory to the Ship Canal Company which did not deal with the sewage by means of settling tanks or some other method. Mr. Gelsthorpe said the Ship Canal had cut the town- ship off from the Mersey estuary and the Ship Canal Co. ought to bear the expense of settling tanks or any other process, Mr. Newman said that question had nothing to do with the inquiry. Mr. John C Alier, overseer put in a petition presented , totbe overseers signed by numerous ratepayers, object- ing to the scheme. Mr Taylor inquired the rateable value these ratepayers represented.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier said he did not know. Mr. Taylor said his firm. the Telegraph Works, repre- sented one-third of the rateable value. _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. James Brandreth als) Dpposed the scheme. There was no further evidenle, and the Inspector then made a personal inspection of the district, and intimated that he would replrt in due course.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the above Board, was held at the Workhouse, Dutton, on TuPsday last. There were present :—Mes4rs J Moseley-Williams (chairman). J Meadowcrift, T Reynolds, A R Norman, the Rural District Councillors, with Mr OF Ashton (clerk), Mr W E Hough (deputy clerk). And Mr James Cunnah (master).</p>
        <p>THE NORTH-WESTERN _POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, In accordance with notice,moved that Messrs. A. R. Norman, G, Pilling, and C. E. Linaker, with the Clerk, be appointed the deputation to represent the Board at the North-Western Polr Law Conference to be held in Manchester on the 27th and 28th October ; also that Mr. Bradford be nominated provisionally to fill any vacancy should one of the before-named gentle- men be unable to attend, and that their expenses be paid by the Board. Mr. Knowles seconded.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had been in the habit of attend- ing this conference for 201110 years, and he proposed to attend this year at his own expense: Mr. T. Wright also intr nded to be present at his own cost, and to put the matter in order he suggested that their names b 3 added to the others.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woolward also intimated his intention of attend- ing the conference, and paying his own expenses. The names of the Chairman and Messrs. Wright and Woodward were added to the list and the motion was then:carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>COIiTH&amp;CTS.</head>
        <p>THE COIiTH&amp;CTS.</p>
        <p>The tenders of the following tradespeople for the sup- ply of the House for the half year ending Lady day next were accepted :—George Bowcock, butcher's meat ; Edwin Gregory, ero-eries ; Newton Chambers and Co., disinfectants ; T Jackson Lei, clothing; Percy R Wright, drapery ; R Evans and Co., coal ; Joseph Baziny, boots ; W Fowles straw and potatoes ; Exors. of I Speakman, drugs ; John Barrow, COffiali, T Booth and Sons, convey- ances ; W Grounds, aeneral cartage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>TUE CHILDREN GET A WETTING, AND A TREAT.</head>
        <p>TUE CHILDREN GET A WETTING, AND A TREAT.</p>
        <p>The Master reported that the children who were in- vited to an outing by Mr. and Miss Best, of Ashton Lodge, started on the was', but the weather turned out so unfavourable' as to compel their return to the House which they reached in a very wet condition. However, dry clothing was provided, and the children were re- galed with tea, cake and seed bread kc., meat pies and other luxuries which Mr. and Miss Best forwarded to the houge,so that the lit'le ones did not altec;ether lose their treat —On the motion of Mr. A. R. Norman, a vote of thanks was heartily accorded Mr. and Miss Best.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>OUT-RELIEF.</head>
        <p>THE OUT-RELIEF.</p>
        <p>The amounts granted for nu relief was as follows : Runcorn £80; Frediham £26 and Great Budworth £24.</p>
        <p>A BROKEN-UP 111MESTEAD.</p>
        <p>The man, Simul Grindloy, of Sutton, who a short time ago was sentenced to a term of imprisonment to- gether with his wire for neglect of his six children, ap- peared before the Board and asked that the children be allowed to remain there until his wife came out of prison and he coull again get his home in order,—ln answer to several questions the man said he had only be able to get employment off and on at 3s. a day sine'. he was liberated, and paid 3s. a wTk for his lodgings at Frodsham Bridge.—After the man had retired the Board decided to allow the children to remain for 8 weeks if the man would contribute 6s, a week.—On being made the offer Grindley gladly consented and thanked the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PIGEON SHOOTING MATCH.</head>
        <p>PIGEON SHOOTING MATCH.</p>
        <p>There was a capital attendance at the grounds attached to the Dog and Partridge Inn,Nlanchester- road, on Saturday, to witness a shoot at pigeons between J. Baldry and J. Glover, both of Warring- ton. The stake at issue was £lO, and the conditions to shoot at seven birds each, 24 yards rise, and one ounce shot. Glover outshot his opponent at the sixth bird.</p>
