        <p>TIIE ACCRECATE PRIZE. Messrs. Maple and Rodgers have had three points placed to their credit for the Aggregate Prize, and Messrs. Hall, Back. house, Jones, Auber, Cooke, Funnel: and Mason, one each. The total number of competitors in the running for the £lOO Aggregate Prize is now 34.</p>
        <p>shine on the evil and on the good, and rain to fall on the just and unjust, and it was just the same with His spiritual husbandry. There were few lives which were singled out by great, gifts to do magnificent work for God, but the things which all men needed, every- thing which was requisite for life and godliness, were offered to all. Every fife had its privilege. The preacher asked what made so many indifferent to religion ? Why did they come across so many who went through life apparently witheut any deep thought or concern for truth, righteousness, and immortal hope ? It was not because they were great sinners or because they had no opportunities, but because their lives had gradually al. most without their knowing it, drifted into indifference. The thorns of careless habits of life, of weak and enervating selfishness, had sprung up and choked the seed. They were, the preacher said in conclusion, that day joining in a great act of thanksgiving, and teanhe. giving was always associated in the Christian mind with offering to God. Thanksgiving and dedlcation went to- gether, and what more beautiful offering could they bring to God than the quiet prayer that they might be more faithful in the coming days to His reasonable ser- vice. They could not serve Him with half their mind. There must be freshness in th-ir love, enthusiasm in their faith, and they must be preparPd to make some sacrifice. They could bring to His service, if not great gifts that which was much better, the manliness, the simplicity, the earnestness and the deep personal religion of the honest and good heart.</p>
