      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services were held at the Parish Church on Sunday, when large congregations attended the morning and evening services. Sermons were preached a: each service by the Rev. G. W. Gilbertson, vicar of St.Alban's, Stockport, mid formerly curate at Daresbury, fr:im the words " Labour not for the meat perisheth,"&amp;c., and "First the blade, and then the ear," &amp;c. Special hymns, and the anthem " Thou crownest the year," by Edwyn Clare,were admirably rendered by the choir, and also the Choral Communion'service after morning service, at which a very large number of parishioners communicated. Tae offertories were de- voted to the Sunday schools and the Warrington In- firmary. and amounted to nearly £lO. Sr. JOHN'S CEURCH, EARLESTOWN The harvest festival s.rvices in connectiou with the aV)ove church were cmtinued and concluded on Sunday. The morning sorvice was conduc-ei th,- Rev. James Ryder, vicar of S. Peter's, Newton, and the evening by the Rev. R. Tibles.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN CHAPEL, EARLESPOWN. Special harvest festival servic-s were held in the above chapel on Sunday. The building was very prettily de- corated with fruit, flowers, vegetables, and corn, the mottoes being a feature of the decorations. Although the weather was ant bin but p-opitious there were large congregatioos at each service, the pulpit being occupied morning and evening by the Rev. G. Lunn of Prescot. Special anthems were sung hr the choir. In the afternoon the sacred cant Ira "The Messiah's Reign," was rendered by the cheir before a large audience. The principal soloists were Messr4. F. Ciare, Twmlow,Wehs- ter, Misses Clarke, Gosliu. M. Barr, and Higgins. Mr. T. Clare conduct ai and Miss Greenall presided at the organ. On Monday evening the servivs were continued, when the Rev. C. Sellars,the new circuit superintendent preached. WARGRAVE CHURCFI, EARLESTOWN. The annual harvest festival services were held in the above church on Sunday. The church was decorated vimething after the style of previous years, but there was not the same profu;ion of fruit and vegetables. The decorations were carried out by Mrs. Carson, the Misses Carson and several lady metnlprs of the congre- gation. The pulpit at the morning service was occupied by the Rev. Hon C. F. Cross, vie .r of Stretton, and in the evening by the Rev. Canon Penrhyn. rector of Win- wick. Special hymns and anthems were sung by the choir. In the afternoon an organ recital was given by Mr. E. Leyland, with musical selections by the choir. The following was the proeramme submitted :—Organ, "La Serenata " ; vocal solo," The Better Land," Mr. S. Dring ; anthem, " 0 Sing unto the Lord," soloist. Miss R. Uren ; organ, Fantasia on "My God, my Father." vocal solo " 0 avine Redeemer, ' Miss A. Molyneux ; crgan. " Schubert's Serenade," vocal Solo " The PPgrims " Miss K. Corlett vocal solo " The Voice of the Fathee," Mr. W. Carson ; organ, ' " Coronation March," chorus, " The Wildernes.." The I services were continued on Monday evening, when an ' excellent discourse WAS given by the Rev W. J. Adams, vicar of St. Augustine's, Liverpeol. The collections throughout amounted to upwards of £l4, in aid of the Church Renovation Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A FOOTBALLER.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>While playing football during the dinner hour on Tuesday on the ground attached to the works of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Company, Wilders- pool, an employee named John Ifollingworth (24), living in Marbury-street, Latchford, had the mis- fortune to break one of his legs. He was taken to the Infirmary, where his, injury was attended to. LADIES BIIST GLACII BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, BILIDGE-STRBET.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RESULT OF "LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>RESULT OF "LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>£27 10s. WON</p>
        <p>The following were the result of matches played Saturday September 23 :—Two of the matches in this competition— Chesterfield v. New 13: ighlon and Gravesend v. Queen's Park Rangers—were not played The other matches resulted as follow!: Wolverhampton Wanderers beat Glqssop North End, Notts. Forest beat •:toke. Everton beat Liverpool, Man- chester City beat Notts. County, Luton drew with Leicester Fosse, Walsall beat Woolwich Arsenal, Southampton beat Bristol City, Huddersfield drew with Halifax, Rochdale Hornets beat Warrington, Devonport Albion beat Exeter. As only ten matches were played, the prizes offered lor 11 or 12 correet results could not be Won, but we have decided to award the £2O prizl to the competitors who gave ten correct results, and the £5 prize to the competitors who gave nine correct results. The following lucky individuals will there- fore share the £2): MR. B. MAPLE, Oaklands, Bushmead Avenue, Bcif 3rd (2 coupons).</p>
        <p>MR. J. W. RODGERS, 16, Mill Green. Staveley. ANOTHER WARRINCTON WINNER. The following competitors gave nine correct results and therefore divide the £5 consolation prize:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Thera were large congregations at the harvest thanks- giving services in connecthn with the above chapel held on Sunday. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. T. Bi.-hop (superintendent), who took for the subject of his discourse " Continuity of merciei," The choir sang the anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem," the solo being taken by Mr. Corbett. A special chil- dren's service was held in the afternoon, when an ad- dress was deliverel by the TON. C. H. Hocken. Special music was rendered. The solo "Nazareth" was sung by Mr. Gough, and Mr. Baker ably rendered" Be thou faithful unto death" (" St. Paul.") At the evening service there was a very large congregation, and the Rev. C. H. Hocken preached on " Sowing and reaping." Mr. Baker sang " What will the harvest be ?" ana the choir was heard to advantage in the anthem " While the earth remaineth." ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued in the above church on Sunday. There was a good con- gregation at the morning service, when the Rev. W. Bracecatrip, of St. Peters, Warrington, occupied the pulpit, and preached an appropriate sermon from the words "Let bath grow together until the harvest" Matthew iii, 30. Special harvest hymns were sung. In the afternoon a children's service was held, when the Rev. T. H. G. Hollingworth, of St. Paul's Warrington, delivered a very interesting address. There was a crowded attendance at the eveninz service, every avail- able seat being occupied. The Rev. E. St. Arnaud Duke, M.A., Vicar of St. Dunstan's, Liverpool, and formerly of St. Barnabas, was the special preacher. The service commenced with the singing of the processional hymn " Onward Christians Soldiers," and during the evening Harvest hymns were sung_ The choir rendered the anthem will feel my flock" in a manner which showed careful training, Mr. VVoodcack taking the solo. Mr. Thomas Evans ably presided at the organ, and, as on Thursday evening, a number of the members of Messrs. Fletcher Russell's band, attended and accom- panied the singing of the hymns and anthem. The Rev. E. St. Arnaud Duke preacnel a very,,impressive sermon, taking for his text Leviticus 23, 39, " When ye have gathered in the fruit of the land, ye shall keep a feast unto the Lord." He said that perhaps some of them were old enough to remember the introduction of harvest festivals into the Church in the form that they now knew them, which took place only thirty years ago. They would have to go a long way back into human history to find the origin of harvest festivals. The Jews of old were told to keep three harvest festivals. They kept festivals and services at the time of the barley har- vest, the reaping of the corn, and when the crops were gathered in. If they looked in the calendar of their Frayer Books they would find that the first, day of August was'still called "Lammas Day" and that was a skort form for " Loafrnis Day." Formerly in the parish churches of this country on that day, a loaf made from the first cut corn was brought and solemnly pre- sented at God's altar and was afterwards used for the Holy Communion, without the celebration of which no Harvest Festival was complete. Since those days the seasons had changed he supposed, for they kept their harvest festivals mach later n)w. He thought that keeping one weak in the year for harvest thanksgiving services was a splendid institution. Some said it was a dodge for raising menoy, hut that was n-t tree. The services were to show thcir protestation of faith in the Providence of Almighty God. At the conclusion of his discourse the rev, gentleman appealed to the congregation for support on behalf of the choir fund. He asked them to show their gratitude to God by opening their hearts and opening their pockets to help the choir. It had been a great pleasure for him to speak to them that night. It was his privilege to w- rk and worship in that church on the very day of its censec:eatinn, and he had spent many a happy hour within its walls. Those happy hours would never fade from his memory. He hoped to live to see a chancel added to the church, and also ta see the side aisles built, but above all to see the whole church filled with faithful and worshipping p^ocle—people who were leading lives worthy of sons and daughters of Almighty God.—The offertories at all the services were on behalf of the choir Lind and realized a satisfactory amount, being in excess of last year. CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL. The harvest thanksgiving services were held in this church on Sunday, the pulpit being occupied morning: and evening by the Rev. W. H. Drummond. Appro- priate hymns and anthems were sung. At the evening service, the rev, gentleman spoke upon the words :-- " And that which fell among tholes are they which, when they have heard, go forth and are choked with cares and riches and pleasures of this life, and bring no fruit to perfec'ion. But that on the good ground are they which, in an honest and good heart, having heard the word, keep it, and bring forth fay' with patience." (St.Leke viii.l4 and 15),1n the course of his remarks.Mr• Drummond observed that the `last thing the Lord meant to teach by the parable of the sower was the universality of the Divine gift. God the sower was im- partial in his dealings with men. He caused His sun to</p>
      </div>
