      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>-44</head>
        <p>-44</p>
        <p>UNSOLICITED TESTIMONY.</p>
        <p>No. I.—Mr. Owen, Ty Main Farm, Newborough, writes as follows :—" I can give the highest praise to the valuable remedy, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. I had long suffered from Debility and Weakross,and really thought I should never recover. My medical men were privately of Use same opinion. But when I commenced taking this Precione Medicine,to my great surprise and joy, as well as that of my family, I rapidly improved, and now feel myself Tate another being ; and I shall ever praise Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters as The Best Remedy for Debility and Weakness. No. 2.—"lr. Edwards, of Williamstown, writes as fol- lows :—" I can safely say that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters have done wonders for me. After suffering for twelve months from Debility, and trying several doctors and different remedies without any benefit, I was ad- vised to try Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, and the re- sult has been good." No. 3.—Mr. Brookes. 89. Kelshaw-street, Liverpool, writes as follows :—" I have suffered from Indigestion and Liver Complaint for many years, but after taking the Wonderful Meiitine, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters for only two months,l am happy to say it has given me great relief." No. 4.—Mr. Evans, 41, Henry-street, Birmingham, writes as fol:ows :—"I have very great pleasure in bear- ing testimony to the benefit I have received from taking Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. lam never without a boLtle in the house,and it is invaluable to me. I suffered very much from Indigestion, Nervousness, and Debility, and often I had very severe pains in my head, so that to me it has proved quite a boon, as I can rely upon it to give me relief. I confidently recommend it to everyone I meet who suffers in the same way, and my recommen- dation has always proved a blessing," Ne. s.—Mrs. Kirkham, 31, Danstall-street, Wolver- hampton, writes as follows :—" I have suffered from Indigestion and pains in the Head from three to four years, but after taking two bottles of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, I feel completely cured, and never felt better in my life." No. 6.—Mre. Williams, 11, Surrey-lane, Battersea. writes as follows :—" I shall not fail to recommend Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters to all sufferers that come under my notice, as it is a Medicine that I have proved to be more excellent even than what you profess it to be.</p>
        <p>The World-renowned Preparation Is sold in bottles 2s 9d and 43 63 each, everywhere,or the Proprietors will forward direct on receipt of stamps to the above value carriage free. The Sole Proprietors are the Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. 13,-ware of Imitations. This is most im- portant. When porch ising, always see the name, " Gwilym Evans " on Label, Stamp, and Bottlg,without which none are genuine.</p>
        <p>GWILYII EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. GWILYM EVA.NS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>MOND-LT.—Before Mr. Monks (in th,-3 chair), Councillor Shaw Green, awl gr. S. Wright. . .</p>
        <p>BREACHEs:or THE Pi:seg.—Patrick eurcell, Bank-street, was charged on a warrant with committing a breath of the peace by being disorderly in Bridge-street OR the let July. Joseph Healey. formerly a member of the police force, said that at 11.50 on the date in question he was on duty at Market Gate when he was called to !dr. Shaw s shop, in Bridge-street, to eject prisoner, tie did so and advised the man to go away. Prisoner went down Bridge-street but returned to the shop again and witness ejected him a second time. It we.s raining at th time, and prisoner cams back to the shop and struck Mr. Shaw in the face with his wet cap. Witness reported him. —The Chief Constable said that prisoner was served with a summons, but failed to appear at court. He said he had been there so recently hat he thought the magistrates would deal severely with him if he appeared then.—Prisoeer was bound over to keep the peace and ordered to nay the costs which amounted to 153 —John Foy, 23, School-brow. and John Dutton, Silver-street, were summoned for committing a breach of the peace by fighting In Silver-street on the 16th September.—P.C.Edwards proved the case and defendants were boun over and ordered to pay the costs.—Williara Bradbury, 21, Annie-street. and John Roberts. 56, Chorley-street, were summoned for a similar offence.—P.C. Clough proved the case and defendants were bound over and ordered to ply 7s. costs. MILITARY CLOTHING TAKEN FROM THE BIRRACKe.—John Butler, 1?, Percival's Fold, was charged with receiving from a soldier at Orford Barracks, a military overcoat and shirt, contrary to the Army Act.-3uperintendent lionaghan said that at 9 o'clock on Saturday night he went into a public- house in Scotland-road. Prisoner was inside and as soon as he saw him (witaess) he stepped behind another man. Being suspicious he went to him and found that ea was wearing a military overcoat. He said he had bought it from a soldier. Witness examined the coat and found that th buttons were cut off. They were afterwards found in the man's wickets. He afterwards discovered that he was wears log a military shirt. He charged him with the theft and he said he had bought them from a soldier in uniform who was on furlough from Barv. The other pertion of the kit had since been found.—Prisoner said that a soldier told him to look after the coat whilst he went for a drink, but he never came for them again.—A Sergeant from the Barracks identified the articles as being military ones. They were worth about 18s. He had got the remainder of the kit Prisoner who had made :fourteen appearances was fined 433 with the alternative of a month's imprisonment. KEEPING A DONKEY IN A WORKSHOP.—The Ca9B against John Forster, who was charged with causing a nuisance by keeping a donkey in his workshop was withdrawn it being stated that the Street Improvement Committee had made an order for the workshop to be pulled down. DRUNKENNESS.—"AgERRIR TO THE NEIGHBOURHODD."— Bodelia. Farrel!, (16), 51, Foundry-street was fined 5s for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street on the V.th September. —P.S. Lewis proved the case; Daniel Higharn, 15, Powys-street, was summoned for being drunk and dis- orderly in Atherton-street on the 18th inst.—He pleaded not guilty.—P.O. Clough said that at 7-15 on Tuesday night he was on duty in Powye-street when he heard a disturbance taking place in Atherton-street. On proceeding there ha saw the defendant who was very drunk with his coat and vest off, threatening his wife. He was using very bad laeguage, and said he would wring his wife's neck if he got held of her. He was acting more like a madman than anything else, He asked him to go into the house hut he refused, but eventu- ally some neighbours managed to get him inside.--The Chief Constable said Higham made his 15th appearance. He was a terror to the neighbourhood in which he lived.—Defendant was fined Sc and costs with the alternative of seven days' imprisonment. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Edward Coyne, 11, Dial-at., was fined 5s and costs for making 1139 of obscene language in Dial- street, on toe 16th September. —P.O. Smith proved the ease. SETTING FIREWORKS OFF IN THE STREET.—A WARNING To Bore AND SittoPtcs:EPcni.—Walter Saunders, 144, Church Place, was summoned for setting fireworks off in Manchester- road on the 16th Septernber.—He pleaded gu ity.—P.C. Smith said that at 8-15 on the date in question he was pro- ceeding down Manchester-road, and when at the end of Gas- kell-street, he saw about twenty or thirty fireworks come over the hcardinge. He chased the boys who let them off, but could only catah the defendant. When caught ho had a fire. work in his hand, and it exploded in their faces.—The Chief Constable said he had received a great many complaints about that sort of thing. The fireworks frightened horses and nervous people. The police were going to take proceed- ings in every case that same under their notice, and shopkeepers who sold fireworks to children under thirteen years of age would be prosecuted. They had been warned about doing so, but seemed to disregard what had been told them.—Defendant was fined 2s 64. INCITING Does TO FlGHT.—William Connor, fish hawker, 2, Hardy-street, and Gearge Davidson, 23, Victoria-street, were charged with all swing their dogs to be at large unmuzzted in the Market Place on the 19th September.— Inspector Rowbottom said that at 11 a.m. on Ma date in question he was informed that a dog fight was taking place in the Market. He preceeded there -an.l saw a lance crowd with the two defendants in the centre. Mr. W. 13. ThorAton complalned to him that the men had been making their dogs fight. Both dogs were without muzzles. He asked Connor if he knew that they had not got their muzzles on and he said "Yes. His (Davidson's) dog came to mine and got him by the neck so I nulled his muzzle off and let hum have a pop') at him." Davidson said the same aboat Connor's dog. He examined the dogs and found Davidson's had its ear split and had been bitten between theishoalders. Connor's dog was suffering from a bite on the shoulder. He obtained defendant's names and addresses and reported them.—Defend- ants were then charged with cruelty to the dogs by incited them to fight.—They weeded not guilty. The Chief Constable said he submitted that taking the dog's muzzles off was an act Of iracitement.—Connor said that Davidson's dog was 401bs in weight, whilst his was only :9/lbs—lnspector Rowbottotn Betel that Connor's dog was a fox terrier and Davidson's was an Airedale terrier—lnspector Canning said he examined the dogs. Davidson's Was an Airedale terrior anct had been badly bitten. Connor's dog had three bites on the shoulder—Defendants admitted hav- ing taken the muzzles off. Davidson said that if he had not done so, Cannor's dog wonld have "chewed his up " Connor, —And his is 10Ib to mine 19. (Laughter. David- son said Connor wanted him to go cliwn Arplev, and he would knock his eyes up.—The magistrates decided to fine defendants Is, for allowing their dogs to be unmuzzled, and ss. and costs for inciting the animals to fight CRUELTY To A PONY.—" NOTHING BUT SKIN AND BONES.— James Marsh, Old Barrow Hall, Great Sankey, was sum- moned for Cruelly ill-treating a pony by working it whilst in an unfit state on the 15th September. —Mr. Frank Kerfoot said that on the date in question he was in Liverpool-road going in the direction of Warrington, when he saw a boy and a girl in charge of a pony attached to a spring cart. No icing that it had a difficulty in walking he examined it and found that it was in a poor condition, being nothing but skin and bones There was some tagging on its back to keep the saddle from rubbing against a wound. The-e were sores on the fet- lockenand the animal was entirely unfit to work. The children said they had been taking their father to work in the cart. Inspector Canning. R.S.P.C.A., said that on the 19th inst., he saw the pony. It was a blac:c gelding, and was in poor condition. It was very weak, and had an old wound about the size of a two shilling piece, which was suppurating, o Its back. He afterwards saw the pony at 13 arlestown pulling a cart containing vegetables It was a very bad OSSE3.—P.S. Cheney corroborated.—Defendant's wife, who appeared on behelf of her husband, promised to have the ant vial destroyed. and the mazistrates adjourned the case for a week to see if defendant did so.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Mr. Birchill (In the chlir), and Councilor</p>
        <p>Sh9.w Green.</p>
        <p>OBICRNE LANGUAGE.—JArnes Taylor, a cornerman was charged with making use of obsceoe language in Horse- market-street at 8.49 the previous night —P.C. Ccoper (23) said that at the time in question he was on duty at NI trice Gate when he saw the prisoner following a woman, lie west making use of filthy language towards her, and said he would kick her head off. He was under the influence of drink. Witness took him into cus,ody and he was locked up.—The Chief Constable said prisoner was one of the biggest scamps In Warrington, having been before the mnistrates eleven times. mostly fr theft. He WAS sent to prison for 14 days in July, for vagrancy.—The Chairman said he hoped the dolice would keep their ears open. as there was a great deal of bad language used about the town.—The Chief Constable : I am sure your worships will give us credit for doing that.— Prisone.• was fined ss. and costs, or in default seven days,</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESIONS</p>
        <p>THURSDAY—Before Mr. T. J. Gillespie: (chairman), Colonel</p>
        <p>J. L. Wood. and Mr. Q. B. F. Borron.</p>
        <p>THE JURY LISTS.—he jury lists for the townshins, were presented. The Magistrates Clerk asked Mr. A. Brirnelow, the assistant overseer for Sauthvrarth-with-Croft, why the name of Mr, Joshua Be I htd been struck off. Mr. Brim,tlow replied that Mr. Ball was a magistrate. The Clerk said that did not disqualify him and the name was ordered to be put on the list. The other lists were all approved of. BREACHES or THE HIGHWAY REGULATIONS.—WiI!iarn Fair- clough was fined Is. and costs for driving a horse without, having any reins attached to it on the 16th September ; George Dagnall was fined 2s. 6d. for 3eing in charge eta waggon which had not a lighted lamp attach td to it on the night of the 20th September. ASSAULT AT LowToN. -Charles Russell was summonoi by Joseph Glynn for assaulting him at Lowton on the 16th September.—Complainant said that at 11.30 p m. on the date in question he was walking down Bank-street when ha met defendant, who struck him in the face and knotked him to the ground, without any provocation.—Williara Blackburn and Walter Kershaw gave corroborative evidence, and a fine of 53 and cost, was imoosel.</p>
        <p>THRFT FROM A SHOP AT GOLBORNE --SaMeti Clarke, Henry Kershaw, and Harry Marsh. boys, were charged with steal- ing from a shop No. 25, Heath-street, Golborne, at about 10 p.m. on the 26th inst., three rubber balls, valued at 9d, the property of Isaac Eokersley.—They pleaded guilty.— Prosecutor said that he was a general dealer, having a shop in He th-street, Golborne. At the time mentioned. in conse- quence of something that he was toll, he went in tne shop and saw the defendant Clarke behind the counter. He had three rubber balls in his hand, and was in his stocking feet. The other boys were stood outside and when they eaw him they ran away. He detained Clarke until the arrival of a policeman. Defendants were ordered to receive six strokes with the birch.</p>
        <p>A OoxectENTIOUI3 OBJF:CTOR.—A man named George Coyle. applied for a certificate for the exemption of his child from vaccination. He :mid he believed it, would do the child neither harm nor good and did not believe in It—The Clerk: That is not a conscientious objection. Are you prepared to swear that in your opinion vaccination is injurious to health. —Applicant : Yes. sir.—The Chairman : And you would sooner run the risk of the child having small pox than have it vaccinated.—Applicant : Yes sir.—The application Wl3 granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING NEGLECT OF WARRINGTON CHILDREN -••• MOTHER SENT TO PRISON. At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, before Messrs. Birchall and R. T. Fairclough, Charlotte Plun- kett, widow, 19, Stamford-street, was charged with neglecting her four children, Mary, Frank, Maggie and James, on September 21st and other days in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering and injury to health.—Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf ot the N.S.P.C.C. said that no doubt the woman was badly off but she had neglected the caildren so often that the Society felt compelled for the children's flake to bring her before the migistretee. Sae had been prosecuted before in 1897 and the case was adjourned on her pro- mising to effect an improvement. She seemed tem- porarily to have mended her ways, but now, the state of the house was something terrible. Prisoner resided in Stamford-street, and was given to drinking. ,She had four children, Mary, Fr ink. Maggie and James aged 10, 7, 3,and 16 months respectively. Toe h)use was visited by Inspectors H tckett and Taylor and another officer on Wednesday. The only furniture was a small table and a wooden squab. The cooking utensils con- sisted of a saucepan and a wooden bucket, Tnere was nothing else whatever in the house, there being no beds of any description. The two youngest children were lying almost naked on the squab. The other boy was lying undressed on the floor by the fire grate. All the children were filthy dirty and verminous, and the by Frank when asked to stand up by Inspector Hackett, was so cramped with lying onl the floor that be could not do so. Prisoner admitted she had no m ney whatever, and it was decided for the benefit of the children that she should be prosecuted. The children were taken to the workhouse, and Mr.. Fernside, the portress could speak as to their condition when they were admitted. Ms youngest child, aged 16 menthe, weighed only 14 pounds, whereas it should have weighed 231bs. It was taken to the Infirmary to see if it could not be brought to its normal weight. Prisoner would not go to the workhouse and the Society could do nothing but bring her before the magistrates. Mr. Robert Isherweod was called and said he had been repeatedly shut out of the house when he called for rent. He had occasienaly been able to get inside the house, and had found the children lying on the floor almost without clothing.—lospecter Hackett corroborated Mr. Browne's staternent.—lnspctor Taylor said be visited the house with Inspector Hackett, be never saw such a case of misery in his life.—Mrs. Fernside said the chil- dren were admitted to the workhouse on Thursday , night. They were very dirty and verminous. The youngest child weighed only 14 pounds. —Mr. W. Jones, school attendance officer gave corroborative evidence.— Prisoner was cnnmitted to gaol for a month. AN UNHAPPY MARRIAGE.—A BRUTAL HUSBAND. , At tbe Warrington Borough Police Court on Thursday before Mr. Chandley (chairman), and other magistrates John Appleton, a bricklayer, of 20. Hume-street, was summoned on a charge of aasaulting and beating his wife, Mary Appleton, on the 23rd September.—Mr. Browne represented the complainant and stated that the parties were married at St. Michael's Church, Liverpool, in November, 1898. The defendant left his work at onel o'clock on Saturday but did not arrive home until 5-30,1 and he was then under the influence of think. If he would keep sober he could earn goad wages and had earned as much as 503 a week. On Saturday he commenced to use very bad language, but the complainant took no notice of it and suggested to him that she should take his club money in order to save him from going Out again. He told her to get la,s clothes ready and in getting himself dressed he set fire to some ferns and fans by the gas flame. She commenced to cry and he burnt some more and also a portion of the cupboard door. He then rushed at her and struck her behiad the ear which raised a lump. Afterwards he got her by the throat and said he would strangle her. On leaving the house he pulled out a clasped knife and threatened that if he met any of her relatives he would put it to their hearts. At 11 30 p.m. he returned home and dragged his wife out of ted, and commenced to throw things about the house, including the knife box. He again commenced this kind of behaviour on Sunday morning and told his wife to clear out. She did so and she was afraid to live with him again. There was one child of tbd marriage. If the magistrates were convinced that an aggravated assault had taken place he would apply for a separation order.—Mrs. Appleton was then called and bore out Mr. Browne's statement.—A neighbour named Mrs. Bertha Champion stated that she saw the complainant's head swollen. She heard the dis- turbance in the house.—The magistrates fined the de- fendant 103 and costs and granted a separation order, the defendant to contribute 15s a week towards his wife's maintenance.—The Chairman said a more brutal CMS could scarcely be brought before the Bench.—The defendant said the mother-in-law was the cause of all the bother.—The Chairman (continuing) said the defen- --dant was a disgrace to hissea and a more brutal husband • could not exist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>- - WARRINGTON BOROUGET POLICE COURT. SATAIRDAy. —Before Messrs. Birchall (chairman) and H. T. 4, Fairclough. ASSAULT os A PoLICENUN.—Robert Stuart was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Dallam-lane at 3.15 ou Friday afternoon, and with assaulting P.C. Clough at the same time. —P.C. Clough said that at the time in question lattu he was on duty in Horsemarket-street when he was sailed to the residence of Mr. Wood, a master tailor, in Bewsey-street. He went there and found prisoner in Mr. Woods' workshop. Ile was very drunk and made Use of bad language. He refused to leave thepremises, so witness had to eject him. He got him into Back Dallam-lane. when, without the slightest provocation he struck him a violent blow on the side of his face. Witness took him into custody. He was very disorderly on the way to the Poice Station.—Mr. 11:.-Asert Wool, master tailor, 57. Bewsey-street. said he went for P.C. Clough. Prisoner called on him a week ago and asked or work, and knowing he was a good work- man he gave him employment. On Friday he paid him his wages. and he went away satisfied. He return ,d two hours later under the influence 'of drink. Witness asked him to go out of the workshop, but he refused to do so, and remained there for three-quarters of on hour. He used bad language in the hearing ot females who were in the room. Witness sent for the consta,le Indira</p>
        <p>,oW •</p>
        <p>ejected the man by force. When he got outside prisoner tnrned round and deliberately struck the officer in the face. Prisoner was a very excitable man He fell over a cliff in Scotland some time ago and cut his head open, and When he took beer it effected him very much—Prisoner said he was not in the worlishop for the time mentioned by Mr. Wood. He could not understand why the officer should arrest him as he did not create any distnrbanee.—The magistrates took a serious view of the case and fined prisoner 208. with the alternative 014 days' imprlsonmpilt. DRUNK AND DISORDEDLT.--Martlit Griffin, who made his • - 18th appearance was, on the evidence of P.O. Sankey tined 711 hs. and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road at 7.55 on Friday night. .</p>
        <p>To 1110TEa Rli:—STlT."'iVinslow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by miiii,,ns of mothers for their zhildten whlleteethiuß. with perfeebsuccess. It will relieve : the Poorsufferer Immediately. It is pleasant • kot tAste ; and , produces Ratural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from piin and the little cherub awakes bright as a buttbn."Of all Chemists It lid per bottle.—Advt. it,</p>
