      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29 18991</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29 18991</p>
        <p>Fernival, Joseph Bushill, J. Robinson, Robert Peers, and rtobert Jones. young men all of Runcorn, were summoned for card playing on the 17th of September on the public high- way at Runcorn.—All, with the exception of Bushell, pleaded guilty.—Bushill stated he was not playing cards, but was simply reclining noon the canal bank.—Constable Bowyer, stated at 10-30 p.m., on Sunday night the 17th inst. from information received he went in the direction of the Bridge along the Canal bank aiid alma across Bushill whom he took to be " keeping nicks " for the other defendants who were playing cards. Subsequently, he again visited the spit and rushed them, with the result that he captured one who c infessed that money had passed and that he had lost 4s. 6ii. 11,3 knew threes of the others. As for Bushill he was watching for theca. He did not see him playin.; cards. lie hut been tole about these young men playing cards at the top of Nelson street some time ago and numerous cornpiaints had beel laid. The night was a beautiful moonlight, night ; it bein, almost like day —The Magistrates' Clerk : Then there is n, evidence against Sushill 'Witness : No, nothing else.— The Bench dismissed the Case agaisist Bushill. Booth as a first offender was fined 5s and Ss 6d costs or 7 days ; Jones and Peers, who had one previous conviction standing against them, were fined 103 and Is 6,1 costs each or 14 d Lys in default ; and Robinson and Fernival, against whom there were four previous convictions, were fined 15s and Is 6,1 costs or 21 days.—Booth and Jones paid their fines, the rest went to prison. Scsoor. CASES.—The following pareats, summoned at the instanc of the Runcorn School Board for ne4ecting their children's education, were each fined 6d and 4s 6d costs : James Moore. Brie's Kiln Hooses ; Joseph Taylor. Boats. ca cases) ; Thomas Woods, senior, Cooper-street ; Peter Knight, Mill-street ; B. Flannigan, Cooper-s'reet, ; Richard Done. Cartwright-street ; Peter Orme. Edith-street ; Emma Wat- son, Ellesmere-street ; George Morrison (2 cases) ; ghaw- street ; Thomas Hoyle. Princess-street ; Alfred Wakefield, sear.. Bridgewater-street ;Willam R. Sargsnt, Wellington- street, and John Fists, Cooper-street.</p>
        <p>ASLEEP IN CHARGE.—William Rimmer pleaded not guilty to a charge of.being asleqo whilst in charge of a horse and cart at Runcorn, on tue 25th of July. —P.C. Bancroft, however, asserted the man was asleep, and that he had to run after the vehicle before it drew up.--eined 61 and 91 61 costs.</p>
        <p>N view of she approaching Football Season, it may be in- teresting to state that the world-famed manufacturers. GEO. (+. BUSSEY &amp; Co., of London, are preparing a stock of Balls, which tor quality, uniformity, and durability, will be unique. These b dis will be distinguished by the app ,arance thereon of their well known trade mark 4r—egle. Ask the local Sports Dealer for their Catalogue. If not procurable, apply to 36 &amp; 33, Queen Victoria-street London Factories —Peckham, London; and Ellmswell,</p>
        <p>* * Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that r.p.irts of matches payed on Saturday must be sent t,) the Head °Mc*. Bewsey-street, Warrington, n3t later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
        <p>both teams playing with equal determination and dash. The Cestrians showed their anxiety by an attempt at goal by Farmer from nearly half way, which come to nothing, and when the backs got possession they used their opegings to better advantage than the Oldha-n men had. Thomas cleared one attack with a penalty, but Ruacorn were not t be denied, and Davies and Thomas almost let them in by faulty play, Thomas jest recover- ing himself in time. liitimately, hi)wever, Runcorn a,2hieved their ambition, as Joliey passed out to Faulkner, and he to Jones, who threw to Butterworth for the latter to score a clever try. Farmer missed the goal. A point behind, the Oldham men struggled hard, and an effort by Fletcher and Williams placea Runcorn's lice in jeopardy. The defence was superb, however, and for the rest of the game Runc.arn were the aggres- sors for the most part. Whilst Oldham tried unsuccess- fully to open out the play to their own advantage, Run- corn Hied upon long kicking, in which Houghton and Jolley were prominent, to maintain their lea 1. Runcorn were tin better team on the whole, bet more especially at half-back, and the v..irdict of the crowd was that they had thoroui,nly earned their victory. Final score : Hanlon], one try (three points) ; Oldham, one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>id !ra ig</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>--ow- WATER. POLO.</head>
        <p>--ow- WATER. POLO.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. WARRINGTON v. EVERTON.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded attendance at the Corporatirin Baths, WarritiLtton, on ruesday evening, to wirine,is this replayed match. It will be remembered that the first match had to be stopped owing to the disordealy con- duct of the spectators when tne referee allowed the Liverpool Club a questionable goal. The Associatiors considered the appeat of rhe E verton Club and ordered the match to be replayed in the Warrington Baths and appointed Mr. J. Hunt, of Manchester, the well-kuown international player, to act as referee. Prior to the match several club races took place,inclading a 100 yards handicap which was won by A, Boscaw, and an exhib-- tints of fast swimming by A. Baker and W. Sutton. Baker swam eight len4th3 its anias. 55Pecs. and Sutton did it in 3mlns. 13secs. Before the commencement of the match Mr. Locker made an appeal to the specat ors to =lila themseves in a manner which would not cast a nEfle2tiala on the town. The Everton team arrived late, and it was after nine o'clock when the teams entered the water as follows :—Warrtngton A G Robertson, goal ; A Bosaow and J E Cronshow, backs; W Ashton, half-back; A Ashton, C Ashton and J Hughes, forwards. Everton, Morris, goal ; Newcombe and Gointnersall. backs; Treanor, half-back; C Formby, J Formby and T rowers, forwards. Everton defended the deep end, and C Ashton in the rac 3 for the ball, secured possession, and the ball travelled to the Evertoa end. The brothers Asiatoa were prominent in some gold passing, and several fine shots were cent in at Morris, who displayed capital form and effected several clear- anoes. The Liverpool forwards attacked, and Robert- Ben was tried, with the result that the vis:tors had again to fall back. Exciting play followed, arid the game ruled even for a lengthened period. Both Robertson and Morris, the rival custodians, were kept busily employed, and after Ashton had experienced hard luck Hughes and Cronshaw made vain st:enspts to scare. Treanor drib- bled effectively for Everton. but all to no purpose, f Robartsoa maintained his brilliant form, and at half time there was no score. Recommencing, the Warring- tonians attacked, but as their snots were anything but well directed, Morris had little difficulty in clearing. Towers and J Formby forced the game to the Warring- ton quarters, and Robertson was warmly applauded by the spectators for his splendid play in gaal. Several shots were cleverly checked, and it was the general opinion that the Warrington goal keeper, had rarely been seen to such advantage. Near the close, Warrington, through the medium of the brothers Ashten and Hughes had a spell of attacking,but all efforts to score were un- successful, and when time was called, neither side had scored, the game thus ending in a pointless draw. OSBORNE BEAT ST. HELENS FOR THE CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>At Leicester on Monday night the above club; met to decide the final tie in the water polo championship of England. Osborne were first to attack, but nevertheless Ripley registered the first goal for St. Holens. Osborne, however, kept up the pressure, and Lister and Derby- shire put on goals in quick succession. Dlrbyshire got through again, and at the interval Osborne led toy 3 to 1. Changing ends, the play continued to rule in favour of Osborne, who, scoring again through Derbyshire and Coe, ran out easy winners by 5 goals to 1. The St. Helens team was :—J Aspinwall, goal J Bretton, and W Preston, backi ; R Sharroek, half back ; W Walker, P Ripley (captain), and J Mavity, forward.s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>-..,...• WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>-..,...• WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ALDRIMAN FRA.NCoMB ON CONSERvATIVR PURITY. The distribution of prizes in connection with the closing main of bowls took place on Satarday evening in the first class billiard room of the Con- servative Club, the ceremony being performed by Alderman Francomb. In the course of a few re- marks.he referred to the fact that Mr.Jolley had to seek re-election in the Latchford Ward. He (Alder- man Francomb) should look upon it as a calamity if he were beaten, as he devoted a large amount of time to his work. He hoped the people of Latch- ford would keep him at it. (Hear, hear.) Flaying spoken in high terms of the game of bowls, the Alderman expressed a hope that the members would do all they could to keep the club as respectable as possible. They were often told that they won their elections with the beer barrel, but there never was a greater lie uttered. He was afraid that the people who called them black were a little blacker themselves. Anyone could come to that club, and he would not hear a wrong word or see a man mis- conduct himself. That was a great deal to say,and it would grieve him if he heard of the club gping down. He was sure that so long as they kept up the good name of the club, sa long would they have the support and enc)uragement of the wives of the members. (Hear, hear.) Mr. S. Caldwell proposed a vote of thanks to Alder- 'man Francomb for his services.—This was secondel by Mr T Potter, and carried.—Alderman Franeomb in reply, said he had beaten the record that night. as he had walked all the way from Wilderspool; he did not think he could have done so two months ago. (Applause.)—On the motion of Mr. Millington, seeonded by Mr Horrobin, and supported by Mr Westwell, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Secretary.—Mr Thornton proposed a vote of thanki to the manager (Mr Watkins), and to the seised- Ibers.—Mr Rawling seconded,and Mr Watkin's reply concluded the proceedings. The prizes were as fol- lows :—Ar S Caldwell,timepiece; Mr W Wilson,time- piece; Mr T Potter,barometer ; Mr Peter Richardson electro-plated tea pot; Mr F lierfoot, glass salad bowl; Mr D Rawling, cruet; Mr W Davies, glass flower stand. Consolation prizes: Mr W Milling- ton, bowls and jack; Mr J Leigh, Gladstone bag ; Mr H Turner, glass tumblers and decanters; Mt A Vernon. wine glasses and jug; Mr J Harrison, bottle of whiskey and 21b of tea; Mr R Straw, bottle of whiskey and bottle of wine; Mr T Horrobin, bottle of wine; Mr J Thornton, pipe.</p>
      </div>
