      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RUNCORN DEFEAT TYLDESLEY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN DEFEAT TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>Monday law.; was Tyldesley wakea, and also the day upon which Runcorn were called upon to visit the town to render an account of tbe,mseives in the Lancashire Senior Competition. The game was fought under cm- ditions unfavourable t..) football, inasmuch as it rained inces3antly throughout its durat:on ; but Tyldesley sup- porters seemed to think it was jusc the day for their team to chine and the hare i of a victory were largely cptrimenttd upon. The teams faced as follows : Tyldesley :—Dickinson, hick; Alldred, 'l' E:ke_l•sley, , El Holt, and H Grindley, three-quarters ; W Berry, and Bateson, halves • Worthington, Weedon, Harberry, Tinker, C elves, igawbury, Ashton and Halliwell, for- wards.</p>
        <p>R.lncorn :—Houzhton, blck ; D Jones, J Butterworth, T. Warder and I Wilkinsu, three quarters; J. Faulkner, and J Jolley. halves; Lighttoat, S Walker, F Gavter, Tcrnlinson,El Farmer, A Taylor, Langley and 0 Hughes, forwards.</p>
        <p>When Farmer licked off there were scaroely 303 spec- tators present, and quite a deluge of rain was falling. Farmer's kick landed the ball well over the Tyldesley line, and the drop out was received by Langley who marked almcst on the spat where Farmer kicked from, Farmer took a stale at goal and only just fell short by irlCheS. Shortly after Farm ir ha I anotaer attempt boa again was unsuccessful. His kicking was however gcod. Alldrel replied and made ground for the homesters. Jolley however brought him up and starting his own men a remarkably fast return was made Faulkner finally knocking into touch. Runcorn presied and as the result of a fine inevement, Hougiatoa got a chance to drop. The ball however, went aside. Owen Hnghea was then conspicuous for a brilliant tackle fetching down Dickin- son smartly. The greasy ball caused a lot of scrambl- ing hereAbauts. Securing from Faulkner, Butterworth, and Jones made capital way. W. Berry kicked wildly and J. Warder receiving ran well eluding all opposition and sLeored near the corner. As Warder fell rather awkwardly Tyllesloy appealed ; but the try which was really a floe effert. was awarded. Farmer failed to convert. Tyldesley were off-side from tee ki-le and Runcorn were again soon press- ing. Jones was secured by Batesoa when dangerous. Tyldealey forwards rushed well, but Jolley, Butterworth and floaghton effected most marvellous catches, and seldom if ever played a safer game. Securing from a scrum Faulkner initiated a passing movement between himself Butterworth and Jones wpich fairly amazed the home hacks, and Butterworth put the finishing touches on a glorious'piece of work by scoring try iiumbn* two. The kick again failed. The game then developed into one of a mare even nature and up to half time Runcorn simply acted on the defensive, though all the time Tyllesley were wondering what was to happen next. At half time the score was—ituncorn 2 trios, Tyllosley nil.</p>
        <p>Wool ward opened the game for Tyldesley without an interval, and Dickinson improved things by making good we for his side. Runcorn repiied with vigour and Allred save I an almost certain try by grasiing Jones, tnoug,h the latter had pluckily accounted for two of his opponents. Tyldesley then rushed and the venue was transfered. Run earn were penalised and Alldred hazarding a drop ltaded a magnidient goal. Warder gained useful ground by judicious kicking and the Tyldesley backs being unable to hold the oval, Runcorn forwards gave them a lively time of it. Dickinson saved his charge time after time and also made most of the play for Tcldealey. Someone took a liking to Jolley's shirt, but could not get him to stop for it and having his first drop during the game he sent the ball right over. After this Raucorn attacked with effect and Dickinson was called upon to touch-d ma and otherwise save his charge. Several scrums followed in Tyldeslay quarter. Faulkner secured from one of tame and feeding his man judici- ously the ball came to Butterworth. Ho coolv tucked it under his arm and showing his heels to his opponents sailed in and scored where he liked. Needless to say Farmer easily couverted. Taking things easily Run- corn contentedly abided the call of time and though Tylilesley made several ;arty rushes in determined style, the Linnets were victors after a spirited game by 2 goals 3 tries (13 points), to Tyldes'ey 1 goal (2 points).</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. BROUGEHON RINGERS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Saturday, Cna,pman was again superse- ded by Nelson at centre three-quarters for Broughton, whilst for Widnes Batey was unable to play through AR injury, Brennan occupying his old p 3 tine at full back. Broughton won the toss, and Aspey kicked off for Widnes again:,t a vary strong wind, which carried the ball into touch. Continuing, Jackson got away, but was sniartly tackled by Bown. From the scrummags James gained po;session, but Murphy defeated the movement. Kelly, of Widnes, then received an injury to his leg, and had to OA carrion off the fled. Sunderland, Barratt, and Nelson got away, but James Hughes tackled the latter s:naray. Rispan passed to Hughes orho raced away, W. James grassing him on the line, and Messenger relieving with a log punt. Wolfendon was ordered off th) field. From the scrummage Rivan again passed nut to Einghes, who took tno pass well, but James efft-sted a brilliant tackle on the line, saving an apparently Ler- Lain try. Half-late arrived without any scoring. Broughton restarted, and the Widnes forwards at once rushed to the Rangers' line, where Murphy, obtaining possession, trickei neatly, and passed to Brown, who was almost over, Sunderland saving. Di James came away, but found his match in Rispan. Broughton were penalised, Murphy's attempt at goal sending the ball wide. Continuing,S JaCkson'eame away, Bovril grassing him smartly on the touch-line. Then Hughes was tackled just OR the lino by W. James. Douglaa(Widaes) retired, but with only thirteen men Widnes made a dash- for the line, and Donnelly passed to Aspey,who at lenrh secured a try at the corner. Aspey's attempt at coal failed. Later. Aspay dritMed over,and Donnelly adde 1 a try for Widn-s, the place kick again failing. A moment la%.er Bo wn dashed over the visitors' goal line, and Aspey placed a goal. Jackson ran strongly, but Widnes returned, and pressed to the finish. Final score : —Widnes, one goal and three tries (11 points); Brough- toa Rangers, nil. MILLONI V. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>The 'Moaners made their initial appearance at Miflora since th 3 inception ot the Northern Union, before 1,000 spectators on Saturday. Both sues were well repre- sented. Wigaa won the toss and play&amp; with the wind. Bucketlt started. Millom cluld make little headway against the strong breeze. Eventually Owen picked up smartly and crossed the line, Lewis kicking a goal. Buckett resum3d,and after a pack in the Wigan quarter, 0 area and Ball dribble.l away, the latter almost scoring, a minor resulting. Wigan played up smartly, but Eagers, time after time, broke away, and completely changed the venue. Half,tima : Wigan 1 goal 1 try, Mill= nil. Millom eventually won by 9 points t 3 5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TYLDESLEY v. SILFORD.</head>
        <p>TYLDESLEY v. SILFORD.</p>
        <p>There was the usual Wakes crowd on the Tyldesley enclosure to witness this fixture, which was decided in stormy weather on Saturday. Toe visitors humght a representative team. while Tvldesley had their best available side, which included H. Holt. of Heckmond- wilre. The back division was further strengthened by the inchvion of Johnson at half back, who turned out for the first time this season. The opening stages were in favour of the visitors, who played the rivaling game to advantage. From a pass by Thomas, T. Williams ran finely to the hltne goal line, and re•passei to Thomas, who scored a fine try behind t'ae posts, Griffiths placing a goal. From the restart W. Berry dribbled dawn the flair]. and Worthington scored a brilliant try for Tyldes- ley, Attired also placing a goal. With the scores equal, b)tn sides played up finely, and after a forward rush Shaw added a try for the visitors,Griffith kicking another goal. Each end was visited in turn, and An- drei, though hard pressed, kicked a brilliant goal. Half- time arrived with the score :—Salford, two pals and two tries; Tvldesley, two goals and one try. Woad- ward reatartel, and the home forwards at once bore down to the Salford line, where a series of exciting scrummages took place, the ball eventually being kicked &amp;ear. Holt then made a splendid run and kick when his side was in difficulties, in return,passing by Williams Grey, and Thomas resulted in Pearson scoring perhaps the bet try of the match, Griffith again c Inverting. Immediately the game was renewed Pearson again forced his way over the home goal line, Griffith failing at goal. Pearson again succeeded in scoring. Griffith placing his fourth mat. Final score :—Salford, four goals and five tries (23 Dints) ; Tyldesley, two goals and one try (seven points.),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>STOCKPORT v. SWINTON.</head>
        <p>STOCKPORT v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>At St.inkport, on Saturday, in boisterous weather, be- fore 4,000 spectators. St ckport had the advantage of a stroog breczo in the firet half. James Valentine scored an unconverted try for the visitors, and Saville kicked a penalty goal for Stockport. In the second portion Hammon and Messer scored unconverted tries, and Goodman dropped a goal for Swinton. Worsley scored an unconverted try for S'oekport. Result--Swinton, 11 points ; Stockport, 5 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>STOCKPORT v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>STOCKPORT v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>On Monday, st Stockport, in wet weather hefote 3,000 people. Stockport were weak. In the first half Stockport had most of the pray, but could not rcore,</p>
        <p>wbile Lees and Williams registered trial for Oldham. To the second half the game was fairly even, though Stock- port played thirteen men. Oldham were not in good form, and the football was p,or. Result : OldhArn, 2 tries (6 points). StoApart nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION, LANCSUIRE SENIOR COAIPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION, LANCSUIRE SENIOR COAIPETITION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON LOSE AT ROCPIDALE.</p>
        <p>The first match this season between Warrington and Roelidale took place at Rochdale on Saturdev before 4,000 spectators. The weather was bad, rain falling heevily and frequently, and the ground was in a shock- ingly soft state. Warringtoa took their selected fifteen away and expected to secure a victory, but they were disappointed, for the Hornets won the game in the last five minutes. House was absent from the home side and a local youth named Turner filled the vecaecy on the wing. Mr. S. Williams, of Salforii, was the referee, and the players in his charge were as follow : Warrington : Moores, full ba,ek ; Wood, Isherwool, Joles, and Merryweather, three.quarters ; Bate and Daek worth, half backs ; Taylor, Naylor. Bo trdmae. Seholtz Eden, Fairhurst. Sudloe , and Swift, forwards. Rochdale Hernets ; W. Rize„ full back ; Turner, Davies, Field, ane Bo othroyd, three-quarters ; Kruger and Alien, half backs ; Chadwick, Harrison, Haaeliker, Hodekinson, Schofield, Simpson, Wilson, and Wood, three-qnarters. Bites' usual good luck remained with him and he won the toss. The choice of positions was of some import- ance, for the wiad blew strongly down the field, and Werringt in were glad to take advantage of it. Field started for the Hornets, and Isherwood returned. Eechenge kicking in which Meores figured creditably. followed, and Warrington reached the home 25 use. Carrying the scrummages they reached the Hornets line where Bete, Duels worth and Wood passed neatly only for Jones to spoil the movement in front of goal by furubline. Kruger relieved grandly by a good run and punt to centre, but Warrington soon returned, Rigg and Davies both receiving injuries which classed a tempor- ary stoppage. A spell of even play followed, Warrington showing slackness in combination. A rush by the Hornets was well-checked by Bat, and then Duckworth broke away cleverly making a lovely opening for Jones, but the Welshman missed the simplest of passes with a clear course before him, Warrington again fell off, and Kruger seapeing .a pass handed to Boathroyd, who raced in from the quarter-flag. Rigg landed a fine goal. Play livened up at this, Bate shining with good work which only laeked support to have resulted in a score. Warrington attacked, and again the three- quarters boat the Hornets, Jonas having another oppor- tunity which he wasted through selfishness. Wood was waiting for the pass and could easily have scored, but Jones held too long and was tackled from behind. War- riagton had now missed no less than three clear chances to scare, and the interval approached. Rigg and Field kicked splendiJle for the homesters and managed to keep Warrington at bay. Smart play by Duskworth, who tackled Rigg, resulred in Warrington getting Close up. Davies kicked feebly and Wood marked, Jones atoning somewhat for his past failures by kicking a goal. A sudden movement by Kruger and Field nearly broke through the Warrington defence, Isherwood brineing the centre down near the line in fine style. Warriag- ton had a warm time of it until just before the interval when Bate cleared and kicked across the field, the ?Far- rington forwards having hard lines in not defeating Rigg. Half-time, Hornets, 1 goal 1 try, Warrington. 1 goal Jones restarted and some lofty punting took place, Duckworth being tackled in Warrington territory. The Hornets had the best of the opening stages, Kruger nearly drooping a goal, but Warrington at last roused up. The forwards played a great game and won nearly every scrummage, the Hornets' line beings', reached in quick time. Jones centred and the ball diopped right over the rut in front of the Warrington eight. A score seemed certain but Rigg jumped up at the dropping ball and broaght off a marvellous if lucky save. Field effected temporary relief with a dodgy run to centre,bnt Warrington woiald not be denied. Rigg saved again when al seemed lost and Warrington got desperate. Clever marks and kicking by the Hornets kept the line clear for a time, the Warrington forwards breaking through with a spanking dribble half the length of the field. Isherwood picked up and threw acrozs to Merryweather who dashed in at the corner only to be knocked over the flag by Rigg. Kruger changed the venue temporarily and Moores was tackled. Wood tried a drop which nearly came off and from mid- field the Warrington backs made a nice run to the line. Isherwood parted to Jones and the latter equalised with a well-earned try. Jones failed to raise the ball when kicking for goal. It now seemed all odds on a draw, but the Hornets rallied and got near the Warrington goal. Tl'n scrimmage was carried by Warrington and danger seemed averted when Bate made a sad blunder. He attempted to kick clear, but instead sent the ball into the hands of the watchful Rigg, who steadied him- self and dropped a leveler goal amid great cheering. Time was almost up and Warrington looked disheartened. They made one spasmodic, hepeless effort, and then the whistle !Divided with the oseore reading, Hornets 2 goals, 1 fry (7 pints) ; Warriogton 1 goal, 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RUNCORN v. OLDHA.M. FINE PERFORNIANCE BY THE LINNE I'S.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. OLDHA.M. FINE PERFORNIANCE BY THE LINNE I'S.</p>
        <p>At Watersheddings. Especial interest was addel to the engagement by the fact that neither had sufferei defeat in a League match, whi'st the visitors had de- feated Wigan, Swinton.Broughton Rangers, and Widnes. There was a great deal of rain and tharader not long before play started, but the attend- ance numbered fully 15.000 when the teams, both of which were strong, lined up. Old- ham : R Thomas, full back ; T Davies, T Fletcher, S Lees, and S Williams, three-quarter backs ; A Lees (captain) aad JEt Lawtrsn, half necks; E B maser. G Frater, Ferguson, E W Telfer, H Ellis, J Lees, E W Porter, and W Barnes.forwards. Runcora : S Houghton (captain), full bank; D Jones, J Butterworth,T Warder, and T Wilkinson, three-quarter backs ; J Faulkner anti J Jolley, half backs ; S Ws.lker, H Farmer, F Gayter, W Lightfoot, A. Taylor, 0 Hughes, and ,F Darlington, for- wards. Oldham won the toss, and with it the aid of a strong wind, but the "Linnets "were the first aggressors, and early on, Williams having knocked on in a good passing bout, Frater was called on to clear hi 3 lines, Oldham then commenced an onslaught, Fletcher punting well to place them in scoring position. The same player gave ' Davies a pass tram which ho almost scored, Jones and Butterworth together keeping him at bay. A brief hail- storm, to the backs of the Oldhamers, made Runcorn's position still less enviable, but they exhibited splendid powers of defence. Houghton was tackled in psssession, and from the scrummage which ensued Williams completed a round of passes by galloping over the line. The Runcsrn international repeatedly cleared with long kicks from touch, and following one of these Wilkinson snapped up an Oldham pass and gained some ground. The attack was, however, re- peated with renewed vigour, and scrummage after scrimmage was contestad on the verge of the line. Jolley, who was well to the fore for the visitors, was hauhd down uear the posts, and Oldham had several openings which they failed to utilise, Arthur Lees once being Wily at fault by allowing the ball to be carried off by the Runcorn forwards. Sam Lees and Williams tan play back, hut again faulty play gave th. Cestrians a chance to repulse. S. Lees,always in the thick of the work, dribbled up once more, and Thomas with a nicely judged kick dropped the ball over the line, but Houghton got the best of a. race for possession, a dead ball re-u'ting, to the chagrin of Davies.who was coming up in great strides. Au even better opening next pro- spored itself. for Fletcher followed up Lees'a kick and caught the ball over the line, only, however, to lose it again and see it kicked dead against the rails. These prrsistont failures were not relished by the crowd, who murmured loudly when Houghton conceded another dad ball ti a low punt by Thomas; but shortly before the interval a slight reward was given to the Oldhanaers in the shape of a penalty kick, from which S Lees scored. lot rval score r—Oldham, one goal (two points) Runcorn, nil. The game was new slightly in favour of the visitors, Oldham having to fame the wind, but the restart saw</p>
      </div>
