      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>FREIDA MABEL CLARK&amp;</head>
        <p>FREIDA MABEL CLARK&amp;</p>
        <p>Why children should gain a pound a day on an oance a day of Scott's Emulsion has never b-en explained. Nor would an explanation of it make mush difference any way. It is true, however, that this has been ac- complished, and certain it is that Scott's Emulsion meets the requirements of the growing body better than any remedy which has yet been offered to the public. Here is a letter that may intere,t you:— "64, Cromwell Road, Wimbledon, S. W., "February sth, 1899.</p>
        <p>"Gentlemen,—l have much pleasure in testifying to the wonderful banefit derived by our little dau4hter Freida, by the use of Scott's Emulsion. She was an apparently healthy child when born, but having to feed her by hand we had great trouble in obtaining a food or milk to suit her. At four months old 61-1.1 contracted whooping cough with bronchial trouble, which rapidly developed to congestion of the brain. The doctor we called in said it was partly due to teething, and for several days he gave us not the slightest hope of saving her. She wasted away to a mere skeleton, and her pillow was wet through by the copious cold sweats she had when asleep. She rec3vered from the congestion. etc., then diarrhce I and sickness set in, and so on for another four mouths she was one week batter and an- other week worse. At eight months she weighed only eleven pounds. We read ono day of a similar case to ours, and We determined to try your Scott's Emulsi,an. We commenced with a half-teaspoonful twice a day. giving her sterilized milk. Before she had taken the</p>
        <p>(From a photograph.)</p>
