      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>LEEDS PARISE' CFIIIIICFI v. WAKEFIELD TAINITY. At Leeds, before 5,000 spectators. In the first half Smith scored fa: the home aide, M-sley failing at goal. In the sPcond half Fawcett scored a try, but failed at goal. Crompton also s lured, and Mosley kicked a goad. Metcalfe dropped a goal for IVak-,,fi11 from a penalty kick. Reitilt :—Ptrith Church, 1 goal 3 tries (11 points) ; Wakefield, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>HUDDERSFIELD v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>At Fartown in wild weather, before a lair cathering.. In the firAt half Halifax scrod only two minors, though they had the wind in their favour. Iti.cv.; and IVlal!insoa erossel twice, but the ball wt a btek. In the second half wither ored. fluddirstleld had a lot of minors. 11,33u1t :—HuiciErsfiel.l, uil; Efaliftx, nil.</p>
        <p>MANNINGRAU v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>At Valley Parid,t, befo .3 3,000 spocticon. Lockhart, aftsr tninute's play, scored a try for Manningha,tn again3t. the wind. Barke: and Lt.ach aided others. and Rogan dropped a goal for Leeds. On the restart Rogan kicked a penalty g ta'. After this, play was tame, both ends being visited in turn. itttnningh tm, towards the close, played up, bat could not score. R ham, 3 tries (9 points) ; Leedl, 2 goals (4 points.)</p>
        <p>110LBECK v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>Elolback, before 4,003 Ppect‘tors. The home team had a strong wind behind them in the first half, and M'Carthy semed a try, IT-matey kicking a goal. Inathe second half Bra lfard played up well, and tries were scored by Frank Murgdtroyd, Pollard (twice), and Prole. Patrick kicked two fine goals, and Bradford thus word Resu't—Bradford, 2 goals 4 tried (16 pinta) Holbeck. 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HULL KINC:S CON ROVERS v. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>HULL KINC:S CON ROVERS v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>At Hull, beforel7,ooo spectators. Three minutes from the start Batley scored from a mark, Tußock equalising five minutes later wit'i a splendid kick from half—vay. Kemp scored a penalty go!, and jut on haUf-tim3 Tul- lock dripped anther. In th 3 second half Davies ran in a try from a pass, and i coal was kicked. B ttley pressed hard most of the game, bit just on time the Rovers scored a try and a goal. Result—Rovers, 1.1 points ; Batley, 7. LIVERSEDgE v. BR XVLEY.</p>
        <p>At Llversedge. In the first half Bramley had slightly th best of matters, and Parker scored a try, but the same player failed at goal. On the resumption Liver- sedge attacked stronglr, but were some timg before they conld break throuth, Crowther ultimately snoring a try and Todhill kickmg a goal. Tadhill afterwards Fcerel, Midc,rlev kicking a fine goal. Reault—Liversedge, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points); Bransley. 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>BRIGHOUSE WkNGERS v. 11ULL.</head>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE WkNGERS v. 11ULL.</p>
        <p>At Bri;house, befere 2,000 spec:ators. Both clubs were well representnd. Brighouse plaved with thm wind in the first half, Abbe 7 landinl a penalty goal and Jlnes getting a try. In the second half D)rinan and Tanner slored tri,s, Jaques la.nding two coals. Result—Hull, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) ; Brighouse, 1 goal 1 try (5 points). CASTLEFORD v. FIUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Caitleford. The viwora had the advantage of tremendous wind in the first half, but Caatleford had by far the I”at of play. Salmoni and Neil scored, and T,)wriend converted one, and also kicked a goal from a mark. Webster (a Ca,tlPfor4 forward) WAR injured,and retired. Having a lead of 10 points, Caetleford had the wind in their favour in the second Inlf A. Goldthorpe dropped a goal. Result—Ca.stleforcl, 2 goals 5 tries (19 points) ; Hunslet, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LANCASHIRE "A " rEam COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE "A " rEam COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>WAR—R—INOTON " A " TEAM V. ROCHDALE HORNETS " A." This match took place on Saturday last, on the Wil- derEpool ground. rho visitors brought a strong team. There were not many spectators at the time of the kick off. The visitors playing' with the wind in the first half made things very lively for the home team but thNy were unable to score. Warrington working their way up the fold corei a try Ircm Nthich Mason placed a goal. This was all the score up to half time. On chanc- ing ends it was generally expocted the home team would give a hotter show but tls,s was not the case, as pcor football was shown during the match. The home half hacks did not pass the ball with any jqdgment, with the result that the threr quarters had to make all their o Nem play. The game ended : Warrington 2 goals 2. r ies, Rochdale Hornets nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH. On Sunday afternoon Mr. T. E. Gibson continued 1°,14 remarks at the mrn's service held in St. Peter's Cburv.: taking for his subject " The entrance to the Kingdate there was a good attendance of the memb?rs of Bible Class. Mr. Peter GrEen sang two solos in a creJW aNe manner•</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. " The Supreme Quest ; or, putting that first which first "wag the subj,ct of a discourse to young men Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening 1/7, the Rev. John Yonge. He based his remarks on eb,' text " Seek ye first the kingdom of God, and His riirtli; eousnes ; and all the,..e ti,ings shall he added unto Matthew vi. c., 33 v. He said it was of vital imP°r', ance that every man should clearly understand what 10; the first duty and the supreme aim of life in this wctt. He had no new revelation to unfold concerning the supreme quest. There seemed to be greater need ~ the discussion and study of things which were etero and divine because the tendency of human activity peared to be towards the magnification of the temPors:,; They saw the vast majority of p=ople seeking for things which the great teacher spoke of under the ras3" of mammon. E7erywhele they saw mammon seeker' mammon worshippers and sometimes mammon slaVe°,, in a va7iety of forms but the same things. If they were f,y, read men by their deeds they were compelled to-conehl that only very few recognised that the Kingdom of Ci°f/ and His rlghteouBoe3s was the supreme quest of life. ;41 the case of many every hour which was not dernanoe„ by work and business was given up to one form cl; another of self-indulgence. Might he be able to suggelo". to young men—and especially to youne. Christian !Ilea —who were eneaged in cricket., football, or cycling Saturdays until they were so physically tired as to v unfit for worship and Christian work on Sunds whether they were not making that to ,5 first in their habit of life which ought o`r to be first, and when, moreover, they go mitted Sunday hours to be given to cycling 0111;0, they had been prone to consecrate to better purPos;re He knew it was pleaded by some whose minds 144 c uneasy when they thus secularised Sundavr out in the fresh air and amid the scenes of nature theif could worship God as well as in the church. Yes tbigef might ; but did they And even if they did were as members of Sunday schools and Christian corruouce, ties presenting a good example,exerting a good infiLitir or were they encouraging others to break otd tier Sabbath observances ? The preacher proceeded to i`pf to the many difficulties which had to be encountered tho,e who endeavoured to lead a religious life itei• nreent day. They were living in unhealthy surrou'vet logs for a religious life. The world never was and tleej would be in sympathy with the Godly life. Efe urb•Gre them to persevere, however, and for every Ws they would get a compensation and for every they would receive a reward.—The choir very effi/e" sang the anthem "How manifold are Thy works;</p>
