      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899.</p>
        <p>HORRIBLE DISCOVERY IN THE MERSEY AT WARRING [ON.</p>
        <p>Shortly before seven a in. on Wednesday last week, sergeant Evans and Police-constables Hurst and Heap received information that a body was in the river in the new divieion of the river Mersey at Warrington, near Walton Arches. On going there they saw a horrible sight. The body. which was that of a man, was suspended about eight feet above the water on a wire hawser. With great difficulty it was taken into a boat by the officers, and then conveyed to the Lewer Seven Stars, Bridge-street. Ihe body was tnat of a man Sit. Oin. in height, and wes minus head and entrails It was very greatly decomposedsand was almost nude, the only clothing being a pair of black stockings and elastic-side boots, and a portion of a black colt and cloth trousers hanging from the breast. Later in the day the body was identified as that of William Brosdy, aged seventy-four years, shoemaker, formerly residing at 61, Ellesmere-street, Warring- ton, who has been missing since March, 1893. Mr. Jones, deputy coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Police Station on Friday afternoon on tee body. Mr. Ashton was foreman of the jury. The first witness called was Mrs. Brimelow, 1, Gibson-street, who stated that the deceased was William Broady,her stepfather,aud he formerly lived 1 at 61, alllesmere-street. He was a shoemaker and was 74 years of age. She last saw him alive at 11 o'clock on Suaday morning, liarch 27th, 1893. He was then at the house of a relative in Chailoner-ste He had become very infirm owing to his advanced age. He lived by himself but she had never heard that he had threatened to take away his life. she did not think he slept in his house on the Sunday night. On Wednesday she saw the body and ideu- third it as that of her stepfather. She could not identify him by the body but she remembered bind- ing his coat and the coat on the body recovered contained binding like that which she did for hit. He asle wore elastic side boots which he had made himself. Chief Inspector Phillips said the measuremen of the body corresponded with the height of th deceased man. Emily Box, 66, Egerton-street stated that she reniembered seeing the deceased on March 27th, 189 S, at 20 minutes to 10 p.m. as she was going along Church-street. She bade him good night. He then seemed all right and quite sober. Chief Inspector Phillips said he knew the deceased and last saw him alive at 9,20 a.m. on the 29 March 1898. He was then at the corner of Irlam- street and Whittle-street. He was alone and sober. ' Isaac Bostock, in the employ of the Manchester I Ship Canal Company as watch man,said that on Wed- nesday morning he was at Arpley at 5.30 when he saw the deceased suspended on a wire hawser which stretched across the river. The deceased was hang- ing across the wire by his chest, about seven feet above the water. He thought the tide mint have carried the body on the wire and left it there when it went down. It was in the tidal portion of the I river. He reported what he had seen to the police. Sergeant Evans said he received information from the last witness and went to the place indicated in company with P C's Hurst and Hasp about 7.10 a m. He found the body. He procured a boat and got the body into it. The clothing consisted of a pair of elastic-side boots, black stockings and a portion of a pair of trousers and a coat. The body was sus- ' pended from underneath the arms. It was taken to the Seven Stars Inn to await identification. It had been in the water a long time and was greatly decomposed. . The Coroner : You had an unpleasant task. Sergeant Evans : It was one of the worst I have over had. The Coroner hoped the Chief Constable would hear of it. In such cases the efficers deserved a special holiday for doing such work. The Foreman of the Jury said the jurymen would be only too pleased to support any recommenda- tion that might be made. IChief Inspector Phillips said he would report the matter to the Chief Constable. The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned." .....--4.-.</p>
        <p>ANOTILER TREA.T AT CUERDEN HALL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday last week, the final contingent of the members of the Warrington Borough Police Force, and a number of railwaymen in the employ of the London and North-western Railway Company at Bank Quay Station, visited Cuerden Bail, Thelwall, at the invitation of Mr R A and Miss Naylor. The wives and lady friends of the men accompanied them, and as the weather was delightful, the outing was a complete success. The party was conveyed in 'busses and on their arrival was wel- come' by Mr. and Miss Naylor. A minute inspection of the fine picture gallery and other objects of interest was made by the visitors, after which they gathered in the grounds, and a photograph of the police group was taken by Detective Roper. Various games were in- dulged in, and refreshmentq served on the lawn. An excellent tea was provided in the Temperance Hall at six o'clock, after which Mr. JAMBI WHITTAKER, on behalf of the railway men, expressed his appreciation of the kiodnass of Mr. and Miss Naylor in inviting them ta Cuerden Hall. They bad spent a very pleasant afternoon, and he hoped the good feeling which existed would continue. (Applause.) Police Constable ROBINSON, on behalf of the police force, also spoke in eulogistic terms of the kindness of Mr. and Miss Nayler. Mr. R. A. NAYLOR, in reply, said that it was really a great pleasure to his sister and himself to see such a happy gathering that afternoon, and to have their assurance that they had enjoyed themselves. The ex- pression of their appreciation of the little hospitality they bad been enabled to give them was quite sufficient thanks to them. The rail- way organisation of this country represented a very important interest, and he had hoped to have one of their great magnates present on that °maiden, but owing to illness he had been unable to attend. He hoped that would not be the last time they would meet under such !pleasant circumstances. The spolice force was a very important element in their everyday life. A copy of the " Police Review" bad been sent to him by one of the Warrington inspectors, in which there was a very interesting poem. In a jocular style the various—and he might say multifarious— duties of " Poor Robert" were deslribed. Man was placed in the world to do his duty, whether he was a business man, a railway man, or a policeman. A soldier went on the field of battle and performed valiant deeds. and on his return was decorated with the coveted honour of the Victoria Cross. He thought some little inducement might be held out to a policeman or a railwayman by her Majesty in recogni- tion of any brave deed. Ths dangers to which police officers were subjected to in the Metropolis were very numerous, and deeds as brave as those of soldiers on the field of battle had frequently been witnessed. It Wes thought by many that some recognition should be made of the brave deeds of both railwaymen and police- men. He thanked them for their kind vote of thanks. (Applause.) Miss NAYLOR also b!iefly replied. The National Anthem was then heattily sung,and sub- sequently a concert was given in the Temperance Hall.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEA.LTII OF ViA.RRINGTON. Week Ending Saturday, September 16th, 1899. During the week the following births and deaths were registezed Births. Deaths. Warrington Registration District 140 25 Tne excess of births deaths over was 15. The Birth Rate was equal to 32 2 per 1,000. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 20.3 per 1,000, against 34.9 the average of the previeus four weeks, and against 22.3 the corresponding period of last year. The death - rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 56 per:l,ooo,against 21.4 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against 9.1 the corresponding period of 1898. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 8 5 1 2 7 2 25 The deaths included-7 from diarrhcea, 1 from phthisis. 3 from lung dlieA9o9. 3 from heart disease, 1 from injuries, 10 from all other causes. The following CASO9 of Infectious Disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 22 cases, enteric fever 15 cases. Total number of patientq in hospital week ending Sat- urday 16th September 1899, 56. METEOROLOGY—The mean tempnr Iture was 55.7 Fah. and thereforel3 degrees lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 68 F., was recorded on the 13th inst., and the minimum temperature 40 F., on the 15th inst. The Rainfall measured *6B inch. The general dirsc- tion Wa3 JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>OHN WALKER AND He. an established reputation for giving careful and Prompt Attention to the wants of all Customers ; and its facilities for the production of Good work of every kind—frem the smallest typographic trifle to the biggest mural " Mammoth "—are available to the public, at pricei "Which are strictly inederate. ' Care- less, inartistic, and slovenly printing is the bane of busin,ss men. They find it doesn't pay to have It— even at " the lowest tender."</p>
