      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATII.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATII.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening last week,a meeting of the Stock ton Heaib Parochial Cum mitten was held in the National Schoolroum S.;ock:on Heath. Mr. Mason presided, and there were also present Messrs. J Whitley, J Pem- berton, F Pearson, J Clarke, with U Ashton (clerk), J Swinton (surveyor) and J Farrington (nuisAnce in- spector.)</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW BYELAWS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a communication from the Local Government Board dated the 2ud September relative to the byelaw proposed by the Rural District Council of Runcorn, to be made with respect to water closets, earth closets, ashpits, and ceEspoAs in the codtributory place of Stockton Heath. :the Board returned the pro- posed byelaws and requested that the alterations they had made should receive the ccnsideration of the authority. Any observations they might desire to make could be conveniently inserted in toe rdinary draft when returned. Atter oiscussion, it was decided that the Clerk should forward certain observations on some of the suggestions of the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>THE LAYING OF SEWERS.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote enclosing the following letter they had received from Messrs. Spencer Wallhead and Son, solicitors, Warrington ;—" Sir,— Our client, Mr John Pembrrton is the lessor of land in Warburton-itreet, Stockton Heath, Cheshire, to Mr. Cornelius Young, who has erected thereon six houses. Mr. Young is prepared to carry the sewer for the houses 100 feet down the road as required by the bye-laws, but the Rural District Council (although advised by their clerk that they are liable to do so) will not take the sewer up to the 100 feet limit. The consequence is that Mt. Young's houses must remain untenanted as he cannot drain into a cesspool because he has no land on which to make a cesspool ; besides cesspools are at all times very oujectionable, and in this case there is house property all round. We are informed that the cost of carrying the sewer up to Mr. Young's 100 feet limit will be very small, and probably the rural authority will have the work done on hearing from your board. Our client does not think that any authority should he allowed to inflict serious loss on property owners and endanger the health of residents in the locality." The Clerk said ttier2 was one statement in the letter ; that referring to himself which was not corrrct. He did not think that should have appeared. Mr. Clarke : Mr. Pemberton must have instructed his solicitor to say that. The Clerk said he had gone so far as to instruct the Council that they could not compel the propertyowner to go beyond 100 feet under the powers they now had, but that was a differzrat thing to the suggestion in the letter he had just read. Mr: Clarke said perhaps Mr. Pemberton might con- siderer himself an aggrieved person because he could not let other land. But the committee was dealing with Mr. Young, and now that they had settled the point with him the letter was answered. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Pemberton said that in consequence of the com- mittee's action with respect to the sewer of Mr. Young's houses he had lost a customer for a lease of land. He was interested in the land on which Mr. Young's houses stood. He gave instructions for the letter to be written before the settlement of the sewer diffimlty with Mr. Young. He was sorry for the mistake which had occurred in the letter respecting the Clerk. The Clerk was instructed to reply that the matter had been dealt with. THE SHIP CANAL COMPANY AND FLUSHING WATER. The Clerk read the following letter hem Mr. J. Old. field of the Bridgewator Canal Company. "Dear sir,— Your letter of July 13 was pissed on to this office by Mr. Meadowcroft, but consideration has been delayed for ' inquiry as to the probable effect upon the navigation by the withdrawal of water from the canal for flushing sewers. The question is one of serious moment for the Company as the matter of water supply is a costly thing for navigation purposes and the Company instruct me to say alit they regret to find themselves unable to grant the we of canal water for flushing sewers. They have been compelled within a recent period to make provision for an increised supply at considerable cost from certain collieries and in other directions, and yet during the last three months we have been so short of water that the navigation of boats on the Canal has been restricted by six inches or more. It has not been possible to spare any water at all during that period, and at ad times we requir2 all the water sending down to Runcorn locks to reduce the cost of pumping." wait) MUST PAY FOR SEWERS?</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson moved a resolution that the Committee apply for powers under section 150 of the Public Health Act, dealing with sewers. He pointed out that the power which was to be obtained by this section showed that it was the intention of the Ant that the construction cf sewers should be at the cost of the landowners, and not of the ratepayers. They were practically an urban dis- trict, but they were without the powers compelling landowners to pay for sewers. Mr. Whitley seconded. Mr. Pemberton pointed out that the section alluded to referred to street making and the sewering in connec- tion, with such streets. It did not refer to main roads. If they obtained the powers they could not compel acy- boay to make sewers independent of street making. Mr. Clarke said they could discuss what was the meaning of it when they got the powers. It was clear in his naind what was meant.</p>
        <p>The Chairman thought the members ought to make up their minds as to the meaning of the section before they applied for it. (Hear, hear.) He was satisfied that' the section gave them power to compel th 3 owner to put in a main sewer for the street. Mr. Pearson said he teak it that they could compel the property owner and not the ratepayers to construct a sewer. He would ask the Clerk wnether that was so. _</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that was his opinion. Mr. Whitey contended that sewers had already been put in by property owners in the district. Mr.Pemberton said be only remembered two instances. If everybody put in their own sewers it would be quite fair. But up to the present time the Council had con- structed most of the sewers out of the ratepayers' pockets. Then again,the system of everyone putting in sewers would be unworkable. They would put down a sewer of sufficient capacity to carry off their own sew- age, but in later years others might be connected with it and it would then he found inadequate for all purposes. It would be absurd for the Council to say that they could compel the owner to take it up and put down a larger one for some one else's convenience. They had the experience of largo authorities who had tried it, and it was invariably the rule for authorities to construct the main sewers. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
