      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL EVENING.</p>
        <p>There was a snc;,.l gaznerirg in the L3cture Hall of the Wesl3yan Criapel, Earle,town, on Tuesday evening in aid of tho poor members of the congre- gation. The Rev. P. R. Pa4suora preside 1. and songs were given by AIS3 0. Glare, Miss L. Barr,a,nd Mr. Stanley. Mr. Francis recited " Women of Mumble's Head." there were about 70 p3ople present, and during an interval in the recreations, light refresoinaent wag handed round. A. sale was afterwards held of the fruit, &amp;I , which did duty at the decorarions for the harvest festival, the pro- ceeds being devoted to the r6llef of the poor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>BIBLICAL LECTURE AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>BIBLICAL LECTURE AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>At the -6-01-ig-re-gational School room on Monday evening, in connection with the Wigan Sunday School Ulion, a Bibli,•al 1-c.ure was given by the Secretary of ~he Union. Owing to the rough slate of the weather, there was ovily a very poor audi- ence, but the lecture wal thoroughly interesting. Several members of the Newton body had been im- pressed into service, and attired in the costumes said to exist in bible periods, they served to illu- strate the points in the lecture. The Rev. James Allatt presided, and a collection was made at the close in aid of the funds of the Union.</p>
        <p>"C" TROOP, LANCASHIRE Huss ins, NEWTON. The annual (linnet of the "C" troop of Lanca- shire Hussars was held at the Pied Bull vfotel,New- ton on Saturday evening. Besides toe members of the troop, a number of the tenant farmers, who supply horses to the troopers,were present,between 70 and 80 altogether sitting down to an excellently served dinner. After the cloth had bean removed the subs-quent pro .eedings were presided over by Mr.J. E. Parley, who was suppored by the Rev. James Ryder Me-sra. G. Clarke R. Barton, F. Frost' Sergeant-Nlsjor Strange, evict Sergeant-Nlajor Pearce of the First Life Guard s.who will suceed Sergeant- Major Strange on his retirement at the end of the present month. The usual loyal and patriotic toasts were proposed and duly honoured, and after the regimental toasts had beau similarly served, the new Sergeant- Nlajor waP, introduced to the troop by Sergeant-Nlajor 'grange, hi 4 health being drunk on the proposal of Mr. Parday. The harmony of the evening was contrihu'ed by Sergeant Swan, ex- Sergeant Farrier, T. Rirchie, Sergeant Turner, Sergt. Farrier Ritchie, jun., Troop r gale and Nfr.Watkin.</p>
        <p>Horn OF EARLESTOWN LODr4B GOOD TRAIPLARS. The weeklsr meeting was held in ale Co-opera- tive Hall, Wargrave-road on Monday evening. Bro. Francis, CT. presiding. After the opening services one person was tar Tose i for membership and one was restored. Bro F. W Hall, P. D.S.J T., who was visiting, was invited to ad 4.ress the Lodge and he urged all to study so as to be able to de- fend their princinles,relating how Mx Smith, of the Kent County Federation, saw the :advertisement of a leading firm of wine and spirit merchants say- ing that their invalid port wine was recommended by the Medical Faculty. He wrote to them draw- ing their attention to it and told them it was not correct as few dect )rs recommended it nowadays. They replied that they would inquire into it and if not correct would wittrdraw it; Three months later, it not being withdrawn, he wrote again and they replied that on incieiry thef found it, was not correct and therefore would withdraw it which they did. The signs el the times all pointed to the ultimate triuraph of the principles of total abstinence. At the close of a public debate be- tween a reoreentative of the Brewers and the gon. Mr. St. John, the trade representeive closed his remarks by saying" We have the drinking element on our side; we have the foreign element, with us. and the money with us; end don't you forget it!" to which the Hon. Mr. St. John replied" We have the sober element. with us; we have the woman with us, and we have God with us, and God is a power; and don't you forget it !" (Applause.) Bro. Francis urged all to refuse to deal with liquor- dealing grocers. The members sang the ode" Yield not to temptation " ; song, "The Gipsies Warning," Sister Thorne., song. accompanied by concertina, "The Farm Yard," Sister Mellor: dulcimer and banjo- duet, Mr. Eaton and Mr. Walker; song, with laughing chorus, Mr. Eaton ; concertina solo, Sister Mellor; recitation, "The Master's touch," Sister Francis; song, "'Fell me," Sister Woods ; solo, Mr. Walker; song, "The Laughing Coon," Mr. Eaton. Bro. Clark proposed that the thanks of the Lodge be given to Sister Wcods, and to those who had taken part in the programme, which was car- ried, and the meeting was then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A LUNATIC AT ST. HELENS. On Wednesday at the St. Helens Police Cpurt, the magistrates, Councillors J. Burchall and T. Glover, corn- mitted Thomas Margerson, of Gre-nbank, to the work- house for 14 days for b.inF.; of unsound mind. Dr. Cotton said Margerson was suffering from delusions, and had said he had been dead three times that morning, and somebody had tried to poison him. COMPENSATION PO Et INJU RIES. At the St. Helens County Court on Wednesday, before his Honour Judge Collier, a case was down for hearing in which George Jones, a day-wage man, of School-brow Rainford, claimed compensation for injuries received from Messrs. Bromifew, FGster and Co., of the Ashton's Green Colliery. It was stated twat j9t...N3 was knocked down by a box of coals in the mine on the 23rd March- He received a double fracture of the legs, and was to- tally incapacitated from work. He claimed 7s 3i per week until recovery. The firm had paid him £2. An award of £lO compensation, with half a guinea for casts, had been arranged, subject to his honour's ap- proval. His Honour assented, and the judgment was entered accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>PARLIAMENTARY REVISLON COURT</head>
        <p>PARLIAMENTARY REVISLON COURT</p>
        <p>AT NEWTON,</p>
        <p>Mr. J. T. Foard held a court at the Town Hall, New- ton. on Wednesday, for the purpose of revising the lists of Parliamentary voters for the districts of Newton-in- Makerfield, Wiewick-with-Hulme, Houghton, Middle- ton and Arbury, Southworth-with-Croft and Burton- wood. Mr. H. B. Shores conducted the revision on be- half of the Liberals, and Mr. W. Holland Owen, with Mr. Mawdsley, represented the Conservatives.—There were a large number of claims and objections on both sides.—George Sutton claimed an occupiers vote for the shop 4, Bridge-street. Mr. Shores objected, on the ground that the shop was a lock-up one, and claimant did not reside on the premiSee, and the objection was sustained. NOT " DESIRABLE " NOR REPREHENSIBLE." George Bailey appeared on behalf of George Jackson in support of an occupation claim in respect of 127, Earle-street, Barlestown. Mr. Shores said this gentle- man apparently bad gone round collecting rent books. He had proved one claim already. He knew his face from last year. and he had a very strong suspicion that he was employed by the Conservative party, and he (Mr Shorts) maintained that he should not go round collect- ing rent books. The Barrister said within reason be did not see that was any objection. He had only made two claims, and it appeared so him to be a convenience to tbe voter himself, and within reason and commonsense he could not see any objection.—Mr. Shores : If your Honour thinks it is a desirable practice I will give way. —The Barrister: lam a long way from saying it is a desirable practice, 1)15 not a reprehensible one.—Tbe claim was ultimately allowed. OBJECTION TO LORD CROSS. Mr. Shores objected So the name of Richard Assheton Cross, of Ecclerigg's, Broughton-in-Furness appearing on the ownership list of Southworth-with-Croft. He said this was Lord Cross, and being a Peer his name should not be included in the list. The objection was sustained.</p>
        <p>TEE M CORQUODALM FAMILY STRUCK OFF. Mr Shores enccessfully objected to the names of A C McCorquodale, G F McCorquodale, N McCorquodale, H S McCorquodale, appearing on the occupation list, as joint occupiers of land and builiings situate in Church- street, Newton, on the ground that they were members Of a Limited Company.</p>
        <p>_ OBJECTION TO MR. GILLESPIE. An objection by Mr Shores to Mr T G Gillespie, of Park-road, Newton, on the ownership list in respect of the Paper Works, was successfully sustained on the ground that the works belonged to a limited company, of which Mr. Gillespie was a mPrnber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LEASE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>A LEASE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>George &amp;urge's—claimed al; ownership vote in respect of leasehold property in Houghton-street, Earlestown, but the claim was declared bad, because of the land being leased from a sub-lessor. and not from the ground landlord. s4lai</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>GOLBORNE-STREET -BAPTIST CITA Pim.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STREET -BAPTIST CITA Pim.</p>
        <p>The first of a series of entertainments held under the auspices of the Band of Hope in connection with the Golborne-street Baptist Chapel, took place in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening. The Rev. T. J. Schofield presided over a good attendance, and during the course of his opening remarks said that the Band of Hope was in a more prosperous condi- tion than it bad ever been since he came to War- rington. He expressed his pleasure at seeing such a large audience. During the evening addresses were delivered by Mr. E. Lloyd Jones ',secretary of the Warrington and District Band of Hope Union) and Mr. Few, and recitations and songs were contri- bawd by several RI ember s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>RUNCORN INSTITUTE SCHOOLS. OPENING OF TUE NEW WING BY SIR JOHN BqIINNER,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN INSTITUTE SCHOOLS. OPENING OF TUE NEW WING BY SIR JOHN BqIINNER,</p>
        <p>PRIZZ DISTRIBurioN.</p>
        <p>The new wing of the Runcorn Institute Schools, presented to the town through the generosity of Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., was on Wednesday afternoon formally opened by the generous donor, in the presence of a large and influential gathering of townspeople. Sir John Brunner entered the schools by the principal entrance shortly after four o'clock, and was met by Sir Francis Wiswall, J.P. (chairman of the Runcorn Urban District Council), Mr J Wilson (chairman of the Technical Institute Committee), Mr Turnbull (member of the Technical Instruction Comreittee), W Handley, Mr E Imison, C.C., Mr R Norman, J.P.,Mr E Marshall (secretary of the Rural District Council), Mr A F Lake (law clerk), Mr T A Lawrenson.M.A., B.Sc. (hes.dmaster), Mr HE Mayston Mr GE Pilgrim, and Mr. T I, Irving (assis- tant masters), Miss A Johnson,B.A. (mistress of the preparatory schools), Miss Goble (teacher of cookery), and a large number of the general public. Havingput on his chain of office, Mr. F. Wiswall conducted Sir John Brunner, personally,to the door of the new wing, on arriving at which Mr. Wiswall said, on behalf of the Technical Instruc tion Committee and the town generally, he had great pleasure in presenting Sir John Brunner with a silver key, and cerdially inviting him to open the new wing of the Institution for them. The were all deeply indebted to him for all that he had done for them, and were de- lighted to have him present aneongst them that after- noon. (Applause.) Sir Joen Brunner in accepting the key expressed the pleasure he felt at doing so. Re never came to Runcorn without finding them engaged in some good work, and as a result he never came to Runcorn without having his heart filled with feelings of pleasure. Mr. Wiswall and others had given them what he could not give— time and loving thought—and on his part he would tender them his hearty thanks—(here Sir John inserted the key in the lock)—he declared the room open for the cenv elience of the young people of Runcorn an t dedi- catedlit in the hop-:,hat the benefits they received from it might be remembered by theca with pleaeure and com- municated by them to their children, and in such a way that their chddrea might follow them into it. (Ap- plauie.) A move was then male and the room rapidly filled. The platform was occupied by Mr. F. Wiewell (chair- man), Sir John Biunner, M.P., Messrs J Wilson, J Turn- bull, I R Norman, E Imison, and T A Lawrenson (head- master). The scholars occupied seats in the front, and the remaining apace was quickly taken up by the public. The room, which had been decorated with bunting and flowers for the occasioe, pre4ented a gay and cosy appearance. It is about 40 feet by 28 feet in dimen- sions, and practically embodies the whole of the ex- tension. Owing, however, to structural considerations ene of the old classroems on the south side has been added to the new room by the removal of a wall, the same classroom accommodation being secured by the insertion of a patent sliding screen. Tne room is so ar- ranged that on the insertion of mother screen two other classrooms are formed, each of them 28 feet by 20 feet. It is also adaptable as a whole for the use of public meetings (as the present occasion) and on the south wall is a plaster screen to facilitate the delivery of demon- strations and illustrated lectures, and the whole is well lighted and ventilated and replete with all the academi- cal acoessories necessary. The exterior corresponds in nature and architecture with the rest of the building, and the whole reflecte creditably upon Mr. James Wild- ing (the surveyor) who was responsible for the plans of the addition.</p>
        <p>Before proceeding to the business of the distribution of prizas apologies for absence were read [Torn Mr. F. J. Narman, LP., and Mr. R. H. Posn-tt.</p>
        <p>iThe Chairman then said as they ?were all aware, nerseeally, he had been very active in the establishing of the Inetiture at the outset, and more particularly with regard to the evening classes. They were all aware' of their beginning and the question of the ld rate, which was all they wo .1d be allowed to spend, and which they considered was a god one, Then it was they thought it nec.ssary and desirable to go further and finish what was begun and make a proper job of it To do that they established the day schools and now as a result it had become necessary to add that wing to I the building. On that point, however, he would leave' it to Sir John Brunner, and Mr. J. Wilson (chairman of 11 the Technical Instruction Committee) to tell them more. Since the day school had been established he heel not , taken that active interest in its affairs that he did,and many of them were aware that at that time he said" A master-shipwright was not necessarily a master mariner." (Laughter.) The ship having been properly launched only wanted conducting upon its voyage and that be left to others. He must congratu- late Mr. Lewrenson and the Council and all concerned upon the result of their work, for there wes no doubt they had gone one better, as was evidenced that day. (Applause.) It was only the other day that he beard a growl—and some people always indulged in grumbles and growls—and a business man, who himself had re- ceieed a London education, in his day, had asked him " Was it wise to go and import this popular education of the day in the way that they and others were doing?" He replied that he was surprised and asked that man was he aware—to put it that way—that this country could not bold its own with other countries without it. (Hear, hear.) Would that man who had been educated in London, and achieved his own success a3'the result of that educatiou, deny to others what had made him. (Hear, hear, and applause). He (Mr. Wis- wall) only wished that be could have had such privileges when he was a young man as they were having there in that Institute. He wished to emphasise what was being done. Might every body feel that it was a proper and valuable work they were engaged in. (Applause.) Speaking of the success of the schools Mr. Wiserall expressed his pleasure at the results achieved by the scholars of the Institute, and congratulated the Head- master and his staff and the Committee. (Applause.) The Committee needed all the encouragement they could get, but he also coneratulated their friend and esteemed member Sir John Brunner on the help which he had given them. (Applause.) That meeting could not have been held and those prizes distributed, had it nut been for the timely help of Sir John Brunner. HA gave gener- ously, and be gave his money intelligently. That covered the whole thing. (Hear, hear.) He helped those whom he found helping themselves. They were. he felt sure, all pleased to have him present that night, and indeed with all the calls upon him he often wondered how he responded to them all. Before calling upon Sir John to address the meeting and present the prizes, he would ask Mr. Lawrenson (the head-master) to submit his report. (Loud applause). Mr T A Lawrenson' headmaster, said he would ask them to join him in thanking Sir John Brunner for his very generous gift of the new wing, which, although only in use a short time, had proved to be quite an acquisition to the school; bat, naturally, in the course of time, a very great deal of work would be turned out of that room. (Applause.) It was just 2 years since the school was started, and at that time they had 48 in the science classes, and 10 in the smaller preparatory class. makine 58 altogether. The following year they bad 69 and 32, making 101; whilst at present they had 104 in the schools, so that as far as members went, con- sidering the short time they had been open, they might reasonably remain in a peaceful frame of mind, feeling that the school was certain of a measure of success. (Applause.) Speaking about the science and art depart- ment of the school. he took that opportunity of explain- ing that the school was one of those opened under the Science and Art Department (South (Kensington). The Department allowed grants for every boy and girl presented for examination on certain subjects. They were also compelled by the Department to teach certain subjects, and were not left to choose their own subjects, and part of the curriculum involved the teaching of sciences. Seme•imes people came to him in a complaining mood, though as a rule he had a great respect fur the British parent..-and he had parents coming to him sometimes wanting their children to be excused from sciences; indeed chemistry had been once or twice the fear of tome people. He wished to say he would be willing enoneh to excuse these subjects if parents were willing to make up the difference in the grant. For instance the grant last year averaged £5 per head, and if they did not present the children the money whioh was the source of income to the schools, was lost. He would, indeed, excuse the parents all of the suejecte, if they would make up the money. (Leuebter and hear, hear.) They were supposed to teach science in tbe schools for 13 hours a week ; ten hours a week were to be devoted to literary subjects i scluding one lan. nage —by the way French was the language they took ' • and 2 hours in the case of the bays, must be de- voted to manual labour, 1.e., every boy had to pier in 24 hours a week at wood work. The girls put in the 24 hours at needlework and cookery. So that was the reason that the schools were called science schools. In return for this the Department sent round their inspec- tors two or three times a year to inspect the schools. There were 66 scholars presented for examination last year. Considering they had to spend so meth time on the subjects he bad enumerated, they could easily under- stand that they could not send in many for outside ex- aminations. But if they looked at the list, they would see that as far as possible they tried to keep up with the public bodies outside the Science Department. (ip. please.) Last year they had three successful passes in the Oxford Junior Local—(applause)—they sent in four and three passed. (Renewed applause.) At Christmas, 1898, they sent in ten boys and nine passed. One passed the Cambridge Local Senior of the Cheshire County Council—Steward Harding—who, be was proud to say, was one of the smartest scholars in the school. (Applause.) He was glad to say that the county scholars contributed by Runcorn were the best metal in the school, and he hoped the County would continue to receive as good. Eight passed the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, SEPTEMBER 29, 1899</p>
        <p>Cambridge I^cal Preliminary ; and this year in July, 1899, Reginald Christien psssed the Of srd local senior. (loplause.)—lt the same time, the nurriculum was not suited for the Oxford senior exaarinations. Also George Baguley had obtained an Agricultural Schwlarship of toe Cheshire County Council, valued £3O a year tor 3 y ears. Referring to scholan,hips Mr. Liwrenson co:nil-leaded the scheen of the Ch-seire C vanty C iuectl which offered scholarships for competition am mgst scholars in elemen- tary schools, They also offerA more va.luabie ones which enabled the scholar who had work_el up to a secondary school to go up hi4her, an I wnich were ten- ' able at Owen's College (Manchester) or Liverpool University, and he hoped to see boys from Runcorn go lup to those Plac,s. (Applause.) Tilll3 boys from the ' eterneetary school could coins there, and then go on to the Uaiversity. Such a ,:coerne wou'd commend itself, and would be best for those who wane to take up a profession in the future. In ad Brion to the scholarships already mentionel they had 6 three year scholarships, or as they had called them Brunner scholarships, and also 15 scholarships for scholars fer elementary schools, and tea County Council scholar- ships, making a total of 31. (Applause.) The income of the schools was derived chiefly fro n grants from the Science and Art Dspartment and Aso the County Coun- cil contributed something like £5O and a certain amount for any children who att ndeil the scaoll from outside the Runcorn Urban District Council area. In conclusion Mr. Ltwrensen desired to put on public record the valuable assistance he had received from Mr. E. Marshall the secretary of the Urban District C eincil from time t 3 time, in the management of the school ; and though people seemed inclined to credit himself with the success of the school,he wished, also, to say that the greater part of that credit should go to the assistant masters and mistress. He took that public opp aquaity of thanking them for the help they had given him on eve.y occa- sion. (Loud applause.) Sir John Brawler then distributed a large n imher of valuable book prizes and certificates to the following successful scholars .</p>
        <p>Cambridge Locals, 1893. Prize; and Cerificates S Hardy, W Lach, F P Johnson, J Flughes, F Sherlock, T Williamsn, H Wilson, J Nlarshall, fl Stanway, Oxford Local Senior, 1399 :—R Christien. Prizes for Highest Positions in R ,spective Classes :—R COristien, E Sinildq, J J Stubbs, J Nixon, A. WAlker. Scripture :—W Reynolds, C Boyers, F Howard. French Wilson, Lucy Sutton, F Yates. Eoglish StioPcts :—F Johnson, W Hughes, H Rodiek. English Essay :—T Williamson, E Rob.rts. Science ;—F Handing, F Lea, G Bagu!ey, L Horse- field.</p>
        <p>Drawing :—E Andrews, G Mack, T Earlam, M Crad- dock, J Basnett. Shorthand :—F Sherlock, A Chringhall, L Gors. Woodwork i—W Leach, D Moore, H Cpokery and Needlework :—S Caineron, _NI Morris, F Price, A Beckett. Prizes for Regular Attendance and Good Conduct : w Sutton, A. Walton, T Anderson, G Ashmore, J Mar• shall.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner who received cent 3 an oration on rising to speak said he had to thank them for the in- vitation to attend that ceremony and also for the very kindly reception he received when first he made his appearance in the building. It heil been spoken of in very kindly terms, about what he ha I been able to do in furthering the good work carried on in that building. Well he wished them to bAieve that there was no greater pleasure to him then to be permitted in a very small way ito help those who were able and willing to help themselves, and a still greater pleasure it was to be associated as he was in that work wi-h so ne any of his friends. He especially referred to Mr. Wiswall, Mr. Wileen and Mr. Imison and the members of the Technical Committee. He had t 3 thank them too for the patience they exhibited over the business they had in hand. It wig a great sacrifice on the part of the men of Runcorn to give up such a large portion of their time to the furthering of the cause of education, and he thanked them for the encouragement and enthusiasm their presence gave net only to the young people of Runcorn but to the whole population of Runcorn. Their presence was a distinct encouragement to the whole people of Runcorn to carry on that colossal work. It had been a matter for regret in many schools in:various parts of the country, that there was no record of the later doings of their successful ,chelars, and as they at ' Runcorn were but starting upon their career, he sugges- ted they should carry out that idea and keep an accurate record of the future career of the scholars they turned out year by year. (Applau‘e.) He had tio doubt that such a record would be of value to the school and would be a very distinct encouragement to those who followed the young people who were now filling the rooms of that school. He hoped too, to see in that room record boards on which the names of the successful scholars were re- corded with the addition to, of any future success they might attain elsewhere. He did not know whether many of them would recognize the saying, but it was very common amongst boys attending public schools. " Wire in and get your name up." (Laughter.) That meant getting their names on the record boards, and he hoped his young hearers would determine in their own minds to " get their names urn" (Laughter.) He dared say that most of them were aware that the system of giving scholarships was one greatly debated, and greatly disputed, and there were many who gave great attention to the subject of education who were of the opinion that they were not doing wisely in the giving of scholarships. That ther were making what they called " young prodigies" and were not raising the general average of intelligence of our own young people. Now, there was no country in the world where the average intelligence was so high as in Scotland, and there was no e iuntry in the world where the system of scholar- ships had been so widely diffused and so widely kept up as in Scotland. The number of scholar- Iships was absolutely enormous in proportion to the number of students. The result of that system had beena that for years the working people of Scotland have earned their livieg in the sum- mer and more and had spent the surplus in going through the winter half year in the great Universities of Scotland. (Applause.) And this too in face of the fact that they often enough had to earn their living as shep- herds and drovers. That the system was being eneeur- aged and becomieg more universal was a matter for congratulation. People returning from the Uni-e1 States were filled with enthusiasm about the feet that the people of America were doing the same thing to-day. Wherever they turned they would find the fruits of it. If they boarded a yacht, the man burnishing the brasses of the vessel had possibly saved money to go through his university in the winter, and had a full acquaintance with some language. They would fini on going to a fashionable seaside resort that the head waiter was per- chance a remarkably good Greek seheiar —(laughter)— and had saved enough to go through his university and it was getting to be a regular joke that the smart girl waitress was being coached in her Greek by the had waiter. (Laughter and applause) It was extremely pleasing to him to come into that room and find it so bright and cheering. It was a room that was easy to speak in as Mr. Lawrenson had already foued out and he hoped it would be as easy to learn in. (Hear, bear.) It was to be a science room—that was one of the rooms devoted to science teaching and it had been built in order to set free another room to be used for the exclusive teaching of art. His friends, Messrs Wiswall, Handley, Wilson, Leverenson and !nelson had said that a good deal of the actions of the Science and Art Department were a lot of " red tape." and he thought so too. They had threatened the Runcorn Technical Institute Committee that they would withdtaw £5OO of the £l,OOO grant they had given them if the room was not exclusively devoted to arteso that they were compelled to build that room they were assembled in to secure their £soo— hear)— and fcr his part he was inclined to chuckle over it. (Laughter.) They had dodged the Government. (Increased laughter.) Still he sometimes wondered where on earth that £5OO would have gone to if they had not kept it. (Hear, hear.) He was not there to talk politics but it might have zone to mitigate the rates of the Irish landlord—(laughter) —and it might have gone to places they cared little about, but to pat it candidly he did not know a better place for the £5OO to stay in than Run- corn. (Applause.) It was known all over the world that Eeglish racehorses and English shorthorns, etc., beat all the world. An enormous amount of time and money that had been expended upon the improvement of the breed of horses and cattle, and a long time ago he came to the conclusion that it wad time they took to thinking how to improve the breed of human beings, and he had, and always had felt satisfied that if they devoted the same amount of money to insure the improvement of the people as they tad to the improve ment of horses, that their people would have been better physically and mentally as well as morally and bodily able to beat the world as did English rac3 horses. (Loud applause.) It was because the Runcorn Technical Institute was part of the great work of improving the breed of human beings of this country that he was s pleased to be associated with it. The work the young people were going through in that Institute was a preparation for what was usually called the struggle of life. He frankly confessed that he had not foand life a struggle—not a little bit, and he wished to give his hearers the hope that if they gave their neighbours the impression that they were doing their best and taking the advantage of the privileges they had their neighbours would be kindly disposed to- wards them as he, Sir John, had found in his life his neighbours kindly disposed towards him. If the students in that school would so demean themselves as to earn the good opinion of their neighbours depend upon it their lives would be very much less of a struggle than they were to those who rather fought against society than with it. (Applause.) It was usual upon occasions of that kind to tell those who had not won scholarships or prizes that they had done as well as those who were euccearful. He was not, however, going to give them any such nonsense. He believed that those who had been successful had done a good deal better than those who had not. His con- gratulations were for those who had won prizes,and his best good wishes to those who had not. (Applause.) Sir John Brunner concluded his address with remarking that he was struck by one of Mr. Lawrenron's references respecting the education being better fitted for anyone desirous of entering a profession in future life. He would have Shen understand that this edueation from</p>
        <p>the elementary schools to the secondary school and on to the univer,ity was a preparation for those who were de- ; sirous of being tradesmen and indeed for all branches of I life. If they took to retail business they would conduct that busin-ss under far better conditions. They would be better shopkeepers and better cJinmercial people all round. Having exhorted hi 3 y nearers to learn all whilst they were young, for tue tim3 came when their learning capacities were not so good, he asked them to exercise their faculties so as to be doing the best for themselves and also fulfilling their best functions as eitiz3ns. (Loud applause.) Mr. J. Wilson who described his duty as a light one ' said he had merely to so Omit to them a vote of thanks to Sir John Brunner for his kindness in coining amongst them that evening au' encourauing them in the w irk, and al3o for his spl,indid genero3ity towards their school. In an address upon the work of the schools Mr. Wilson especially pointed out that the schools did not cost the ratepayers of Runcorn—AS a ratepaying community—.A single penny. They hid been allowed aid in the E trom the rates, and when he told them that this realis something like £lB2 they cowld understand that as it Vr...1 first called upon by the Evening Continuation Classes it did not get much further than the provision of the staff. He also testified to the unqualified success of the schools. (Applause.) When compelled to either build the new wing or forfeit the £5OO he was proud to testify to the generous way in which Sir John had corn. forward and though that building had cost £540, Sir John Brunner had contributed £3OO towards its com- pletion. (Applaus%) It was, therefore, with the greatest of pleasure he suhmittei the resolution.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Turnbull seconded, and the vote was acc)rded with acclamation.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner rsturned thanks, and propo3ed a vote of thank 4 to Mr. Wig wall, their president for the night. He was sure they all agreed with him when he said there was no better specimen of a cheerful typical John Bull than Mr. Wiswall, and it was always p'easure to be near him, for there was always a peaceful atmosphere about him. (Loud applause). Mr. Wiswall responded. At the conclusoa of the meeting, Mr. Lawrecsm announced that tea was obtainable upstairs, and inti- mated that the school , could be inspected by the visi- tors, and also an exalt:a:A(3a of the boys' woodwork ; he was only sorry there was not an exhibition of the girls' cookery. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meettng of the above Council was held at the Work'muse, Datton, on Tu,sday, when there were ure3ent :—Messrs. J‘Jserh Knowle3, J.P., (‘,-,hairnian), S Woo,iward, J Moseley. Williams, Admiral Jones-Parry, J Mclntyre, T Baxtr, C.C., C E Linaker, W Fletcher, T Wright, J Stokes, J Bar:uley, J B Chadwick, P Leather, J Tu for, E Clarke, G PlLiig ; with G F Ashton (clnk). W E /lough (deputy clerk),JSwinton,and W Diggle (sur- ve, ors), J Farrington (inspector), and Dr J Adams (mßdical officer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>FIELD CLUB AFLOAT.</head>
        <p>THE FIELD CLUB AFLOAT.</p>
        <p>[By JOHN PEERS.] On Saturday the Field Club made its last excur- sion for this season. For some time now it has been the custom of the club to make occasional visits to Milord Island for the collection of speci- mens of marine biology. This trip, although not an annual one, is one much appreciated by the mem- bers, and is looked forward to with pleasant antici- pation. Well ! it was decided by the club executive that the 19th century should not lapse without an attempt being made to continue so laudable and pleasanr, a custom, "a custom more honoured in tt e ' continuance ' than in the breach." Usually, the route is by Central excursion train to Liverpool, thence by Mersey Tunnel to West. Kirby where these naturals in their enthusiasm divest—or diboot -and paddle barefoot across the intervening stretch of squelchy sands where they can study the habits of the scuttling shore crab, the athletic performance of the stranded shrimp and the alarmed ejaculation of the cockle as he closes his place of business in a hurry—puts his shutters up as it were, on the rade intrusion of our feet. So, the fervour of scientific research having surmounted this slight drawback, these devotees of science on reaching the compara- tively dry land of the island, resume the ordinary garb of civilised life. This time however, things were to be conducted ; differently. By the generous interposition of Mr. Councillor P. W. Monks, who offered the Use of S.S. " Helvetia" to convey the club from Liverpool to the island, this wading episode was evaded. That the change of programme met with the concurrence of many members of the club was evidenced by the number who sent in their acceptance. Messrs. John Milbourn and Company to the order of Messrs. Councillor J. Sliaw-Green, , J.P.. and A. Silcock, had provided a substantial tea for 30. Great then were the anticipations of a pleasant and successful excursion; great also the , preparations for its successful prosecution. Eut, alas! man proposes but woman—in this case 'Amphitrite—disposes. The week ending September 23rd kad been unusually stormy, and the morn of the appointed day rose with no better promise—and fulfilled it. This the writer found to his cost, for the programme including a possible return by the steamer to Liverpool and the uncertainty of its ' time of arrival with the inconvenience of train times, &amp;c., decided him to use his " Winmarleig h,' which strove gallantly against the gala to the tram terminus at Old Swan where it was left for the night journey home. Now arrangements had been made, ana announced by card of invitation,that the members availing themselves of the trip should meet at the Central Station, Warrington—go by express 1.50 to Liverpool. thence to Shropshire Union Quay to embark. But here again the fickle elements had their say in the matter, "What were the will waves saying ?" Not here, my dear ! keep clear, for fear! So it was thought desirable to keep the vessel on the windward side of the river, under the lee of the land at Tranmere. So, our local savants arrived with the apparatus for the capture and carriage of Neptune's seldom seen treasures, which the great tides and the corresponding loss- ebbs promised to reveal to the diligent enquirer. As "coming events cast their shadows be- fore" so the uneasy rollicking of the Woodside ferry boat gave a foretaste of what was coming. Ominous, too, the fact that no landing would be effected at New Brighton; and the knowledge that many vessels had been trying to go out but returned owing to stress of weather added nought to the prospect of a calm passage. But away with all maudlin forebodings ; are the folk who can "enthuse" scientifically with wet feet to be de- terred by such trifles as a war between the winds an 4 the waves? No ! they determined to set a stout heart—and stomach—to the job. Besides,it is the Club's MiSSiOR to study nature, and here was a new field—the watery plain,a very uneven one it is true—in which to do it. No mere carpet savants thesejseeking only the silken side of nature studies. And indeed here was a noble chance to see the seamy side of the sea, and very seamy indeed was the sea. Well, the good ship "Helvetia" haying taken her adventurers aboard, cast off her connec- tions with the slip and backed out into the stream, now fiercely running with curly crested waves, down again to the sea. Not here and now, as our local poetess has said, does "Mersey creep inglori- ous" and certainly "dares to emulate" more than " a classic tide." Before New Brighton was reached things began to be lively. The vessel being light, without cargo, rushed up one wave to fall with a resounding bang down upon the next, with a sound as though she had struck the bottom, which raised questions among us as to what that bottom was made of. Nor were these performances accomplished without their accornpanyment of showers of salt spray invading the privacy of the deck and much ingenuity was shown in evading these incursions; which the members good humouredly took as amusing incidents of the trip. So things were in the river. But when the Rip-Rap and Rock Light were rounded and we were some half-way down the Rock Channel things began to show in their true colours. Away over the great Burbo bank to our right could be seen the heads of great seas rushing to dash themselves to pieces on its hidden sands; we could see, and but for the nearer noises of our surroundings.would have heard, the boil and smother of its surf. And we now were in a comparatively sheltered place, surrounded by banks on every side; but must go through that ere we could reach our goal. And what then ? Ah ! what then? What dire possi- bilities were there ! It is hinted that already some of the weaker brethren were asking if such a life were worth living, or such a game worth the candle? So aft went the order to 'bout ship and away sped the "H elvetia " before the breeze and out crept the sheltering savants from their various coigns of vantage, and as the prospects of a scien- tific feast faded from their sight 80 the more grossly material form of tea and sandwiches took its place. For alas ! even scientists have stomachs —to ha perturbed by Neptune as well as appeased by the cook. So while the ship threaded her way through the Wallasey entrance into the intricacies of the Birkenhead Docks, the good things provided to this end were discussed. After which the members inspected a torpedo destroyer in the same dock and then dispersed to their several homes, while the writer rode a stormy ride, wind driven, homeward, and the rising moon shed fitful gleams from behind silver-edged clouds, the high-strung wires making Ifiolian music overhead. bow, although, from the collector's point of view, the trip may be said to be a failure, in other ways it was by no means so. If it shook the members together a little—that was no harm—and if it washed some of the starched con- ventionality out of them—that, also, was good. And, as kind acts are seldom thrown away, the club returned feeling and expressing gratitude to those who had so generously placed at their dis- posal the means by which so much genuine enjoy- ment was obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>HELSBY SEWERAGE INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>HELSBY SEWERAGE INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted a notice of an inquiry to be held at the National Schools, Helsby, on Thursday, the 287 h inst., with reference to the aprlieation of the Parochial Commi3e f3r 3anction to borrow £5,200 for the sewer- age of the township of Helsby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>SMALL DWELLINGS ACQUISITION.</head>
        <p>SMALL DWELLINGS ACQUISITION.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board- torwarded a circular letter with reference to the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act, 1899, which contains important provisions for facilitating the acquisition of small 11011303 by persons who desire to reside in them by enabling local authori- ties to advance, under cerAin conditions part of the purchase money. The provisions aif the Act were ex- plained in &amp;tail. This matter was on the motion of Mr. S. Woodward, seconded by Mr. Moseley Williams, delegated to the Par- liamentary Committee.</p>
        <p>TIIC SMOKE NUISANCE FROM WESTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Percy Allen, on behalf of the Castner Kellner Alkali Company, Umitei, Weston Point, Runcorn,wrote as follows, under date 14th September, 1899 :—" Dear sir,—l br‘g to aknowledge yc ur letter of the 13th int. containing copy of complaint from Mr. Mortimer con- cerning the smoke from our chimney. We are unfortun- ately well aware that one of our big chimneys does emit more smoke than was expected. The boilers which dis- charge into the chimney were intended to be fitted with mechanical stokers with a view to obviate • any smoke. Unfortunately this type first fitted proved quite unsuit- able for the purpose and had to he removed and pending other arrangements the boilers here have to be fired by hand. I may say that our Chairman, Mr. Mather, feels very strongly the importance of the matter and has taken the question up himself with the makers of the boilers, and they have ! now promised to fit all these boilers with their improved form of chain grate stoker, which is practically smoke- less. Owiri.g to press of work they would not promise delivery before January, but I understand that they are ! making arrangements be which we can have some of them before then. The Company are desirous of mak- ing every effort to alter the present condition of things and no expense will be spared in completing the new arrangments at the earliest possible moment, but unfor- tunately we are in the hands of the makers as to delivery." Mr. Chadwick said he also had a telegram handed to hirn which signified that the makers were desirous of doing their utmost to forward the stokers.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Clarke said it was a matter of great import- ance to the Company and they would be naturally as anxioui to abate the nuisance as themselves.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward proposed that the letter be acknow- ledged, and a request forwarded that the matter be remedied as soon as possible. Mr. C. E. Linaker seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>TELEGRAPII FOR G RELT BUD WORM AND NORTHWICII. The Post-Master General wrote stating that he was about to erect a lino of telegraph from Northwich to Great Budworth, arid was desirous of obtaining the con- sent of the Runcorn Rural Districc, Council for the erection and maintenance by the Department of a line of telegraph along the poblio road from the j inotion with the North wich Rural District to the Post Office at Great Budworth. He undertook to make good any damage which might be done in the execution of the work.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward prpposed that the Council's approval be given, on the understanding that everything was carried out " subject to the approval of their surveyor." Mr. J. M,Aatyre seconded, and the Council concurred.</p>
        <p>RE-APPOINTMENT OF NIIISA.NCE INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Mose:ey-Williams proposed that Mr. James Farrington be re-appointed inspector of nuisances and Inspector under the Dairies and Cowsheds and Milkshops Ord-r 1835 for one year enchng the 29th September. 1900, at a salary at the rate of £l9O per annum ; and that he be re-appointed inspector of canal boats for the same period at a salary at the rate of £lO per annum ; and that the sanction of the Local Government Board be requested thereto. In doing so, ho considered that latterly Mr. FarriPgron's duties had been better dis- charr2,ed than they used to be, a circumstance which was no doubt due to the fact that now he had a conveyance he was able to cover his district, which was a very scattered one, much better. Mr: Farrington's duties were very numerous and very much spread,and be hoped he would have his health and strength to again faith- fuily disch4r4o them. Mr. C. E. Linaker seconded, and the motion was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>- Mr. J. Farrington thanked the Board, and intimated tha the would try to 64c:1-large bis duties in the future as well if not better than in Chi, past. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>BIICKLBY VICTORIA V. NEWTON•LE- WILLOWS. —Th is match was played at Buckley. The start was delayed owing to the non-arrival of the referee, one of the on• lookers hdding the whistle for ten minutes, when the appointed official arrived. The home team held the advantage, but Newton defended strongly. Badger was tripped in the anal mouth, but he only hit the post from the penalty. A few minutes before half-time Peter Davies, inside left,scored a lovely goal,and "Vies" finally won by three goals to nil, another penalty a!so being missed. For the winners, all the forwards did well, Badger, in his new position, shaping well. The defence was safe, but the goal keeper was not troubled often. The centre right half and both backs were best of the losers.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN SHAMROCKS V. THATTO HEATH ATHLETIC. —Played at Tnatto Heath on Saturday. The Athl,tics won the toss and decided to kick against a strong wind in the first half, Lloyd setting the ball in motion on be- half of the Shamrocks in the presence of about 300 spectators. The start was sensational as aided by Ilse wind the visitors early assumed the upper hand and a line pass by Turton enabled Glynn to score their first goal within the first minute. The homesters roused themselves at this early reverse and forced a corner which was cleared by Odlum. The same player after- wards scored the Shamrocks second gcal front lone range. Half-time ; Shamrocks 2, Athletics nil. Resum- ing, the visitors soon took up the attack and the home custodian was severely tested by a splendid screw shot from Koary. The homesters only goal which was sc ired about 15 minutes before the whistle blew was a curious one. Play was proceeding in the visit -Ts half and a scrimmage took place in the goal mouth, and after a while the homesters claimed for a goal alleging that the bill had been aver the line whereas it had net been within a Yard of the goal. After consulting the linesmen the referee allowed the point, whereupon the spectators roared with laughter at what they called the referee's weakness. Final : Shamrocks, 2 ; Matto Heath Athletic, one. LANCASHIRE LEAGUE. EARLESTOWN V. BLACKPOOL.— rhe local pointless ones had as visitors on Saturday one of last season's members of the second division, viz., Blackpool. As Haydock managed to gain a substantial victory over the sea- eiders a week or two ago, it was thought that Earles- town would at least make a draw, some of the most enthusiastic of :heir supporters even going so far as to predict a win. Esrlestown played their full Lancashire Leagus team, but the visitors arrived minus Anderson, his place being filled by Birkett. Punctnally at 3.30 the teams lined up as follows, under the supervision of Mr. Marquis</p>
        <p>Earlestown, Chew, coal ; Fleyes and Wilkinson, backs ; Green, Parr, and Cottam, half backs ; Linton, Barnes, Harnpson, L.ach and Settle, forwards: Blackpool ; Fletcher, goal ; Stirzaker and Scott, backs ; Threlfall, Howson and Atherton, half backi ; Speight, Morriss, Birkett, Tipping, and Leadbetter, for- wards.</p>
        <p>The homesters won the toss and elected to play with wind and hill in their favour, Birkett setting the ball in motion in the presence of about 500 spectators. Aided by the strong wind Earlestown early assumed the aggressive and a fine combined movement by the whole of the forwards resulted in Settle skimming the crossbar with a beauty. From the goal kick, Leadbetter and Tipping carried the ball nicely up the field and the latter player caused Chew to run out and.clrar, The homesters were down again in a trice and Linton and Settle both narrowly missed scoring, Another fine effort' by Settle was luckily for Blackpool charged down by Scott. A foul against Sampson relieved the pressure and by a series of throws in, the visitors eat a footing in the home half, Speight eventually spoiling a grand opening by sending the ball yards wide. The whole of the Earles- town front rank participated in a tine advancing move- ment from which the visitors goal had a couple of nar- row escapes, the lengthy custodian only just managing to scoop one shot from Parr round the post and over the line. The ensuing corner was safely negotiated and a priimising move by Leadbetter and Tipping was nipped in the bud by Green. The home left were next in pro- minence, Settle dribbling the ball finely down the wing, and when near the corner flag he shot in. The custodian who evidently thought the leather was going outside, allowing it to strike the upright and roll slowly into the net, this point coming as the result of thirtyfive minutes play. Uneventful play followed until the interval when the score read : Eirlestown one, Blackpool nil. On resuming, Earlestown were the first to attack Cottam and Sampson each having shies at goal. A grand run up the field by Linton WAS followed by a splendid centre which left Leach with practically an open goal, but he dallied and Scott dashing up cleared the danger. Fifteen minutes after the resumption Birkett made the score level with a shot which gave I Chew no chance. This seemed to dishearten the homesters, who from this point to the close rarely crossed the half-way line, Blackpool in the meantime piling on three more goals and winning comfortably by our goals to cue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RliiißY FOOTBALL. [BY "OvaLisT "] There is very r3tt clangor ot Warrington sticking in the rut I so,ke of last wok, for thlugh they made a strong effort on ;-aturday the :failed to cline) out of it. Few people vno saw no Stockport match imagined that Rochdale Hornets woull succeed in vanquishing War- rington but they did. The margin was only small, it is true, and the best team lost, but tile het remains that the Hornets took two valuable points from Warrington, and thus acclmplished a feat that they have not done for two vein at least. L was a great disappointment to the Warrington players and supporters alike when they dropped two points in the valley of the Roach. Nearly all were confident of iticcory—perhaps over-con- fident—and when the home team emerged from the struggle victorious by a couple of points obtained in the last five tninutei, the pill was a bitter one for Warrin,tonians. The first four matches have only pro- duced 3 points, which is a shade below last season's average, and the promised improvement had better be forthcoming.</p>
        <p>Seeing tha't Warrington were so unexpectedly defeated it behoves MP to explain away the defeat—and the task is not a vary difli.Allt one. To begin with, it can scarcely be questioned that the better teim lost, for Warrington weze superior to the Hornets in every department of the game except full back, but the exception was an impor- tant one. the reader will naturally ask: " Then why in the name of all that's (un) holy did they lose ? and the answer is forthcoming. It was the Warrington men's own fault that the two points did not come back with them. They had every advantage and opportunity in the first half and did not exert themselves over much. The game seemed well in hand, and it was only when about three tries had been thrown away, and the Hornets suddenly got five points ahead, that they seemed to awake to the fact that someone was trying hard if they were not. Then Cee Warrington lads set to with a will, but it was too late. The opportunities which went a-begging in the opening stages w(re found bard to obtain later on. The luck changed. Instead of their efforts being crowned with the success they appeared to merit something went wrong just at the critical moment. The Hornets were defending continuously, but at last Warrington got in and equalised. Finally, as a punishment for Warring- ton's e trlier slackness Duna F n.tune stepped in with a gift for R schdale and Rigg obtained a goal in the last few minutes.</p>
        <p>There is no doubt whatever that Warrinton would have won easily if they had only played their hardest when the wind was behind them, but they held the foe too cheap and were punished in c-ms,quence. Ten points ought easily to hays been scored bilore the interval, and lit was indeed a marvel how Jones failed to score on three occasions. Good combination provided the opening, , there was a clear course, but somehow or other Jones must let the ball slip or be tackled unnecessarily. The try was actually gained, all but the actual tou3h-down, for in each case the Warrington backs had beaten the Hornets and got the line at their mercy. Yet they mulled the chances—er rather Jones did—and no won- der there was some metaphorical tearing of hair. These chances came, too, when the men were not putting all they knew into the game. When they did " buck up," the R-chdale line keptiescaping in a manner for which they were not responsible.</p>
        <p>Rochdale is a moiit place. I never remember being there when it did not rain, and generally very heavily. The ground is ill-adapted for absorbing moisture, in fact it does no absqrbing but leaves all the wet on top for the players to mop up. This is all right for the Hornets, who are used to it, but fair-weather teams like Warrington feel at s )me disadvantage when they are playing in pools of water and greasy quagmires. The player, too,ne)ds a special training before he can handle a ball which comes at him double the usual weight and as slippery as an eel. This special training the Rochdale men gat often enough, and when they have one of their favourite wet days they feel that the odds are largely on their side. There is no doubt that at fielding a wet ball they will take some 13,,ating, though many a club besides Warrington could teach them something about football.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding these adverse conditions the game on Saturday was a good one. There was plenty of bright play, and little of the kick and rush usually adopted under such cir- cumstances. Indeed, Warrington seem to have thrown over the tactics they used to follow. They are cultivat- ing the open passing game and it is having good results. Had the elements bent favourable the Warrington backs would have given a good account of themselves on the score sheet. Not one of the Warriogton men played badly, and fault could only be found with Merryweather who is too slow for a wing position. Isherwood and Duckworth placed brilliantly, while Bate, Jones, and Wood were all in good form. Moores is not a class full-back, but he performed creditably. The forwards accomplished wonders, I should think they were overweighted about a stone per man, yet they carried nine out of every ten scrim- mages and bustled the Hornets eight off the ball in aston- ishing style. I never, on any occasion, saw them show such superiority over their opponents. The Hornets forwards ware big enough for anything, but they ap- peared to know little about their work and will need a lot of drilling before they are good for much. On the home sile Rigg stood head and shou!ders above every. body else. The worse the day and the better the little full-back plays. and I am not exagger- atit.g when I say that no man living could have played a better game than he did on Saturday. But for him Warrington would have scored many a time, and but for him they would not have been beaten in the last minute. I respectfully raise my hat to' Billy " Rigg, as a custodian worth his weight in gold. The only other men worthy of mention were Field and Kruger, both of whom played a sound and clever game.</p>
        <p>Thorn is another cbe,nce of picking points up to-lay. Widnes ought not to be ahle to win on the Wilderspool ground and I am quite certain they cannot if Warrington only play for all tne7 are worth from the kick-off to the finish. There must be no slackning. Another defeat at home in the fir.t; month would be a crushing disaster. Warrington owe WidnEs just about as big a licking as they owe Leigh, and let us see them imflict it to-day.</p>
        <p>The team tei met Widnes was selected on Wednesday evening and is as follows :—J Ashurst, full back; Harris Isherwood, Jones, and W 01, threequarters ; Bate and Duckworth, half backs ; Taylor, Naylor, Morrison, Sud- low, Bo trdman, Fairhurst, Eden,and Scholtze,forwarls. The changes are AQhurst. Harris, and Morrison, for Moorw, Merryweather, and Swift, and they ought to be for the better. I have thought for some time that of the available material for a full back, Ashurst was the beat, and lam glad to see him given a trial. He can kick well, tackle well, and run well. The experiment is certainly a good one. Fish would have appeared again on the wing but his thigh troubled him in practice this week, and he could not rely on it for a match. He will be welcomed when he comes back. Harris and Morrison both need to improve on previous displays to win general favour.</p>
        <p>The League tatln un to date is as folio wS : P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. For. Against. Runcorn Widnes 4.. 3. 1. 0.. 2 10— 34... 1 1... 5... 6 Salford Oldham Leigh Swinton St. Helens Warrington 4.. 1. 2.. 1. 5 7 31 .. 4 4... 20... 3 Root:dale Hornets 2... 1... 1... 0... 3 2 - 12... 2 5... 19... 2 Stockport 4 1. 3.. O. 4 5 23... 5 9... 37... 2 Brought'n Rag'r's 4... 1... 3... 0... 1 2- 8... 8 8... 40... 2 Millom - 20... 5 12... 46... 2 Tyldesley 5.. 0. 5.. 0 5 2 16 ..15 24...102... 0 Wigan</p>
        <p>Runcorii's magnificent win at Watersheddinga keeps them well ahead, and stamps them as a team to be feared. What a difference the rs.turn of Butterworth and Faulkner has made ? And Warrington so near se- curing them too. Alack-a-day !</p>
        <p>The " A" team notched a coqple more points on Saturday, though the game was an awful spectacle. Football was absent entirely, and one could hardly say the best team won. It would be more correct to say that the worst team lot. There was nothing good about either of them. Hallam and Ashurst were far, very far, below first team form. The Ecore is now 80 points to 3, and it will want some beating.</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>TETE TR.ANSVA.AL 131FORT,Yr STA i:FVFNI" Br MR. RITCHIE.</p>
        <p>Mr liitobie. president of the Boa-r-1 or Trade, e;Ting in Loudon yesterday Ufternoon, said they could not say how soon Parliament might re-assemble. The situation was undoubtedly very serious. The demands of the Government must he complied with. EXPECTED DASH BY TEIE BOERS. GLENCOE, NATAL. It is stated on exc7llent authority that in the case of any further movement of British troops in this direction the Boers who are massing at Vryheid, propose making a dash in force through a portion of Zaluland. They will cross the Buffalo river at a point below Rorke'S Drift. their object being to cut off the troops at Dundee and Ladysmith by.setzing the rAlway. Such a move- ment would not only interrupt the British lines of com- munication, but would seriously interfere with the forward movement. Mr. Rhodes considers War certain. THE LARGEST COTTON MILL IN THE WORLD. NEW YORK, THURSDAY. The building has commenced at Manchester (New Hampshire) on what kvill be the largest cotton mill in the world. It is to measure 1,600 feet long, 100 feet wide, and will be four storeys high.</p>
