      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-09-29_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>CYCLING,</head>
        <p>CYCLING,</p>
        <p>The members of the Orchid. Cycling Club met at Cuerden Hall on Friday. They were accompanied by their patron and president, Mr. R. A. Naylor, Cuerden Hall ; Mr. John Naylor, Chief Consul,C.T.C., of Beeston Tower; :Jr. J. Naylor, jun., Oxford, and the Captain, Mr. Geor,-.3 Lambert. The road taken lay through Knuteford, Mobberley, Alderley Edge, Nether Alderley, Over Ahlerley, into Macclesfield township, where a most enjoyable tea was partaken of. The start back was made in the moonlight. The cyclists had beautiful weather for the run. No rain fell, and the wind had dropped considerably, making this one of the most en- joyable runs of the season.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. THOMAS E. PICKTO r. Many of our readers will hear with regret of the death of Mr. Thomas E. Pickton, which took place at his residence, Froghall-lane, on Tuesday morning shortly before one o'clock. The deceased gentleman, who was only taken ill on Sunday, died from a heart affection. He was 72 years of age. For more than half a century he nad carried on business as a wheelwright and general smith, during the last few years at premises in Edgeworth-street. Though of a retiring disposition, and one who took no active part in political or municipal affairs, he was ex- tremely well-known throughout the town and dis- trict, and the news of his somewhat sudden death will occasion general regret.</p>
